--- MiniMessage a player reads, like `"<green>Done!"`: a string. A `<glyph:pack/key>` tag in it names a glyph the way the script's own files would: one of its own package's resource packs bare, or `<glyph:namespace:pack/key>` for one a package it depends on exports. The tag means that glyph wherever and whenever the text is shown, whoever shows it; one naming a glyph the script can't use draws nothing, and the server log says why. Text handed back to a script names its glyphs the same way.
---@alias Text string

--- Everything outside the script itself. The core verbs (events, timers, tasks and waiting, saved data, the budget) are on `nf`; everything else is grouped into namespaces under it: `nf.server`, `nf.commands`, `nf.players`, `nf.bots`, `nf.worlds`, `nf.structures`, `nf.centities`, `nf.spawning`, `nf.menus`, `nf.dialogs`, `nf.items`, `nf.blocks`, `nf.recipes`, `nf.loot`, `nf.bossbars`, `nf.teams`, `nf.schedule`, `nf.economy`, `nf.placeholders`, `nf.particles`, `nf.cutscenes`, `nf.screens`, `nf.files`, `nf.text`, `nf.looks`, `nf.json`, `nf.http`, `nf.web`, `nf.wasm`, `nf.network`, `nf.time`, `nf.random`, `nf.math` and `nf.test`. Plain functions on tables, so call them with a dot: `nf.players.online()`.
---@class nf
---@field server nf.server The server itself: its clock, its performance, its version, its settings (players, message of the day, whitelist), and saying or running things as the console.
---@field commands nf.commands Slash commands a module adds to the server for as long as it runs, with typed arguments, tab completion and usage messages from their declaration.
---@field players nf.players Players: everyone online, or anyone who has ever joined.
---@field bots nf.bots Bots: fake players that scripts join to the server and drive, for opponents and teammates in games, crowds, and anything a player does that an NPC can't. A bot is a real player to the server and to every other plugin: it's on the player list and counted in the player count, its joins, moves, clicks, chat and deaths are heard like anyone's, and it does everything by sending what a player's client would (walking a step a tick, aiming before it clicks), so the game's rules and every other plugin's checks apply to it. Each is a new player every time it joins (a fresh UUID, nothing of it saved: no inventory, statistics or advancements kept), marked as an NPC for other plugins (the `NPC` metadata they check to leave fake players alone), and it loads only the chunks right around it. A bot belongs to the script that joined it and leaves when that script unloads. A package declares `"requires": { "bots": true }` in its `netherforge.json`, and the server owner allows bots (`bots.enabled` in `config.yml`, with `bots.max` online at once); until then a package that declares them has a warning (`runtime.bots-disabled`). The editor's dev server always allows them. For a figure that stands and looks like a player without being one, spawn a `Mannequin`: it costs far less.
---@field worlds nf.worlds The server's worlds: finding them, and making, loading and copying them.
---@field structures nf.structures Structures: saved boxes of blocks that `world:place_structure` places. The project's are `structures/<id>.nbt`; scripts save more with `world:save_structure`. A build (`builds/<id>/`) is a structure too big for one file, kept in parts, which `world:place_gradually` places over several ticks; these functions name both alike.
---@field centities nf.centities Centity instances: spawning new ones and finding live ones.
---@field spawning nf.spawning Natural spawning (a centity's `spawning` block in `centity.json`): conditions a script decides, which a `spawning` block names with `when`.
---@field menus nf.menus The project's menus (`menus/<id>/`), and menus scripts make.
---@field dialogs nf.dialogs The project's dialogs (`dialogs/<id>/`), and dialogs scripts make.
---@field items nf.items The project's items (`items/<id>/`): item kinds of its own, whose stacks the server can always tell apart from any other stack of the same Minecraft kind.
---@field blocks nf.blocks The project's blocks (`blocks/<id>/`): blocks of its own, drawn by its resource pack, with their own hardness, drops and sounds, held in the world as states of the game's blocks (their carriers: note blocks, tripwire, leaves). A player places one with an item whose `block` names it, or a script with `ProjectBlock:place`.
---@field recipes nf.recipes The project's recipes: those in `recipes/<id>.json`, and those scripts register. A recipe can take the project's own items as ingredients (`{ item = "ruby" }`), which only a stack of that item fits, however it looks; and a recipe takes a project item only where it names it, so a ruby built on paper never crafts as paper. Players see a recipe in their recipe book once they've discovered it (`player:discover_recipe`), but can craft it either way.
---@field loot nf.loot Loot tables: the project's own (`loot/<id>.json`) and the game's. A roll picks from each of a table's pools, weighing its entries against each other, and gives the items it picked. The same `seed` always gives the same items (the editor rolls the same way). A table the project doesn't have, or that the game doesn't, is an error; so is a game table that needs something the roll doesn't say (a mob's table needs the mob, as `looted`).
---@field bossbars nf.bossbars Boss bars: bars across the top of players' screens.
---@field teams nf.teams The project's scoreboard teams: prefixes and suffixes on names, name colours and glow colours, friendly fire, name tags and collision.
---@field schedule nf.schedule Runs a function at a time of day, every day or week, or by a cron expression: real time, as opposed to ticks (`nf.after`, `nf.every`). Times are in the server owner's time zone (`schedules.time-zone` in `config.yml`, the server's own zone by default), not the player's. Daylight saving follows the zone's rules. A daily or weekly time the clocks skip runs the same distance after the gap begins (`"02:30"` runs at 03:30 on the day clocks jump from 02:00 to 03:00), and one that happens twice runs the first time only. A cron expression matches the clock as it reads: a time the clocks skip doesn't happen that day, one for a single time of day that happens twice (`"30 1 * * *"`) runs the first time only, and one that matches every half hour keeps matching through an hour that repeats. A schedule belongs to the script that made it and ends when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping), so the script's body makes it again. It's checked once a tick, so a callback runs within a tick (50 ms) of its time. A run the server missed (it was off at the time) is skipped, unless the schedule has `catch_up = true` and an `id`: then it runs once, on start. What a schedule last ran is kept in the server's store by `id`, per package.
---@field economy nf.economy Players' money, as the server's economy plugin keeps it, through [Vault](https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/vault.34315/): whichever economy is registered with it (EssentialsX, CMI, …) answers, so a project never names one. Needs Vault and an economy plugin. The plugin has to be on the server and enabled: a package that declares it (`requires`) while it isn't is a problem on the project's problem list until it is, and calling without it is an error. A plugin that is enabled after NetherForge counts from then on. The economy is looked up when it's asked, so one that registers late (after NetherForge starts) works from then on. Amounts are plain numbers in the economy's own currency; a player who is offline works as well as one who is online.
---@field placeholders nf.placeholders Placeholders, through [PlaceholderAPI](https://www.spigotmc.org/resources/placeholderapi.6245/): fill in the `%player_name%`-style placeholders every plugin offers, and offer your own as `%<namespace>_<key>%` to every plugin that reads them (scoreboards, chat formats, holograms). Needs PlaceholderAPI. The plugin has to be on the server and enabled: a package that declares it (`requires`) while it isn't is a problem on the project's problem list until it is, and calling without it is an error. A plugin that is enabled after NetherForge counts from then on.
---@field particles nf.particles The project's particle effects (`particles/<id>/effect.json`): timelines of particles authored in the editor, played at a place by scripts.
---@field cutscenes nf.cutscenes The project's cutscenes (`cutscenes/<id>.json`): camera paths keyed in the editor, played for one player at a time.
---@field screens nf.screens Screens: walls of maps, 128 by 128 pixels each, that scripts draw on (pixels, shapes, text, pictures) and play videos on. The project's own are its `screens/<id>/screen.json` files; a script can make more. What a screen shows is sent to each player who can see it, a change at a time, so drawing every tick costs only what changed.
---@field files nf.files Files in the package's own folder (`plugins/NetherForge/data/<namespace>/`), which no other package's scripts see unless they're handed a file: the only state a script has that survives a restart. The server owner limits how much each package may keep there (`files:` in `config.yml`); a write past it fails.
---@field text nf.text Helpers for the MiniMessage text players see: escaping what players typed, stripping tags, resource pack glyphs, moving the cursor, and measuring.
---@field looks nf.looks Resource pack looks (item models, glyphs, skins, tooltips, equipment looks, sounds) handed from one package's code to another's. A look is a value, not a use: any script can show any look the server has, by its full name `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>`, whether or not its package exports it or is one the script's package depends on. That's how a project hands a package it uses looks of its own to draw with (see [Packages](packages.md#looks-are-values)).
---@field json nf.json JSON text to and from Lua values.
---@field http nf.http Requests to web services (a Discord webhook, a status API). A package declares the hosts it talks to in `netherforge.json`'s `requires` (`"http": ["discord.com"]`, or `"*.discord.com"` for every name under it), and the server owner sees every host the project and its packages declare when it starts (`/nf requires`); a request to any other host is an error. Every request is held to the package whose code makes it. The server looks a name up once, checks the addresses it leads to and connects to exactly the address it checked: a loopback, private, link-local, shared (`100.64.0.0/10`), unique-local or IPv4-mapped address is refused even when its name is declared, unless the server owner turned on `http.allow-private-addresses` in `config.yml` (for a service on the same machine or network). Redirects (at most 5) are followed, and each one is held to the same rules, host declaration included. The server's proxy settings are never used. Only `http` and `https` URLs work. The server owner also limits requests in `config.yml`'s `http:` section: how large a request and a response may be, how long a request may take, and how many a package may make a minute. Connections that stay open (`stream`, `websocket`) are held to the same hosts and addresses, count as a request when they open, and have limits of their own in `http.connections`.
---@field web nf.web Web requests to the server: webhooks from stores, CI and chat services, "log in with…" callbacks, small pages and JSON for a companion site. A package declares `"requires": { "web": true }` in its `netherforge.json`, and its routes live under its own path, `/<namespace>/`, so packages can't collide. They're served only once the server owner turns web routes on (`web.enabled` in `config.yml`), on the port the resource pack is served from, usually behind a reverse proxy that adds HTTPS; until then a package that declares them has a warning (`runtime.web-disabled`). Every request waits for the main thread: handlers run there, under the script's budget, like any handler, and the owner limits how large a request may be, how many each address may make a minute and how many may wait (`web:` in `config.yml`). `/nf requires` lists every route.
---@field wasm nf.wasm Heavy computation in WebAssembly: a chess engine, pathfinding over a big grid, noise, compression, a library compiled from C, C++ or Rust. A module is `wasm/<id>/module.wasm` in the project (or a package's, exported), built for WASI preview 1 and single-threaded (`wasm32-wasip1`); every other file in its folder is data it can read, never write, as files from its root (`/`). It runs off the main thread, with no access to the server, the network or any file but its own data: it can only answer the script that asked. A package declares it runs modules with `"requires": { "wasm": true }`, and the server owner sees that (`/nf requires`) and limits it in `config.yml`'s `wasm:` section: how many threads run modules, how much memory each may use, how long a call may take, and how many instances and programs a package may keep. There are three ways to run one: `nf.wasm.instantiate` for a library whose functions you call (`instance:call`), `nf.wasm.run` for a program that reads its input and prints its answer, and `nf.wasm.start` for a program that keeps running and talks line by line (a UCI chess engine). The server compiles every module off the main thread as the project loads; one that couldn't be compiled fails when it's run, saying why (and the problem `runtime.wasm` says so too). A module the project doesn't have is an error at the script's line. A script that runs a module restarts when the module changes.
---@field network nf.network The other servers of the network this one is part of: a proxy (Velocity) running NetherForge's proxy plugin, which is the network's hub, and the servers behind it, each running NetherForge and connected to the hub (`network:` in their `config.yml`). Scripts publish messages on channels every server can listen to (`nf.network.channel`), ask one server and get its answer (`channel:request`), keep values every server sees in shared maps (`nf.network.map`), know who's online on which server (`players`, the `network_player_*` events), and send a player to another server with data that arrives with them (`send_player`). A package declares it with `"requires": { "network": true }`, and the server owner sees that (`/nf requires`). Channels and maps are named in the calling package's namespace, so packages never see each other's unless one depends on the other. Everything goes through the hub: a server that isn't connected (`is_connected()`) misses what's published meanwhile, and what it sends returns `false` or fails. Delivery is at most once, and in order between any two servers. On the editor's dev server, unless its `config.yml` puts it in a network, the server is a network of its own, named `"dev"`, so channels and maps work there with no proxy (sending players doesn't). A server whose owner hasn't put it in a network has none: its packages that declare it have the warning `runtime.network-disabled`.
---@field time nf.time Dates and durations as text, in the same unit as `nf.server.unix_time()`: milliseconds. For timers, use ticks (`nf.after`, `nf.wait`); this is for showing and reading clock times and lengths of time.
---@field random nf.random Randomness `math.random` doesn't give: generators of your own that repeat for a seed, smooth noise, and random ids.
---@field math nf.math Small arithmetic `math` doesn't have, for animating by hand: blending, clamping, rescaling and easing.
---@field test nf.test What a test file runs on. A project's tests are the `*_test.lua` files anywhere in it (`tests/shop_test.lua`), run by `netherforge test` (or the editor's Run tests) on a fake server: no Minecraft, no network, a world that holds only what the test puts in it. A test file runs like a module (it can `require` the project's modules, and files beside it) and registers its tests with `nf.test.case`; each test runs alone, on a fresh server with the project loaded and its scripts started, so one can never leave something behind for the next. The file's own code runs once for every test, before it, so it's where to `require` and build what the tests share. Nothing here exists on a real server: `nf.test` is `nil` there.
nf = {}

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this table doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event nf.Event A built-in event's name, or a custom event's: any name with a `:` in it, like `"shop:purchased"`.
---@param handler fun(event: CustomEvent) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(event: "tick", handler: fun(event: TickEvent), options?: EventOptions): Subscription
---@overload fun(event: "unload", handler: fun(event: Event)): Subscription
