--- Raises a custom event for every script: every `nf.on(event)` handler hears it now, in the order they were registered, before this returns. The payload is handed to them as it is, not copied, so a field a handler assigns is there for the caller to read afterwards. A name without a `:` is an error: built-in events are the server's to raise.
---@param event string A custom event's name, with a `:`: `"shop:purchased"`.
---@param payload? table The event's fields. Leave it out for none.
---@return boolean # Whether a handler cancelled it (`event:cancel()`), so a custom event can be vetoed.
function nf.emit(event, payload) end

--- Calls `callback` once, `ticks` server ticks from now (20 ticks are a second). Timers stop when the script unloads. One that errors is logged. To do several things with pauses in between, start a task (`nf.task`) and `nf.wait` in it.
---@param ticks integer How long to wait. 0 runs on the next tick.
---@param callback fun()
---@return Task # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop it first.
function nf.after(ticks, callback) end

--- Calls `callback` every `ticks` server ticks, starting `ticks` from now, until cancelled or the script unloads. One that errors is logged, and stops after 20 errors in a row.
---@param ticks integer At least 1.
---@param callback fun()
---@return Task
function nf.every(ticks, callback) end

--- Starts a task: `callback` runs now, with the extra arguments, until it first waits (`nf.wait`, `nf.wait_until`, `nf.wait_for`, `dialog:ask` or an asynchronous function like `nf.bots.join`, `nf.worlds.copy`, `nf.http.request`, `nf.wasm.instantiate`, `nf.wasm.run`, `nf.network.send_player`, `bot:walk_to`, `bot:break_block`, `world:fill_gradually`, `world:locate_biome`, `world:place_gradually`, `database:query`, `database:execute`, `database:transaction`, `wasminstance:call`, `wasminstance:call_text`, `networkchannel:request`, `networkmap:add` or `networkmap:insert` called without its callback), and carries on from there when the wait is over. Only a task can wait; event handlers can't, because whether an event is cancelled or stopped has to be known when it returns, so a handler that needs to wait starts a task. Each time a task carries on it gets the script's whole instruction budget again. A task belongs to the script that started it and ends when that script unloads, when `cancel()` is called, or when the handle it waits on goes. One that errors is logged with its file and line and ends; the code that started it carries on either way.
---@param callback async fun(...: any) The task: plain Lua that may wait.
---@param ... any Passed to `callback`.
---@return Task # Call `:cancel()` on it to end it early.
function nf.task(callback, ...) end

--- Pauses the task for `ticks` server ticks (20 are a second). It can't wait inside a function the server or Lua itself calls back (a `complete` function, `table.sort`'s comparator), nor inside a coroutine of your own; inside `pcall` is fine.
--- Only in a task's own code (`nf.task`): it waits, so calling it anywhere else (an event handler, a timer, a script's body) is an error.
---@param ticks integer How long. 0 waits for the next tick, like 1.
---@async
function nf.wait(ticks) end

--- Pauses the task until `predicate()` returns something other than `false` or `nil`. It's called once straight away and then once every tick, as the task's code.
--- Only in a task's own code (`nf.task`): it waits, so calling it anywhere else (an event handler, a timer, a script's body) is an error.
---@param predicate fun(): any
---@param timeout? integer Ticks to give up after. Leave it out to wait for as long as it takes.
---@return boolean # `true` once `predicate` held, `false` when the timeout ran out first.
---@async
function nf.wait_until(predicate, timeout) end

--- Pauses the task until `event` next happens on `handle` (or, for `nf`, anywhere on the server) and returns that event. The task carries on straight away, while the event is still being delivered, so until its next wait it can still `cancel()` or `stop()` the event or set its writable fields. If the handle goes first (a removed centity, a closed window, a dialog taken away by a reload), the task ends. An event the handle doesn't have is an error.
--- Only in a task's own code (`nf.task`): it waits, so calling it anywhere else (an event handler, a timer, a script's body) is an error.
---@param handle Centity|Node|Entity|World|ProjectBlock|Menu|Slot|MenuTemplate|Dialog|Button|ProjectItem|Effect|HttpStream|WebSocket|OAuthFlow|WasmProcess|NetworkChannel|NetworkMap|Cutscene|Screen|Playback|nf What the event happens to, as for `handle:on`; `nf` for a server-wide event.
---@param event string The event's name, as `handle:on` takes it.
---@param filter? fun(event: CustomEvent): boolean Waits for the first event this returns `true` for; the others pass by. Run as the task's code, so an error in it ends the task.
---@return CustomEvent # The event: its payload and its methods.
---@async
