--- A fake player a script drives (see `nf.bots`): a `Player` with everything players have, and the controls a player's client has. Every action is done as the client would do it, so what it causes is heard like anyone's (`player_move`, `player_interact`, `entity_damage`, …) and a handler that cancels it stops it. Actions that take ticks (`walk_to`, `break_block`) are asynchronous; a new one ends the one before it with an error, as `stop()` does. Every bot online is a `Bot`, those the editor's agents and tests drive included. Once it has left, every action answers `false` (and an asynchronous one `nil, err`).
---@class Bot: Player
local Bot = {}

--- Disconnects it, as a player quitting does: `player_quit` is heard, and it's gone for good (a bot never comes back: join a new one).
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:leave() end

--- Walks in a straight line to a position's x and z, as a player holding forward would: a step a tick, jumping up a block in its way, falling down drops and swimming. It doesn't find a way around a wall: it stops, stuck, when it hasn't come closer for a second, and when it's still short after `timeout` ticks. It takes as many ticks as the walk does, so its position arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `position, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `position, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `position` is `nil` and `err` says why.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param position Vec3 Where to: only its x and z matter.
---@param options? BotWalkOptions
---@param callback? fun(position: Vec3?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with where it arrived (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return Vec3? # Only without a callback, in a task: where it arrived, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: Bot, position: Vec3, options?: BotWalkOptions): Vec3?, string?
---@overload fun(self: Bot, position: Vec3, options?: BotWalkOptions, callback: fun(position: Vec3?, err: string?))
function Bot:walk_to(position, options, callback) end

--- Stops what it is doing over several ticks (walking, mining): that action ends with `nil` and an error saying it was stopped. Nothing to stop is fine.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:stop() end

--- Turns its head to look at a point, as its player would with the mouse: its next click aims there at once. (An `Entity`'s `look_at` turns it from the server's side, which a bot's client hears a tick later.)
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param point Vec3 A world position.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:look_at(point) end

--- Jumps, once it stands on something.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:jump() end

--- Holds sneak, or lets go.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param sneaking boolean
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:set_sneaking(sneaking) end

--- Sprints, or stops: it sprints as it walks.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param sprinting boolean
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:set_sprinting(sprinting) end

--- Says something in chat, as its player typing it. A message starting with `/` is a command it types.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param message string
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:chat(message) end

--- Hits an entity or a centity's node (a left click), turning to its middle first, and swings its arm. It must be within reach (3 blocks for an entity) and its client must have been sent it: an entity hidden from the bot, or too far away to be sent, can't be clicked.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param target Entity|Node What to hit: an entity, or a node with a hitbox.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:attack(target) end

--- Right-clicks an entity or a centity's node, turning to its middle first (or to `options.at`). It must be within reach (3 blocks for an entity) and its client must have been sent it: an entity hidden from the bot, or too far away to be sent, can't be clicked.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param target Entity|Node What to click: an entity, or a node with a hitbox.
---@param options? BotInteractOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:interact(target, options) end

--- Right-clicks with what it holds, at nothing: eats, draws a bow, throws a pearl. `release_item` lets go.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param off_hand? boolean With what it holds in its off hand. Default `false`.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:use_item(off_hand) end

--- Lets go of right-click while using an item: a drawn bow shoots, a raised shield lowers.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:release_item() end

--- Swings its arm at nothing (a left click at the air).
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:swing() end

--- Right-clicks a face of a block, turning to it first: opens a door or a chest, places what it holds against it. It must be within reach (4.5 blocks).
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param position Vec3 The block's position (any point inside it).
---@param face? "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which face. Default `"up"`.
---@param off_hand? boolean With what it holds in its off hand. Default `false`.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:use_block(position, face, off_hand) end

--- Mines a block, turning to it first, for as long as that takes with what it holds, as a player holding the button does. `nil, err` when there's no block there, it's out of reach (4.5 blocks), or the server didn't let it break. It takes as many ticks as the block takes to break, so its broken arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `broken, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `broken, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `broken` is `nil` and `err` says why.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param position Vec3 The block's position (any point inside it).
---@param callback? fun(broken: boolean?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with `true` (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return boolean? # Only without a callback, in a task: `true`, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: Bot, position: Vec3): boolean?, string?
---@overload fun(self: Bot, position: Vec3, callback: fun(broken: boolean?, err: string?))
function Bot:break_block(position, callback) end

--- Swaps what it holds in its hands (F).
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:swap_hands() end

--- Drops one of what it holds (Q), or the whole stack.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param stack? boolean The whole stack. Default `false`.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:drop_item(stack) end

--- Clicks a slot of the menu it has open, or of its inventory with none: the menu's own slots first, then its inventory's, as `slot` numbers them.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@param slot integer Which slot, from 0.
---@param click? "left"|"right"|"shift_left"|"shift_right"|"drop"|"drop_all"|"double"|"swap_offhand" How. Default `"left"`. `"swap_offhand"` is the swap-hands key (F) over the slot.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:click_slot(slot, click) end

--- Presses Respawn on the death screen. `false` when it isn't dead.
--- Needs `"requires": { "bots": true }` in `netherforge.json`: without it, calling it is an error.
---@return boolean # Whether it did: `false` once it has left (or while it's still joining).
function Bot:respawn() end

--- Whoever ran a command: a player or the console. What `event.sender` is in a command handler. For a player, `event.player` is the same person as a `Player`.
---@class Sender
local Sender = {}

--- Their name: a player's name, or `"CONSOLE"`.
---@return string
function Sender:name() end

--- Sends them a line: a chat line for a player, a log line for the console.
---@param text Text MiniMessage, like `"<green>Done!"`.
---@return boolean # `false` for a player who has gone offline.
function Sender:send_message(text) end

--- Whether they have a permission node. The console has every one.
---@param permission string
---@return boolean
function Sender:has_permission(permission) end

--- Whether a player ran it.
---@return boolean
function Sender:is_player() end

--- Whether the console ran it.
---@return boolean
function Sender:is_console() end

--- A world on the server (the overworld, the nether, the end, or another the server loads). A handle is its name, so keeping one is fine: if the world is unloaded its methods answer `nil` or `false`. Positions are world space, in blocks.
--- A `data()` table, `Item.data` and `nf.json` can keep one: it is saved as what identifies it and comes back as a handle.
---@class World
local World = {}

--- Its name, as `nf.worlds.get` takes it: `"world"`, `"world_nether"`.
---@return string
function World:name() end

--- Whether the server has it loaded now.
---@return boolean
function World:exists() end

--- What kind of world it is, or `nil` once it has gone. A world of a custom dimension type says `"normal"`.
---@return "normal"|"nether"|"end"|nil
function World:environment() end

--- A `Location` in this world, for the functions that take one (`player:teleport`, `nf.centities.spawn`). Give both `yaw` and `pitch`, or neither for a location with no facing; a `yaw` alone faces level.
---@param position Vec3 Where.
---@param yaw? number Which way it faces horizontally, in degrees (0 south, 90 west).
---@param pitch? number How far up or down, in degrees (−90 up, 90 down).
---@return Location
function World:location(position, yaw, pitch) end

--- The time of day in ticks, 0 to 23999: 0 is sunrise, 6000 noon, 12000 sunset, 18000 midnight. `nil` once the world has gone. It's the game's clock, not the wall clock (`nf.server.unix_time()`).
---@return integer?
function World:time_of_day() end

--- Sets the time of day, keeping the day count. Taken modulo 24000. Doesn't stop the clock (that's the daylight cycle game rule).
---@param ticks integer 0 sunrise, 6000 noon, 12000 sunset, 18000 midnight.
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_time_of_day(ticks) end

--- Whether it is night here: the time of day is 13000 to 22999, from dusk to just before sunrise, as `/time set night` and a centity's `"night"` spawn time mean it. `false` once the world has gone.
---@return boolean
function World:is_night() end

--- Whether it is day here: the time of day is 23000 to 12999, the rest of `is_night`'s. `false` once the world has gone.
---@return boolean
function World:is_day() end

--- How many whole days have gone by in this world, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return integer?
function World:day_count() end

--- Tonight's moon, or `nil` once the world has gone. It moves on a phase each day: full, waning gibbous, last quarter, waning crescent, new, waxing crescent, first quarter, waxing gibbous, then full again.
---@return "full_moon"|"waning_gibbous"|"last_quarter"|"waning_crescent"|"new_moon"|"waxing_crescent"|"first_quarter"|"waxing_gibbous"|nil
function World:moon_phase() end

--- The weather now, or `nil` once the world has gone. Thunder always comes with rain.
---@return "clear"|"rain"|"thunder"|nil
function World:weather() end

--- Changes the weather, for `options.ticks` or for as long as the game would have kept it. A `ticks` below 1 is an error, as is a key `options` doesn't take.
---@param weather "clear"|"rain"|"thunder"
---@param options? WeatherOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_weather(weather, options) end

--- The sky set over the world (`world:set_sky`), as the calling script names it, or `nil` for none (or once the world has gone). A player's own (`player:set_sky`) is theirs alone.
---@return string?
function World:sky() end

--- Sets a sky of the project's over the world (`skies/<id>.json`): everyone in it sees its colours in place of the biomes' own, the sky, fog, clouds and water, fading over `options.fade_ticks`, until it's cleared or another is set. Only what players are shown changes: the world, its biomes, its mobs and its weather are as they were, and a restart clears it. A player's own sky (`player:set_sky`) is shown them instead. A sky the project has no file for is an error, and so is one added since the server started (its colours are biomes, which the game learns as it starts: restart the server).
---@param sky string A sky of the project's (its file's name under `skies/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it depends on exports).
---@param options? SkyOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_sky(sky, options) end

--- Takes the world's sky away (`world:set_sky`): its biomes' own colours come back over `options.fade_ticks`. Nothing when it has none.
---@param options? SkyOptions
function World:clear_sky(options) end

--- Where players appear in this world when nothing else decides, or `nil` once the world has gone.
---@return Location?
function World:spawn_location() end

--- Moves the world's spawn. A `Location` in another world is an error.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where: a `Location` in this world (its facing is used, when it has one) or a position.
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_spawn_location(location_or_position) end

--- The lowest block y there can be, or `nil` once the world has gone.
---@return integer?
function World:min_height() end

--- One above the highest block y there can be, or `nil` once the world has gone.
---@return integer?
function World:max_height() end

--- The block at a position: a handle that reads the world live, so it sees later changes. The position is floored to the block it's in. `nil` once the world has gone.
---@param position Vec3 Any point in the block.
---@return Block?
function World:block(position) end

--- Every block in a box, read in one call: for code that needs many blocks at once (a mob's collision, a flood fill of a room), where a `world:block` and a `:kind()` per block would cost each one. The corners are floored to the blocks they're in and both are included, in any order. What each block is given as is `options.what`. At most 32,768 blocks (32 × 32 × 32): a bigger box is an error. `nil` when any of the box is in a chunk that isn't loaded, or the world has gone.
---@param from Vec3 One corner: any point in its block.
---@param to Vec3 The opposite corner, included: any point in its block.
---@param options? BlockBoxOptions
---@return BlockBox?
function World:blocks(from, to, options) end

--- Fills outward from a block through every block `options.through` names, one neighbour at a time, as far as it reaches: the blocks of a tree (`through = { "#minecraft:logs" }`), the air of a room (`through = { "passable" }`), a lake. It answers every block it filled (`positions`, nearest first by steps) and every block it stopped at (`edges`: a room's walls), with what each is when asked (`kinds`), and `stopped` says why it didn't finish when it didn't: it hit its `limit`, a chunk that isn't loaded or the world's top or bottom (`"unloaded"`: a room open to the sky or the unknown), or its `bounds`. It runs in the server, reading the blocks a box at a time, so a fill of thousands of blocks costs a script one call. A start block it can't fill through answers no positions, and the start as an edge. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't be read or changed: that answers `nil` or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk` does).
---@param start Vec3 Any point in the block to start from.
---@param options FloodOptions
---@return FloodResult? # `nil` once the world has gone.
function World:flood(start, options) end

--- Changes the block at a position. A block state the server doesn't have (an unknown block, property or value) is an error. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't be read or changed: that answers `nil` or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk` does).
---@param position Vec3 Any point in the block.
---@param state string A block state: `"minecraft:stone"`, `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east]"`. Properties left out take their defaults.
---@param options? BlockSetOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it changed: `false` in an unloaded chunk or a world that's gone.
function World:set_block(position, state, options) end

--- Sets every block in the box between two corners (both included) to one state. One call changes at most 32768 blocks: a bigger box is an error (`fill_gradually` spreads a big fill over several ticks). A block state the server doesn't have is an error. Blocks in unloaded chunks are skipped.
---@param from Vec3 One corner.
---@param to Vec3 The opposite corner.
---@param state string A block state, as `set_block` takes it.
---@param options? BlockSetOptions
---@return integer # How many blocks it changed: 0 in a world that has gone.
function World:fill_blocks(from, to, state, options) end

--- Sets every block in the box between two corners (both included) to one state, like `fill_blocks`, but over as many ticks as it takes, for a box too big for one call: up to 16777216 blocks (256 × 256 × 256). It goes a few thousand blocks a tick, shared with every other gradual placement and fill on the server (the owner's `structures.blocks-per-tick` in `config.yml`), so even a big one never stalls the server; each package may have a few going at once (`structures.max-jobs-per-package`), past which one fails at once. It goes a chunk's column at a time, from the bottom up, loading the chunks the box covers as it reaches them (`fill_blocks` skips unloaded ones). A bigger box, a block state the server doesn't have, or a `per_tick` below 1 is an error at once. The world going, or this script stopping, stops it part way, and what was set stays. It takes as many ticks as the fill does, so its changed arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `changed, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `changed, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `changed` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param from Vec3 One corner.
---@param to Vec3 The opposite corner.
---@param state string A block state, as `set_block` takes it.
---@param options? GradualFillOptions
---@param callback? fun(changed: integer?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with how many blocks it changed (a block that was already that state is not counted) (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return integer? # Only without a callback, in a task: how many blocks it changed (a block that was already that state is not counted), or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: World, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, state: string, options?: GradualFillOptions): integer?, string?
---@overload fun(self: World, from: Vec3, to: Vec3, state: string, options?: GradualFillOptions, callback: fun(changed: integer?, err: string?))
function World:fill_gradually(from, to, state, options, callback) end

--- The highest block at a column that isn't air, ignoring the position's `y`. `nil` in an unloaded chunk, or a world that has gone.
---@param position Vec3 Any point in the column.
---@return Block?
function World:highest_block(position) end

--- Whether the chunk a position is in is loaded: whether its blocks can be read and changed now.
---@param position Vec3 Any point in the chunk.
---@return boolean
function World:is_chunk_loaded(position) end

--- Loads the chunk a position is in, now, generating it if it's new: that can take a while, so do it rarely. The server unloads it again when nobody is near, as it does any chunk.
---@param position Vec3 Any point in the chunk.
---@return boolean # Whether it's loaded: `false` for a world that has gone.
function World:load_chunk(position) end

--- Finds the nearest place where the world's generator puts a biome, as `/locate biome` does: it asks the generator, so it finds places in chunks nobody has been to, and never loads or generates a chunk. It looks out from `options.near` in a square spiral, every 32 blocks across and 64 up and down, so the place it gives is in the biome but may be up to 32 blocks past its nearest edge, and a biome narrower than that can be missed. A biome the world's generator never places (a nether biome in the overworld) is found nowhere at once. An id that isn't a biome on this server (a bare id that isn't one of the project's is an error saying how to write the game's), or a `radius` out of range, is an error at once. Finding nothing within the radius, or a world that has gone, fails. It works off the main thread, so its position arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `position, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `position, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `position` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param id string The biome, named as a file names one: the project's own by its id (`"ruby_grove"`, `biomes/ruby_grove.json`), a package's `"acme:grove"` (if it exports it), the game's always written in full (`"minecraft:desert"`).
---@param options? BiomeSearchOptions
---@param callback? fun(position: Vec3?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the place it found: a block position in this world, at the height it found the biome (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return Vec3? # Only without a callback, in a task: the place it found: a block position in this world, at the height it found the biome, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: World, id: string, options?: BiomeSearchOptions): Vec3?, string?
---@overload fun(self: World, id: string, options?: BiomeSearchOptions, callback: fun(position: Vec3?, err: string?))
function World:locate_biome(id, options, callback) end

--- Everyone in this world now. Empty once the world has gone.
---@return Player[]
function World:players() end

--- The live centity instances in this world: the same as `nf.centities.all` with this world as the filter's `world`.
---@param filter? CentityFilter Only some of them, as `nf.centities.all` takes it. Its `world`, if given, must be this one.
---@return Centity[]
function World:centities(filter) end

--- The entities in this world's loaded chunks, players included (as `Player` handles), or those the filter picks. Never the entities centities are drawn with. Empty once the world has gone. A filter key that isn't one of `EntityFilter`'s is an error.
---@param filter? EntityFilter Only one kind, one tag, living ones, or those near a point.
---@return Entity[]
function World:entities(filter) end

--- Spawns an entity, set up as `options` say before anything sees it (its `entity_spawn` handlers included). A kind the server doesn't have, or one that can't be spawned (a player), is an error.
---@param kind string What: `"minecraft:zombie"`.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where: a `Location` in this world (facing its way, when it has a facing), or a position.
---@param options? EntitySpawnOptions
---@return Entity? # The new entity, or `nil` when the world has gone, its chunk isn't loaded, or an `entity_spawn` handler (or another plugin) cancelled it.
function World:spawn_entity(kind, location_or_position, options) end

--- Drops an item stack at a position, as if a block broke there. An item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have, is an error.
---@param position Vec3 Where, in this world.
---@param item Item
---@return DroppedItem? # The dropped item, or `nil` when the world has gone, its chunk isn't loaded, or something cancelled it.
function World:spawn_item(position, item) end

--- Spawns particles at a position. The particle's id comes from the server (`"minecraft:flame"`), and which options it takes depends on its kind: `color` and `size` for dust, `color` for coloured particles, `block_state` for block particles, `item` for item particles. An unknown particle, an option it doesn't take, or one it needs left out is an error. Particles that need data NetherForge can't send (vibrations, trails) are an error too. Players see them within 32 blocks, or 128 with `force`.
---@param particle string A particle id: `"minecraft:flame"`, `"minecraft:dust"`.
---@param position Vec3 Where, in the world.
---@param options? ParticleOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:spawn_particle(particle, position, options) end

--- Plays a sound at a position, for everyone near enough to hear it. A Minecraft sound (`"minecraft:block.note_block.pling"`, checked against the server's sounds) or a resource pack sound (`"<pack>/<key>"`, like `"ui/click"`, your package's own, or another loaded package's as `"<namespace>:<pack>/<key>"`: [looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)). An unknown sound is an error.
---@param sound string A sound id.
---@param position Vec3 Where it comes from, in the world.
---@param options? SoundOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:play_sound(sound, position, options) end

--- Makes an explosion at a position, as TNT would (TNT's power is 4): it damages and knocks back entities, and breaks blocks unless `break_blocks` is `false`.
---@param position Vec3 Its centre.
---@param power number How strong: 4 is TNT, 6 a charged creeper. At least 0.
---@param options? ExplosionOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it went off: `false` when another plugin stopped it, or the world has gone.
function World:explode(position, power, options) end

--- Strikes lightning at a position: it sets fire and hurts what it hits, unless `effect_only`.
---@param position Vec3 Where it lands.
---@param options? LightningOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:strike_lightning(position, options) end

--- Casts a ray from a point and answers the first thing it hits within `max_distance`: a block's collision shape, an entity's hitbox, or a centity's hitbox (tested with the same shapes clicks are). `nil` when it hits nothing, and in a world that has gone. Blocks in unloaded chunks aren't hit.
---@param from Vec3 Where the ray starts, in the world.
---@param direction Vec3 Which way it goes: any length but zero.
---@param max_distance number How far it reaches, in blocks.
---@param options? RaycastOptions
---@return RaycastHit?
function World:raycast(from, direction, max_distance, options) end

--- Everything whose hitbox is inside or touches a box, nearest the box's middle first. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
---@param min Vec3 One corner, in the world.
---@param max Vec3 The opposite corner. The corners may come in either order.
---@param options? QueryOptions
---@return QueryHit[]
function World:overlap_box(min, max, options) end

--- Everything whose hitbox comes within `radius` of a point, nearest first: what an explosion or an aura reaches. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
---@param center Vec3 The middle, in the world.
---@param radius number In blocks, at least 0.
---@param options? QueryOptions
---@return QueryHit[]
function World:overlap_sphere(center, radius, options) end

--- Everything whose hitbox reaches into a cone from a point, nearest first: what a swing or a breath in front of someone reaches. A hitbox counts when its point nearest the cone's axis is within `length` of the tip and within `angle` of the axis, or the tip is inside it. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
---@param origin Vec3 The cone's tip, in the world.
---@param direction Vec3 Which way it opens: any length but zero.
---@param length number How far it reaches from the tip, in blocks, at least 0.
---@param angle number How wide it opens, in degrees from its axis to its side: 0 to 180 (90 is everything in front, 180 a sphere).
---@param options? QueryOptions
---@return QueryHit[]
function World:overlap_cone(origin, direction, length, angle, options) end

--- Moves a sphere of `options.radius` (0, a point, by default) from one point to another and answers everything it touches on the way, in the order it meets them: what a fast or piercing shot passes through in a tick, which a ray (stopping at the first thing) can't say. Blocks stop it unless `blocks = false`: the block it meets is then the last hit, with its `normal`, and nothing past it is found. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
---@param from Vec3 Where it starts, in the world.
---@param to Vec3 Where it would end.
---@param options? SweepOptions
---@return QueryHit[]
function World:sweep(from, to, options) end

--- The nearest thing whose hitbox comes within `radius` of a point, or `nil` when there's nothing: what a homing shot or a mob looking for a target wants. With `line_of_sight = true`, only something a ray from the point reaches without a block in the way. It finds centities by their hitboxes (each shown node's boxes, as `raycast` and physics see them; a hidden node has none), players and other entities by theirs, as `options` allows: everything, by default. A centity is found once, by its nearest node. Only what's in loaded chunks is found. An empty list (or `nil`) in a world that has gone.
---@param position Vec3 Where to look from, in the world.
---@param radius number How far to look, in blocks, at least 0.
---@param options? QueryOptions
---@return QueryHit?
function World:nearest(position, radius, options) end

--- A game rule's value: a `boolean` or an `integer`, depending on the rule. `nil` once the world has gone. A rule the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param rule string Its id, as the server names it: `"keep_inventory"`, `"random_tick_speed"`.
---@return any
function World:game_rule(rule) end

--- Sets a game rule in this world. A rule the server doesn't have, or a value of the wrong type for it (a `boolean` rule given a number), is an error.
---@param rule string Its id: `"keep_inventory"`.
---@param value any A `boolean` or an `integer`, as the rule takes.
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_game_rule(rule, value) end

--- How many mobs of a spawn category the game keeps around each player here before it stops spawning more (the mob cap, per player, scaled by how many chunks are loaded around them), or `nil` once the world has gone. It's this world's own setting, or the server's (`bukkit.yml`) when the world has none.
---@param category "monster"|"animal"|"water_animal"|"water_ambient"|"water_underground_creature"|"ambient"|"axolotl" Which mobs count: `"monster"` for hostile ones, `"animal"` for farm animals, `"ambient"` for bats, and so on.
---@return integer?
function World:spawn_limit(category) end

--- Sets this world's mob cap for a spawn category (see `world:spawn_limit`). `0` stops that category spawning naturally. A negative number goes back to the server's own setting. Mobs already there stay.
---@param category "monster"|"animal"|"water_animal"|"water_ambient"|"water_underground_creature"|"ambient"|"axolotl" Which mobs count.
---@param limit integer The most of them there may be.
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_spawn_limit(category, limit) end

--- How many ticks pass between the game's attempts to spawn mobs of a spawn category here, or `nil` once the world has gone. It's this world's own setting, or the server's (`bukkit.yml`) when the world has none.
---@param category "monster"|"animal"|"water_animal"|"water_ambient"|"water_underground_creature"|"ambient"|"axolotl" Which mobs.
---@return integer?
function World:spawn_interval(category) end

--- Sets how many ticks pass between spawn attempts for a spawn category in this world (see `world:spawn_interval`): lower is faster. `0` stops that category spawning naturally. A negative number goes back to the server's own setting.
---@param category "monster"|"animal"|"water_animal"|"water_ambient"|"water_underground_creature"|"ambient"|"axolotl" Which mobs.
---@param ticks integer Ticks between attempts.
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:set_spawn_interval(category, ticks) end

--- Whether this project may unload it (`world:unload`): it's one the project's scripts created (`nf.worlds.create`, `nf.worlds.copy`) or named in `netherforge.json`'s `managedWorlds`, and it isn't the server's main world. Any script can find, load and use any world; only managed ones can be taken away.
---@return boolean
function World:is_managed() end

--- Unloads the world: players in it are moved out first (to the spawn of `options.move_players_to`, else of the main world), then the server saves it (unless `save` is `false`) and lets it go. With `delete`, its files are deleted too, off the main thread, and the project forgets it. Only a world this project manages can be unloaded (see `world:is_managed`): any other, the server's main world included, is an error, as is a `move_players_to` in this same world. A world it created stays the project's after a restart; find it again with `nf.worlds.load`.
---@param options? WorldUnloadOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it was unloaded: `false` when it had gone already, or the server (another plugin) kept it.
function World:unload(options) end

--- The world's border: the wall players can't pass, shown to everyone in the world (except a player given a border of their own with `player:border()`). `nil` once the world has gone.
---@return WorldBorder?
function World:border() end

--- Saves the box between two corners (both included) as a structure named `id`, to place later with `place_structure`: an arena to reset, a prefab to stamp. It's saved in the server's data folder, not the project, and kept across restarts; saving the same id again replaces it. An id that isn't an id (lowercase letters, digits and `_`), or that one of the project's structures (`structures/<id>.nbt`) or builds (`builds/<id>/`) already has, is an error, as is a box over 48 blocks along any side. Air is saved too, so placing it puts back the empty space as well; it loads the chunks the box covers, as `/place` does.
---@param id StructureId Its name: `"arena_reset"`.
---@param from Vec3 One corner.
---@param to Vec3 The opposite corner.
---@param options? StructureSaveOptions
---@return boolean # Whether it was saved: `false` when the world has gone, or the file couldn't be written.
function World:save_structure(id, from, to, options) end

--- Places a structure with its smallest corner at `position` (before it's turned): one of the project's (`structures/<id>.nbt`) or one a script saved (`save_structure`). Loads the chunks it covers, as `/place` does, all in this tick: `place_gradually` spreads a big one over several. A build (`builds/<id>/`) is always placed that way, so naming one here is an error, as is a structure there's none of, one whose file can't be read, a `rotation` that isn't a multiple of 90 or an `integrity` outside 0 to 1.
---@param id StructureId Which structure.
---@param position Vec3 Where its smallest corner goes, floored to a block.
---@param options? StructurePlaceOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when the world has gone.
function World:place_structure(id, position, options) end

--- Places a structure or a build (`builds/<id>/`, a structure too big for one file) like `place_structure`, its smallest corner at `position` before it's turned, but over as many ticks as it takes. It goes a few thousand blocks a tick, shared with every other gradual placement and fill on the server (the owner's `structures.blocks-per-tick` in `config.yml`), so even a big one never stalls the server; each package may have a few going at once (`structures.max-jobs-per-package`), past which one fails at once. Its blocks go in from the bottom up (a build's a part at a time), loading the chunks it covers as it reaches them, then its entities, once every block is in. Blocks go in as they were saved, with no updates to their neighbours, as `/place` puts them. A structure or build there's none of, a `rotation` that isn't a multiple of 90, an `integrity` outside 0 to 1 or a `per_tick` below 1 is an error at once. A part that can't be read, the world going, or this script stopping stops it part way, and what was placed stays. It takes as many ticks as the placing does, so its placed arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `placed, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `placed, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `placed` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param id StructureId Which structure or build.
---@param position Vec3 Where its smallest corner goes, floored to a block.
---@param options? GradualPlaceOptions
---@param callback? fun(placed: integer?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with how many blocks it placed (those `integrity` left out, or outside the world's heights, are not counted) (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return integer? # Only without a callback, in a task: how many blocks it placed (those `integrity` left out, or outside the world's heights, are not counted), or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: World, id: StructureId, position: Vec3, options?: GradualPlaceOptions): integer?, string?
---@overload fun(self: World, id: StructureId, position: Vec3, options?: GradualPlaceOptions, callback: fun(placed: integer?, err: string?))
function World:place_gradually(id, position, options, callback) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event World.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_break", handler: fun(event: BlockBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_place", handler: fun(event: BlockPlaceEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "entity_spawn", handler: fun(event: EntitySpawnEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "dusk", handler: fun(event: DaylightEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "dawn", handler: fun(event: DaylightEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_ignite", handler: fun(event: BlockIgniteEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_burn", handler: fun(event: BlockBurnEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_spread", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_flow", handler: fun(event: BlockFlowEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_grow", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_fade", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_form", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "leaves_decay", handler: fun(event: BlockEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "piston_extend", handler: fun(event: PistonEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "piston_retract", handler: fun(event: PistonEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_redstone", handler: fun(event: BlockRedstoneEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "sign_change", handler: fun(event: SignChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_start_break", handler: fun(event: BlockStartBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_drop_item", handler: fun(event: BlockDropItemEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "weather_change", handler: fun(event: WeatherChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "thunder_change", handler: fun(event: ThunderChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "lightning_strike", handler: fun(event: LightningStrikeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "chunk_load", handler: fun(event: ChunkLoadEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "chunk_generated", handler: fun(event: ChunkEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "chunk_unload", handler: fun(event: ChunkEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "furnace_smelt", handler: fun(event: FurnaceSmeltEvent)): Subscription
function World:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `world:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event World.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_break", handler: fun(event: BlockBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_place", handler: fun(event: BlockPlaceEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "entity_spawn", handler: fun(event: EntitySpawnEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "dusk", handler: fun(event: DaylightEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "dawn", handler: fun(event: DaylightEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_explode", handler: fun(event: ExplodeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_ignite", handler: fun(event: BlockIgniteEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_burn", handler: fun(event: BlockBurnEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_spread", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_flow", handler: fun(event: BlockFlowEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_grow", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_fade", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_form", handler: fun(event: BlockChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "leaves_decay", handler: fun(event: BlockEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "piston_extend", handler: fun(event: PistonEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "piston_retract", handler: fun(event: PistonEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_redstone", handler: fun(event: BlockRedstoneEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "sign_change", handler: fun(event: SignChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_start_break", handler: fun(event: BlockStartBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "block_drop_item", handler: fun(event: BlockDropItemEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "weather_change", handler: fun(event: WeatherChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "thunder_change", handler: fun(event: ThunderChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "lightning_strike", handler: fun(event: LightningStrikeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "chunk_load", handler: fun(event: ChunkLoadEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "chunk_generated", handler: fun(event: ChunkEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "chunk_unload", handler: fun(event: ChunkEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: World, event: "furnace_smelt", handler: fun(event: FurnaceSmeltEvent)): Subscription
function World:once(event, handler) end

--- A border: the wall at the edge of a square area, which players can't walk through and which hurts them outside it. Either a world's (`world:border()`), which everyone in the world sees, or one player's own (`player:border()`), which only they see and which the server draws for them but doesn't enforce: it doesn't hurt them, and doesn't stop what isn't them. Sizes and positions are blocks; times are ticks (20 a second). Its methods answer `nil` or `false` once its world has gone, or for a player's own, once they've left or gone back to their world's (`player:reset_border()`).
---@class WorldBorder
local WorldBorder = {}

--- Its centre, with `y` 0, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return Vec3?
function WorldBorder:center() end

--- Moves its centre. `y` is ignored. More than 29,999,984 blocks out is an error.
---@param center Vec3 Where.
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_center(center) end

--- How wide it is, side to side, in blocks: while it's growing or shrinking, how wide it is now. `nil` once it has gone.
---@return number?
function WorldBorder:size() end

--- Changes how wide it is: at once, or moving steadily there over `options.ticks`. A size below 1 or over 59,999,968, or negative `ticks`, is an error.
---@param size number Side to side, in blocks.
---@param options? BorderSizeOptions
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_size(size, options) end

--- How it hurts players outside it, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return BorderDamage?
function WorldBorder:damage() end

--- Changes how it hurts players outside it; what `damage` leaves out stays as it was. A negative value is an error. A player's own border is only drawn, so this changes nothing for them.
---@param damage BorderDamage
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_damage(damage) end

--- When players' screens turn red as it comes near, or `nil` once it has gone.
---@return BorderWarning?
function WorldBorder:warning() end

--- Changes when players' screens turn red; what `warning` leaves out stays as it was. A negative value is an error.
---@param warning BorderWarning
---@return boolean # `false` once it has gone.
function WorldBorder:set_warning(warning) end

--- Whether a place is inside it. A `Location` in another world is outside a world's border; a player's own border has no world, so only the position counts. `false` once it has gone.
---@param location_or_position Location|Vec3 Where: a `Location`, or a position.
---@return boolean
function WorldBorder:contains(location_or_position) end

--- One block position in one world. The handle is the position, not what's there: it reads the world live, so a `Block` kept from earlier sees later changes. Blocks in chunks that aren't loaded can't be read or changed: that answers `nil` or `false`, and never loads the chunk (`world:load_chunk` does).
---@class Block
local Block = {}

--- The world it's in.
---@return World
function Block:world() end

--- Its corner with the smallest x, y and z: whole numbers, in the world.
---@return Vec3
function Block:position() end

--- Its corner as a `Location`, with no facing.
---@return Location
function Block:location() end

--- What block it is, like `"minecraft:stone"`, or `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return string?
function Block:kind() end

--- Its whole block state, like `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"` (properties sorted), or `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return string?
function Block:state() end

--- One property's value, like `"east"` for `facing`, or `nil` when the block has no such property or its chunk isn't loaded.
---@param name string The property: `"facing"`.
---@return string?
function Block:property(name) end

--- Every property and its value; empty for a block without any, and `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return table<string, string>?
function Block:properties() end

--- Whether it's air (any kind). `false` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return boolean
function Block:is_air() end

--- Whether it's solid: something players stand on and can't walk through. `false` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return boolean
function Block:is_solid() end

--- Whether it's water or lava. `false` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return boolean
function Block:is_liquid() end

--- Changes it, as `world:set_block` does. A block state the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param state string A block state: `"minecraft:stone"`.
---@param options? BlockSetOptions
---@return boolean # `false` in an unloaded chunk.
function Block:set_state(state, options) end

--- Changes one property, keeping the rest of its state. A property the block doesn't have, or a value it can't take, is an error.
---@param name string The property: `"facing"`.
---@param value string Its new value: `"north"`.
---@return boolean # `false` in an unloaded chunk.
function Block:set_property(name, value) end

--- Breaks it as a player would: it drops what it would drop (for that tool, when given) and turns to air.
---@param tool? Item What it was broken with, for its drops: silk touch, fortune, or the wrong tool dropping nothing.
---@return boolean # Whether it broke: `false` for air, or in an unloaded chunk.
function Block:break_naturally(tool) end

--- The block at an offset from this one.
---@param offset Vec3|"up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" An offset in blocks (floored), or the face of the neighbour: `"up"` is the block above.
---@return Block
function Block:relative(offset) end

--- What a container block holds (a chest, a barrel, a hopper, a furnace, a shulker box), as an `Inventory`: the same one players see. A double chest's is both halves. `nil` for a block that isn't a container, and in an unloaded chunk.
---@return Inventory?
function Block:inventory() end

--- The project block that's here, as a `CustomBlock` (the same handle: one position, one block), or `nil` when what's here isn't one of the project's blocks, or its chunk isn't loaded.
---@return CustomBlock?
function Block:custom() end

--- How bright it is there, 0 to 15: the brighter of block light and sky light. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return integer?
function Block:light_level() end

--- How much sky light reaches it, 0 to 15, whatever the time of day. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return integer?
function Block:sky_light_level() end

--- The biome here, named as `locate_biome` takes one: a biome of the project reads as its id (`"ruby_grove"`), a package's as `"acme:grove"` (to a package's own scripts, its own is bare), the game's as `"minecraft:plains"`. The game keeps biomes per 4×4×4 blocks, so neighbouring blocks share one. `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return string?
function Block:biome() end

--- This position's own table for scripts to keep things in, saved with the chunk: the same table every time, shared by every script. Change it in place; it's saved when the server autosaves, when the chunk unloads and when the server stops. It stays with the position, whatever happens to the block. It may hold strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them (keyed by strings, or a list numbered from 1), and the API's own values: `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player`, `Entity`, `Centity` and `World` handles, which come back as the same types (a handle to something gone answers `nil` or `false`, as handles do). Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window, a `Subscription`) is skipped when it's saved, with a line in the log naming where it was (`data.inventory[3]: a function can't be saved`). `nil` in an unloaded chunk.
---@return table?
function Block:data() end

--- A block of the project's own at a position, which is a `Block` like any other: it reads the world live, so one kept from earlier turns into whatever is there now (`block:custom()` says whether it still is one). The game holds it as a state of its carrier (a note block's, tripwire's or a leaves block's) its resource pack draws as the block, so `kind()` and `state()` answer that; `id()` and `project()` say which block it is. `data()` is its own table: it starts empty when the block is placed and is gone when the block is, whatever replaced it. `break_naturally` rolls its loot table; replacing or filling over it with `set_state` or `world:fill_blocks` removes it without drops.
---@class CustomBlock: Block
local CustomBlock = {}

--- Its id: its project block's folder name under `blocks/`, or `namespace:id` for one a package it depends on exports, as the calling script names it. `nil` once what's here isn't a block of the project's.
---@return string?
function CustomBlock:id() end

--- The project block it's one of, or `nil` once what's here isn't one.
---@return ProjectBlock?
function CustomBlock:project() end

--- The way its front faces, for a block that turns (`rotation` in its `block.json`): what it was placed facing, or turned to since. `nil` for a block that doesn't turn, or once what's here isn't one.
---@return "north"|"east"|"south"|"west"|"up"|"down"?
function CustomBlock:facing() end

--- Turns it to face another way, keeping everything else about it: its table, its centity (turned with it). `false` when it doesn't turn that way (it doesn't turn, or turns only `horizontal` and this is up or down), or once what's here isn't one of the project's blocks.
---@param facing "north"|"east"|"south"|"west"|"up"|"down" Which way its front faces.
---@return boolean
function CustomBlock:set_facing(facing) end

--- A project block from `blocks/<id>/block.json`: `this` in the block's script, and what `nf.blocks.get` hands out. It's the kind of block, not one placed in the world: its handlers hear what players do with any block of it (see `CustomBlock` for one placed). If the block is deleted, its methods answer `nil` or `false` and its handlers go.
---@class ProjectBlock
local ProjectBlock = {}

--- Its id: its folder name under `blocks/`.
---@return string
function ProjectBlock:id() end

--- Its `properties` from its `block.json`: the project's own facts about it, any JSON (`{ damage = 12, class = "melee" }`), as a table. A new table each call, so keep it rather than ask in a loop. An empty table when the file has none, `nil` once the block is gone.
---@return table?
function ProjectBlock:properties() end

--- Whether the project still has it.
---@return boolean
function ProjectBlock:exists() end

--- Places one at the block position a location is in, as `world:set_block` would: whatever is there is replaced, and nothing drops. Its `place` handlers are not asked (a script placing a block isn't a player placing one), and it isn't a `block_place` either. If the block has a centity, it's spawned over the block. `nil` when the chunk isn't loaded, or when the game has no state of its carrier left to hold the block in (the project has more blocks of that carrier than that).
---@param location Location Where.
---@param options? BlockPlaceOptions
---@return CustomBlock?
function ProjectBlock:place(location, options) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event ProjectBlock.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "place", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockPlaceEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "break", handler: fun(event: BlockBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "tick", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockTickEvent)): Subscription
function ProjectBlock:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `block:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event ProjectBlock.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "place", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockPlaceEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "break", handler: fun(event: BlockBreakEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: ProjectBlock, event: "tick", handler: fun(event: ProjectBlockTickEvent)): Subscription
function ProjectBlock:once(event, handler) end

--- A real inventory: a player's (`player:inventory()`), their ender chest, a storage of the project's that they keep (`player:storage(name, size)`), a container block's (`block:inventory()`: a chest, a barrel, a hopper, a furnace) or an entity's (a chest boat, a donkey, a villager). Its item methods work like `Menu`'s. The handle is where the inventory is, so it reads live; once that's gone (a chest broken, a player offline, a chunk unloaded) its methods answer `nil`, `false` or nothing. Slots are numbered from 0. A player's: the hotbar 0 to 8, the rest of the main inventory 9 to 35, armour 36 (feet), 37 (legs), 38 (chest) and 39 (head), the offhand 40, then body armour 41 and a saddle 42 (what the game keeps there for any entity). A double chest's inventory is both halves, 0 to 53.
---@class Inventory
local Inventory = {}

--- Whether it's there now: the player online, the container still a container, the entity in the world and its chunk loaded.
---@return boolean
function Inventory:exists() end

--- What kind of inventory it is: `"player"`, `"ender_chest"`, `"storage"`, `"chest"`, `"barrel"`, `"shulker_box"`, `"hopper"`, `"dispenser"`, `"dropper"`, `"furnace"`, `"brewing"`, … `nil` once it's gone.
---@return string?
function Inventory:kind() end

--- How many slots it has: 43 for a player, 27 for a chest, 54 for a double chest. 0 once it's gone.
---@return integer
function Inventory:size() end

--- Whose it is: the `Player` (for a player's inventory, ender chest and storage), the `Entity`, or the `Block`. `nil` once it's gone.
---@return Entity|Block|nil
function Inventory:holder() end

--- What is in a slot, or `nil` for an empty one (or once it's gone). A slot outside it is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@return Item?
function Inventory:item(index) end

--- Puts an item in a slot, replacing what was there, or empties it. A slot outside it, an item table with a misspelled field, or an item the server doesn't have is an error.
---@param index integer From 0.
---@param item? Item Leave it out (or `nil`) to empty the slot.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Inventory:set_item(index, item) end

--- Every item in it, keyed by slot. Empty slots are left out, so `pairs` visits only what is there.
---@return table<integer, Item>
function Inventory:items() end

--- Puts an item wherever it fits, topping up matching stacks first, the way picking something up does. In a player's inventory only the hotbar and main inventory take it, never armour or the offhand.
---@param item Item
---@return integer # How many didn't fit: 0 when all of it went in, all of it once the inventory's gone.
function Inventory:add_item(item) end

--- Takes away items that match, from the first slot on, until `count` are gone or none are left.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@param count? integer How many at most. Leave it out for every one that matches.
---@return integer # How many it took.
function Inventory:remove_item(match, count) end

--- How many items match, across every slot.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@return integer
function Inventory:count_item(match) end

--- Whether at least `count` items match.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@param count? integer Default 1.
---@return boolean
function Inventory:has_item(match, count) end

--- The first slot holding an item that matches, or `nil` when none does.
---@param match string|ItemMatch An item kind (`"minecraft:diamond"`), or a partial `Item` that matches a stack when every field it gives matches: `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }`. Its `data` matches as a subset, so `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@return integer?
function Inventory:first_slot(match) end

--- Empties every slot. To empty one, `set_item(index, nil)`.
---@return boolean # `false` once it's gone.
function Inventory:clear() end

--- The players who have it open on their screen now: for a storage, those looking at a window with a slot bound to it. (A player's own inventory, open in their own inventory screen, doesn't count: the server can't tell.)
---@return Player[]
function Inventory:viewers() end

--- A bar across the top of players' screens, made by `nf.bossbars.create`. It shows to the players it's shown to (`show_to`), and stays theirs when they leave and come back. It belongs to the script that made it and goes when that script unloads (or `remove()`): from then on its methods answer `nil` or `false`.
---@class BossBar
local BossBar = {}

--- Whether it's still there: `false` once it's removed or its script has unloaded.
---@return boolean
function BossBar:exists() end

--- Shows it to a player, now if they're online and again whenever they join.
