---@return boolean # `false` when the socket has ended (or just ended over the limit), so nothing was sent.
function WebSocket:send(text) end

--- Ends it now, with reason `"closed"`: the other side is told with `code` and `reason`, and messages that arrived but weren't handed over yet are thrown away.
---@param code? integer The close code: `1000` (a normal close, the default) or one from `3000` to `4999`, which applications define.
---@param reason? string Why, for the other side to read: at most 123 bytes.
---@return boolean # `false` when it had already ended.
function WebSocket:close(code, reason) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event WebSocket.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: WebSocket, event: "open", handler: fun(event: WebSocketOpenEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: WebSocket, event: "message", handler: fun(event: WebSocketMessageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: WebSocket, event: "end", handler: fun(event: WebSocketEndEvent)): Subscription
function WebSocket:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `socket:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event WebSocket.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: WebSocket, event: "open", handler: fun(event: WebSocketOpenEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: WebSocket, event: "message", handler: fun(event: WebSocketMessageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: WebSocket, event: "end", handler: fun(event: WebSocketEndEvent)): Subscription
function WebSocket:once(event, handler) end

--- A route a script answers, from `nf.web.route`. It goes when its script unloads or `remove` is called.
---@class Route
local Route = {}

--- Stops answering: later requests to it are `404`, and requests still waiting are answered `503`.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already gone.
function Route:remove() end

--- Whether it still answers.
---@return boolean
function Route:exists() end

--- How a route's handler answers its request: once, with `send`, `json` or `redirect`, during the handler or later (from a task, or a callback). Every answer also says `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and `Cache-Control: no-store` unless you set one. One not answered within `web.handler-timeout-seconds` is answered `504`; one whose route's script unloads first, `503`. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
---@class WebResponse
local WebResponse = {}

--- Answers with `status`, `body` and `headers`. The `Content-Type` is `text/plain; charset=utf-8` unless `headers` sets one (`["content-type"] = "text/html; charset=utf-8"` for a page). A body over `web.max-response-bytes`, a status that isn't one, or a header that can't be sent (one the server sets, like `Content-Length`) is an error at the script's line.
---@param status integer The status code, 100 to 599: `200`, `204`, `404`.
---@param body? string The body, as text (UTF-8). Default empty.
---@param headers? table<string, string> Response headers by name.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already answered (or timed out), so nothing was sent.
function WebResponse:send(status, body, headers) end

--- Answers with `status` and `value` written as JSON (`application/json`), as `nf.json.encode` writes it.
---@param status integer The status code, 100 to 599.
---@param value any What to write: a table, a string, a number, a boolean.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already answered.
function WebResponse:json(status, value) end

--- Answers with a redirect to `url` (`302`, or `status`: `301`, `303`, `307` or `308`).
---@param url string Where to: an absolute URL, or a path on this server.
---@param status? integer Default `302`.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already answered.
function WebResponse:redirect(url, status) end

--- Whether it still waits for an answer: `false` once it's answered, timed out, or its route's script unloaded.
---@return boolean
function WebResponse:is_pending() end

--- A "log in with…" flow, from `nf.web.oauth`: `begin` a login for a player, and hear its `complete` or `fail`. It belongs to the script that made it.
---@class OAuthFlow
local OAuthFlow = {}

--- Begins a login for `player`: the address of the service's page to send them to (as a link in chat), with a new state and code challenge that only this login's callback can use, for 10 minutes. `nil` while the server owner hasn't turned web routes on, or once the flow has gone.
---@param player Player Who logs in: their `complete` or `fail` names them.
---@return string?
function OAuthFlow:begin(player) end

--- Ends the flow: its callback route goes and logins under way are forgotten.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already gone.
function OAuthFlow:remove() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event OAuthFlow.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: OAuthFlow, event: "complete", handler: fun(event: OAuthCompleteEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: OAuthFlow, event: "fail", handler: fun(event: OAuthFailEvent)): Subscription
function OAuthFlow:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `flow:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event OAuthFlow.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: OAuthFlow, event: "complete", handler: fun(event: OAuthCompleteEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: OAuthFlow, event: "fail", handler: fun(event: OAuthFailEvent)): Subscription
function OAuthFlow:once(event, handler) end

--- An instance of a WebAssembly module, from `nf.wasm.instantiate`: its own memory, kept from call to call, so a function can build state another reads. Its calls run off the main thread one at a time, in the order they were made. It belongs to the script that made it and is closed when that script unloads. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
---@class WasmInstance
local WasmInstance = {}

--- The module it is an instance of, as the calling script names it.
---@return string
function WasmInstance:module() end

--- Calls one of the functions the module exports and gives its results. It's stopped past `wasm.max-call-seconds`; a call that traps (an out-of-bounds read, `unreachable`, a division by zero) or is stopped fails with `nil, err`, and the instance is closed after it (its memory may be half-written). It works off the main thread, so its results arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `results, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `results, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `results` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param name string The exported function: `"sample"`.
---@param arguments? table The arguments, in order, one for each of the function's parameters: an `integer` for an `i32` or `i64`, a `number` for an `f32` or `f64`. A `string` is copied into the module's memory and passed as two `i32`s, where it starts and how many bytes it is (UTF-8): the module must export its `memory` and an `alloc(size) -> pointer` (or `malloc`) to copy it into, and the server frees it after the call with the module's `dealloc(pointer, size)` (or `free(pointer)`) when it exports one, so a function that keeps the text copies it. A count or type that doesn't match the function is an error at the script's line.
---@param callback? fun(results: any[]?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with its results in order (an `i32` or `i64` as an `integer`, an `f32` or `f64` as a `number`), empty for a function that returns nothing (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return any[]? # Only without a callback, in a task: its results in order (an `i32` or `i64` as an `integer`, an `f32` or `f64` as a `number`), empty for a function that returns nothing, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: WasmInstance, name: string, arguments?: table): any[]?, string?
---@overload fun(self: WasmInstance, name: string, arguments?: table, callback: fun(results: any[]?, err: string?))
function WasmInstance:call(name, arguments, callback) end

--- Calls a function that answers with text: it returns two `i32`s (where the text starts and how many bytes it is) or one `i64` holding both (the start in its high 32 bits). The text is read from the module's memory as UTF-8, then freed as `call` frees a string argument. Otherwise as `call`: for text in and text out, JSON included. It works off the main thread, so its text arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `text, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `text, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `text` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param name string The exported function: `"plan"`.
---@param arguments? table The arguments, in order, one for each of the function's parameters: an `integer` for an `i32` or `i64`, a `number` for an `f32` or `f64`. A `string` is copied into the module's memory and passed as two `i32`s, where it starts and how many bytes it is (UTF-8): the module must export its `memory` and an `alloc(size) -> pointer` (or `malloc`) to copy it into, and the server frees it after the call with the module's `dealloc(pointer, size)` (or `free(pointer)`) when it exports one, so a function that keeps the text copies it. A count or type that doesn't match the function is an error at the script's line.
---@param callback? fun(text: string?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the text it answered (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return string? # Only without a callback, in a task: the text it answered, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: WasmInstance, name: string, arguments?: table): string?, string?
---@overload fun(self: WasmInstance, name: string, arguments?: table, callback: fun(text: string?, err: string?))
function WasmInstance:call_text(name, arguments, callback) end

--- Closes it now, freeing its memory: calls still waiting fail, and later ones do at once. Unloading the script that made it closes it too.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already closed.
function WasmInstance:close() end

--- A program started with `nf.wasm.start`: `send` it lines and hear each line it prints as `output`, then its one `end`. It runs on a thread of its own, waiting while it reads input nobody has sent. It belongs to the script that started it and ends with reason `"unloaded"` when that script unloads (a reload, the server stopping). Once it has ended its methods answer as an ended program does rather than erroring. Ephemeral: it can't be saved in a `data()` table.
---@class WasmProcess
local WasmProcess = {}

--- The module it runs, as the calling script names it, even after it has ended.
---@return string
function WasmProcess:module() end

--- `"running"` until its `end` event, then `"ended"`.
---@return "running"|"ended"
function WasmProcess:state() end

--- Writes a line to its input, with a line ending after it: the program reads it when it next reads. A line over `wasm.max-line-bytes` is an error at the script's line.
---@param line string The line, without its line ending: `"go movetime 500"`.
---@return boolean # `false` when it has ended, its input was closed, or it already holds `wasm.max-queued-lines` lines it hasn't read yet (send again once it has), so nothing was sent.
function WasmProcess:send(line) end

--- Ends its input: once it has read what was sent, it reads the end of the file, which most programs answer by finishing what they were doing and exiting.
---@return boolean # `false` when it had already ended or its input was closed.
function WasmProcess:close_input() end

--- Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`: lines it printed that weren't handed over yet are thrown away.
---@return boolean # `false` when it had already ended.
function WasmProcess:stop() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event WasmProcess.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: WasmProcess, event: "output", handler: fun(event: WasmOutputEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: WasmProcess, event: "end", handler: fun(event: WasmEndEvent)): Subscription
function WasmProcess:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `engine:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event WasmProcess.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: WasmProcess, event: "output", handler: fun(event: WasmOutputEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: WasmProcess, event: "end", handler: fun(event: WasmEndEvent)): Subscription
function WasmProcess:once(event, handler) end

--- A channel of the network, from `nf.network.channel`: publish on it with `publish`, ask one server with `request`, and hear messages and requests as its `message` and `request` events. A server listens to a channel while any of its scripts has a handler on it; handlers go with their scripts, as any handler does.
---@class NetworkChannel
local NetworkChannel = {}

--- Its name, as the calling package names it: bare when it's the caller's own, `namespace:name` otherwise.
---@return string
function NetworkChannel:name() end

--- Publishes `value` on the channel: every server listening hears it as a `message` event, this one included, or only `options.server`. It goes at once, through the hub; nothing waits for it. Anything a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans, `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64 KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an error at the script's line.
---@param value any What to send.
---@param options? NetworkPublishOptions Which server alone, if not every one.
---@return boolean # `false` when it didn't go: this server isn't connected, or its package went over `network.messages-per-minute`.
function NetworkChannel:publish(value, options) end

--- Asks `server` on this channel: its `request` handlers answer, and the answer comes back here. It fails (`nil, err`) when this server isn't connected, that server isn't, nothing there handles requests on the channel, a handler there errored, or no answer came within `network.request-timeout-seconds`. A handler that answers `nil` gives `nil` with no `err`. Anything a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans, `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64 KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an error at the script's line. It works off the main thread, so its reply arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `reply, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `reply, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `reply` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param server NetworkServerName The server's name in the network, as the proxy names it (`"lobby"`); `nf.network.servers()` lists them.
---@param data any What to ask with: their handlers read it as `event.value`.
---@param callback? fun(reply: any?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the answer, what that server's handlers assigned to `event.reply` (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return any? # Only without a callback, in a task: the answer, what that server's handlers assigned to `event.reply`, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: NetworkChannel, server: NetworkServerName, data: any): any?, string?
---@overload fun(self: NetworkChannel, server: NetworkServerName, data: any, callback: fun(reply: any?, err: string?))
function NetworkChannel:request(server, data, callback) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event NetworkChannel.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: NetworkChannel, event: "message", handler: fun(event: NetworkMessageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: NetworkChannel, event: "request", handler: fun(event: NetworkRequestEvent)): Subscription
function NetworkChannel:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `channel:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event NetworkChannel.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: NetworkChannel, event: "message", handler: fun(event: NetworkMessageEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: NetworkChannel, event: "request", handler: fun(event: NetworkRequestEvent)): Subscription
function NetworkChannel:once(event, handler) end

--- A map the whole network shares, from `nf.network.map`: string keys, any values. Reads (`get`, `all`) answer at once from this server's copy. Writes go to the hub, which makes them in the order they arrive and tells every server that has the map: `set` and `delete` don't wait, and this server's copy has the new value once the hub has made the change (the next tick or so, when `change` is heard); `add` and `insert` wait for the hub's answer, so they're safe when two servers write one key at once. A key may expire. The hub keeps them for the whole network, saved on the proxy, within the limits of its `hub.properties` (how many keys and bytes each package may keep).
---@class NetworkMap
local NetworkMap = {}

--- Its name, as the calling package names it: bare when it's the caller's own, `namespace:name` otherwise.
---@return string
function NetworkMap:name() end

--- Whether this server's copy has arrived from the hub: until then `get` answers `nil` and `all` an empty table. Its `ready` event says when.
---@return boolean
function NetworkMap:is_ready() end

--- A key's value in this server's copy, or `nil` when it has none (or the copy hasn't arrived). A table comes back new each call: changing it changes nothing.
---@param key string The key.
---@return any
function NetworkMap:get(key) end

--- Every key and its value in this server's copy.
---@return table<string, any>
function NetworkMap:all() end

--- Sets a key, for the whole network: through the hub, without waiting. Anything a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans, `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64 KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an error at the script's line. A key is 1 to 256 characters. What the hub refuses (a package over its limits) is logged.
---@param key string The key.
---@param value any Its value. `nil` deletes it, as `delete` does.
---@param options? NetworkMapWriteOptions When the key expires, if it should.
---@return boolean # `false` when it didn't go: this server isn't connected, or its package went over `network.messages-per-minute`.
function NetworkMap:set(key, value, options) end

--- Deletes a key, for the whole network: through the hub, without waiting.
---@param key string The key.
---@return boolean # `false` when it didn't go: this server isn't connected, or its package went over `network.messages-per-minute`.
function NetworkMap:delete(key) end

--- Adds `amount` to a key's number, made by the hub in one step: two servers adding at once both count. A key with no value starts at 0; one holding something that isn't a number fails. It keeps the expiry it had, unless `options.expires` gives a new one. It works off the main thread, so its total arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `total, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `total, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `total` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param key string The key.
---@param amount number What to add; negative to take away.
---@param options? NetworkMapWriteOptions When the key expires, if it should.
---@param callback? fun(total: number?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with the key's value after adding (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return number? # Only without a callback, in a task: the key's value after adding, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: NetworkMap, key: string, amount: number, options?: NetworkMapWriteOptions): number?, string?
---@overload fun(self: NetworkMap, key: string, amount: number, options?: NetworkMapWriteOptions, callback: fun(total: number?, err: string?))
function NetworkMap:add(key, amount, options, callback) end

--- Sets a key to `data` only if it has no value, made by the hub in one step: of two servers inserting one key at once, exactly one gets `true`. A claim, a lock or a reservation: give it `options.expires` so a server that stops holding it can't keep it forever. It works off the main thread, so its inserted arrives later: give `callback`, which is called with `inserted, err` once it's done, or call it inside a task (`nf.task`) without one, and it waits and returns `inserted, err`. Without a callback outside a task, it's an error. When it fails, `inserted` is `nil` and `err` says why.
---@param key string The key.
---@param data any Its value. Anything a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans, `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64 KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an error at the script's line.
---@param options? NetworkMapWriteOptions When the key expires, if it should.
---@param callback? fun(inserted: boolean?, err: string?) Called on the main thread once it's done, as this script's code, with `true` when it was set, `false` when the key already had a value (or `nil` and why not). Not called if the script has stopped by then.
---@return boolean? # Only without a callback, in a task: `true` when it was set, `false` when the key already had a value, or `nil`.
---@return string? # Why it failed, when it did; `nil` when it worked.
---@overload async fun(self: NetworkMap, key: string, data: any, options?: NetworkMapWriteOptions): boolean?, string?
---@overload fun(self: NetworkMap, key: string, data: any, options?: NetworkMapWriteOptions, callback: fun(inserted: boolean?, err: string?))
function NetworkMap:insert(key, data, options, callback) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event NetworkMap.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: NetworkMap, event: "change", handler: fun(event: NetworkMapChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: NetworkMap, event: "ready", handler: fun(event: NetworkMapReadyEvent)): Subscription
function NetworkMap:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `map:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event NetworkMap.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: NetworkMap, event: "change", handler: fun(event: NetworkMapChangeEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: NetworkMap, event: "ready", handler: fun(event: NetworkMapReadyEvent)): Subscription
function NetworkMap:once(event, handler) end

--- One cutscene playing for one player, from `nf.cutscenes.play`. Once it has ended (it finished, was stopped or skipped, was replaced by another, its player left, or the script that played it unloaded) its methods answer `nil` or `false` rather than erroring, except `kind()` and `length()`. A cutscene is ephemeral: it has no `id()` and can't be saved in a `data()` table.
---@class Cutscene
local Cutscene = {}

--- Which cutscene it is: its file name under `cutscenes/`, even after it has ended.
---@return string
function Cutscene:kind() end

--- How long it lasts, in seconds, even after it has ended.
---@return number
function Cutscene:length() end

--- Who is watching. `nil` once it has ended.
---@return Player?
function Cutscene:player() end

--- Whether it's still playing. `false` once it has ended, for any reason (its `end` handlers still see `true`).
---@return boolean
function Cutscene:is_active() end

--- Seconds played so far. `nil` once it has ended.
---@return number?
function Cutscene:time() end

--- Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`, and puts the player back.
---@return boolean # `false` when it had already ended.
function Cutscene:stop() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Cutscene.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Cutscene, event: "end", handler: fun(event: CutsceneEndEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Cutscene, event: "cue", handler: fun(event: CutsceneCueEvent)): Subscription
function Cutscene:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `cutscene:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Cutscene.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Cutscene, event: "end", handler: fun(event: CutsceneEndEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Cutscene, event: "cue", handler: fun(event: CutsceneCueEvent)): Subscription
function Cutscene:once(event, handler) end

--- A screen: maps (each 128 by 128 pixels) side by side, which scripts draw on and play videos on, shown in item frames (`place`, or the file's `place`) or as map items in players' hands (`item`). Pixel `0, 0` is its top left. What it shows is the server's to send and isn't saved: draw it whenever its script starts. Once a screen is gone (a script's screen whose script unloaded, a project screen whose file was deleted) its drawing does nothing and its getters answer `nil` or `false`.
---
--- A screen is drawn for every player near it (`viewers = "near"`), or only for those a script shows it to. `for_player` gives a player a view of their own: the same maps, drawn differently for them alone.
---@class Screen
local Screen = {}

--- The project's screen it is (its folder name under `screens/`), or the `key` a script made it with; `nil` for a script's screen made without one.
---@return string?
function Screen:name() end

--- Whether it's still there.
---@return boolean
function Screen:exists() end

--- How wide it is, in pixels: 128 a map.
---@return integer
function Screen:width() end

--- How tall it is, in pixels: 128 a map.
---@return integer
function Screen:height() end

--- Fills the whole screen with one colour. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param color? string A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through. Default `"clear"`.
function Screen:clear(color) end

--- One pixel's colour, as the map draws it: `#rrggbb`, or `"clear"`. `nil` for a pixel off the screen, or once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer From the left edge, 0 first.
---@param y integer From the top edge, 0 first.
---@return string?
function Screen:pixel(x, y) end

--- Sets one pixel. One off the screen is left alone. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer From the left edge, 0 first.
---@param y integer From the top edge, 0 first.
---@param color string? A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through. `nil` is `"clear"`.
function Screen:set_pixel(x, y, color) end

--- A block of pixels' colours, row by row (`#rrggbb`, or `"clear"`): what `set_pixels` takes. Pixels off the screen are `"clear"`; an empty list once the screen is gone. A block of more than 65536 pixels is an error.
---@param x integer The block's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The block's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param width integer In pixels.
---@param height integer In pixels.
---@return string[]
function Screen:pixels(x, y, width, height) end

--- Sets a block of pixels from a list of colours, row by row, `width` to a row: the quick way to draw many pixels at once. What's off the screen is left alone. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer The block's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The block's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param width integer Pixels in a row of `colors`.
---@param colors string[] Each pixel's colour. A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through.
function Screen:set_pixels(x, y, width, colors) end

--- Fills a rectangle with one colour. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer The rectangle's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The rectangle's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param width integer In pixels.
---@param height integer In pixels.
---@param color string A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through.
function Screen:fill(x, y, width, height, color) end

--- Draws a rectangle: filled, outlined, or both, with rounded corners if `style.radius` says. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer The rectangle's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The rectangle's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param width integer In pixels.
---@param height integer In pixels.
---@param style ShapeStyle Its fill and outline.
function Screen:rectangle(x, y, width, height, style) end

--- Draws a straight line between two pixels, both included. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x1 integer Where it starts.
---@param y1 integer
---@param x2 integer Where it ends.
---@param y2 integer
---@param color string A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through.
---@param thickness? integer In pixels, 1 to 32 (default 1).
function Screen:line(x1, y1, x2, y2, color, thickness) end

--- Draws a circle round a pixel: filled, outlined, or both. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer Its middle, from the left edge.
---@param y integer Its middle, from the top edge.
---@param radius integer In pixels, 0 or more.
---@param style ShapeStyle Its fill and outline (`radius` is not used).
function Screen:circle(x, y, radius, style) end

--- Draws an ellipse round a pixel. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer Its middle, from the left edge.
---@param y integer Its middle, from the top edge.
---@param radius_x integer Across, in pixels.
---@param radius_y integer Down, in pixels.
---@param style ShapeStyle Its fill and outline (`radius` is not used).
function Screen:ellipse(x, y, radius_x, radius_y, style) end

--- Draws a closed shape through points, `{ x1, y1, x2, y2, … }` (at least three points). Fewer than three, or an odd count of numbers, is an error. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param points integer[] The corners, each an x then a y.
---@param style ShapeStyle Its fill and outline (`radius` is not used).
function Screen:polygon(points, style) end

--- Writes text with its top left at a pixel. Lines break at `\n`, and at spaces past `options.width` when it's given. MiniMessage colours, `<b>`, `<u>` and `<st>` are drawn; glyphs and other tags aren't. An unknown font is an error. Returns how big it was, as `nf.screens.measure_text` would say (`0` by `0` once the screen is gone).
---@param x integer The text's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The text's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param text Text What to write.
---@param options? ScreenTextOptions Font, colour, size, alignment.
---@return ScreenTextSize
function Screen:text(x, y, text, options) end

--- Draws a picture: a texture of a resource pack, as `<pack>/<path>` (`ui/logo` is `resource_packs/ui/textures/logo.png`). Its colours become map colours as `options.dither` says; pixels less than half opaque draw nothing. A picture the project doesn't have is an error. Returns `false` when the picture can't be read. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer The picture's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The picture's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param image string The texture, `<pack>/<path>` without `.png`.
---@param options? ScreenImageOptions Its size and dithering.
---@return boolean
function Screen:image(x, y, image, options) end

--- Copies a rectangle of the screen to another place on it (overlapping is fine): a quick way to scroll. What's off the screen is left alone. Nothing happens once the screen is gone.
---@param x integer The rectangle copied's left, in pixels from the screen's left edge.
---@param y integer The rectangle copied's top, in pixels from the screen's top edge.
---@param width integer In pixels.
---@param height integer In pixels.
---@param to_x integer Where its left goes.
---@param to_y integer Where its top goes.
function Screen:copy(x, y, width, height, to_x, to_y) end

--- Hangs it on a wall: an invisible, fixed item frame a map, its top left map at `location`'s block (the air in front of the wall) and the rest going right and down as a player facing it sees them. The frames need no wall behind them. A screen already hanging somewhere is moved. A project screen hangs where its file says again when the server restarts. Returns `false` when the world isn't loaded.
---@param location Location Where its top left map goes.
---@param facing "north"|"south"|"east"|"west" The way it faces: out of the wall, towards whoever looks at it.
---@return boolean
function Screen:place(location, facing) end

--- Takes its frames down. It can still be drawn on, and its map items still show it.
---@return boolean # `false` when it hung nowhere.
function Screen:take_down() end

--- Where its top left map hangs; `nil` when it hangs nowhere.
---@return Location?
function Screen:location() end

--- Which way it faces where it hangs; `nil` when it hangs nowhere.
---@return ("north"|"south"|"east"|"west")?
function Screen:facing() end

--- A map item showing one of its maps, to give a player or put in a frame: `column` and `row` count from 0 at its top left. A map off the screen is an error. A screen made without a `key` gets new map numbers each time it's made, so its items show nothing after a restart.
---@param column? integer Which map across, 0 first (default 0).
---@param row? integer Which map down, 0 first (default 0).
---@return Item
function Screen:item(column, row) end

--- Draws it for a player, whoever it's drawn for otherwise (a `viewers = "script"` screen is drawn only for those it's shown to). Lasts until `hide_from`, or the screen goes.
---@param player Player Who.
---@return boolean # `false` when they are offline or the screen is gone.
function Screen:show_to(player) end

--- Stops drawing it for a player (they see blank maps, and can't click it), until `show_to`.
---@param player Player Who.
---@return boolean # `false` when they are offline or the screen is gone.
function Screen:hide_from(player) end

--- Whether it is drawn for a player now: shown to them, or near them on a `"near"` screen, and not hidden from them.
---@param player Player Who.
---@return boolean
function Screen:is_shown_to(player) end

--- Everyone it is drawn for now.
---@return Player[]
function Screen:viewers() end

--- A player's own view of the screen: drawing on it changes what they see alone (the same maps, the same frames), starting from what everyone sees now. It lasts until `release`, they leave, or the screen goes; drawing on the shared screen meanwhile doesn't reach them. Called on a player's own view, it's that view.
---@param player Player Whose view.
---@return Screen
function Screen:for_player(player) end

--- Whose own view this is; `nil` for the screen everyone sees.
---@return Player?
function Screen:player() end

--- Ends a player's own view (see `for_player`): they see what everyone sees again.
---@return boolean # `false` when this isn't a player's own view, or it has ended.
function Screen:release() end

--- Plays a video (`videos/<id>/`) on the screen, from its top left (`options.x`, `options.y` move it), replacing any video playing on it (that one ends, reason `"replaced"`). Its sound plays to the players it's drawn for who are near where it hangs. It belongs to the script that played it: when that script unloads, it ends. A video the project doesn't have is an error; one not yet converted in the editor gives `nil`.
---@param video VideoId Its folder name under `videos/`.
---@param options? VideoPlayOptions How it plays.
---@return Playback? # `nil` when it can't be played, or the screen is gone.
function Screen:play(video, options) end

--- The video playing on it now; `nil` when none is.
---@return Playback?
function Screen:playback() end

--- Takes a script's screen away for good: its frames come down, and its video ends. A screen made with a `key` gives up its maps' numbers too: one made again with that key gets new ones, and an item or frame of the old ones shows nothing. A project screen can't be removed (its file says it's there): that's an error.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was already gone.
function Screen:remove() end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Screen.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Screen, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ScreenClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Screen, event: "hover", handler: fun(event: ScreenHoverEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Screen, event: "leave", handler: fun(event: ScreenLeaveEvent)): Subscription
function Screen:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `screen:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Screen.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Screen, event: "click", handler: fun(event: ScreenClickEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Screen, event: "hover", handler: fun(event: ScreenHoverEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Screen, event: "leave", handler: fun(event: ScreenLeaveEvent)): Subscription
function Screen:once(event, handler) end

--- A video playing on a screen, from `screen:play`. Once it has ended its methods answer `nil` or `false`, except `video()` and `length()`; `exists()` says whether it has.
---@class Playback
local Playback = {}

--- Which video it is: its folder name under `videos/`, even after it has ended.
---@return string
function Playback:video() end

--- The screen it plays on; `nil` once it has ended.
---@return Screen?
function Playback:screen() end

--- How long the video lasts, in seconds, even after it has ended.
---@return number
function Playback:length() end

--- Whether it hasn't ended: paused counts.
---@return boolean
function Playback:exists() end

--- Whether it is paused.
---@return boolean
function Playback:is_paused() end

--- Holds it on the frame it shows, its sound stopped.
---@return boolean # `false` when it was paused already, or has ended.
function Playback:pause() end

--- Plays it on from where it was paused, its sound from there too.
---@return boolean # `false` when it wasn't paused, or has ended.
function Playback:resume() end

--- Ends it now, with reason `"stopped"`. The screen keeps its last frame.
---@return boolean # `false` when it had already ended.
function Playback:stop() end

--- Where it is, in seconds from the video's start; `nil` once it has ended.
---@return number?
function Playback:time() end

--- Jumps to a time in the video, in seconds (held to 0 and its length); its sound starts again from there.
---@param seconds number Where to.
---@return boolean # `false` when it has ended.
function Playback:seek(seconds) end

--- How fast it plays: 1 is as recorded. `nil` once it has ended.
---@return number?
function Playback:speed() end

--- Plays it faster or slower, 0.25 to 4 (held to that). Its sound plays only at speed 1.
---@param speed number? The new speed; `nil` is 1.
---@return boolean # `false` when it has ended.
function Playback:set_speed(speed) end

--- Its sound's volume, 0 to 1; `nil` once it has ended.
---@return number?
function Playback:volume() end

--- Changes its sound's volume, 0 to 1 (held to that); its sound starts again at the new volume.
---@param volume number? The new volume; `nil` is 1.
---@return boolean # `false` when it has ended.
function Playback:set_volume(volume) end

--- Whether it starts again when it reaches its end.
---@return boolean
function Playback:is_looping() end

--- Whether it starts again when it reaches its end.
---@param looping boolean Loop or not.
---@return boolean # `false` when it has ended.
function Playback:set_looping(looping) end

--- Calls `handler` every time `event` happens to this, until the subscription is cancelled or its script unloads, or this goes away. A handler lives as long as both the script that registered it and the handle it's on. Handlers run in the order they were registered, each with the event object; what one returns means nothing. A handler that errors is logged and the rest still run; after 20 errors in a row it's cancelled. An event this class doesn't have is an error, so a typo is caught where it was made.
---@param event Playback.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event: its payload, plus `stop()`, `cancel()` and the rest of `Event`.
---@param options? EventOptions How to listen: `{ every = n }` for `tick`.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop listening.
---@overload fun(self: Playback, event: "end", handler: fun(event: PlaybackEndEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Playback, event: "frame", handler: fun(event: PlaybackFrameEvent)): Subscription
function Playback:on(event, handler, options) end

--- Like `playback:on`, but the handler runs only the next time the event happens, then stops listening.
---@param event Playback.Event The event's name.
---@param handler fun(event: Event) Called with the event.
---@return Subscription # Call `:cancel()` on it to stop waiting before then.
---@overload fun(self: Playback, event: "end", handler: fun(event: PlaybackEndEvent)): Subscription
---@overload fun(self: Playback, event: "frame", handler: fun(event: PlaybackFrameEvent)): Subscription
function Playback:once(event, handler) end

--- A path in the scripts' data directory (`plugins/NetherForge/data/`, on the editor's dev server too). The only state a script has that survives a restart. Files are capped at 1 MiB; writes past it are refused, not truncated.
---@class File
local File = {}

--- The path from the data directory; `""` for the directory itself.
---@return string
function File:path() end

--- The last part of the path.
---@return string
function File:name() end

--- The folder it's in, or `nil` for the data directory itself.
---@return File?
function File:parent() end

--- Something inside this folder, whether or not it exists yet. `nil` for a name that isn't allowed.
---@param name string One name, no `/`.
---@return File?
function File:child(name) end

--- Whether anything is there.
---@return boolean
function File:exists() end

--- Whether it's a folder.
---@return boolean
function File:is_folder() end

--- Its size in bytes, or `nil` when it isn't a file.
---@return integer?
function File:size() end

--- What's directly inside, sorted by name; empty for a file or nothing.
---@return File[]
function File:children() end

--- The contents, or `nil` when there's no file.
---@return string?
function File:read() end

--- Replaces the contents, creating folders above it as needed.
---@param text string
---@return boolean # `false` for a folder, past the size cap, or on a disk error.
function File:write(text) end

--- Adds to the end.
---@param text string
---@return boolean
function File:append(text) end

--- The contents parsed as JSON, as `nf.json.decode` does, or `nil` when there's no file or it isn't JSON.
---@return any
function File:read_json() end

--- Writes a value as JSON, as `nf.json.encode` does.
---@param value any
---@return boolean # `false` for something JSON can't hold (a function, a table inside itself) or a failed write.
function File:write_json(value) end

--- When it last changed, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`), or `nil` when there's nothing there.
---@return integer?
function File:modified_time() end

--- An iterator over its lines, for a `for` loop: each without its line ending (`\n` or `\r\n`). Nothing for a file that isn't there.
---@return fun(): string?
function File:lines() end

--- Moves it (a file, or a folder and everything in it) to another path in the data directory, creating folders above it as needed. Nothing already there is overwritten.
---@param path string From the data directory, as `nf.files.get` takes it.
---@return File? # It at its new path; `nil` when there's nothing to move, the path isn't allowed or is taken, or the move failed.
function File:rename(path) end

--- Copies a file to another path in the data directory, creating folders above it as needed. Nothing already there is overwritten. Folders can't be copied.
---@param path string From the data directory, as `nf.files.get` takes it.
---@return File? # The copy; `nil` when it isn't a file, the path isn't allowed or is taken, or the copy failed.
function File:copy_to(path) end

--- Creates the folder, and any above it.
---@return boolean
function File:create_folder() end

--- Deletes a file or an empty folder.
---@return boolean
function File:delete() end

--- What `:on` and `:once` return: one handler listening for one event on one handle (or on `nf`). A handler lives as long as **both** the script that registered it and the handle it's on: a module's handler on a centity's node goes when the module unloads or the centity is removed, whichever comes first; one on a menu window goes with the window. A handler that errors 20 times in a row is cancelled.
---@class Subscription
local Subscription = {}

--- Stops the handler being called. Cancelling one already cancelled does nothing.
function Subscription:cancel() end

--- Whether the handler is still listening: `false` once it's cancelled (by `cancel()`, after a `once` handler ran, or after 20 errors in a row), its script has unloaded, or the handle it's on has gone.
---@return boolean
function Subscription:is_active() end

--- What `nf.task`, `nf.after` and `nf.every` return: a task that may be waiting, or a callback waiting to run.
---@class Task
local Task = {}

--- Ends it: a task stops where it's waiting and never carries on; a callback doesn't run (again). A task that cancels itself stops at its next wait. Cancelling one that has ended does nothing.
function Task:cancel() end

--- Whether it will still run: `false` once it's cancelled, once a task has finished, errored or lost the handle it was waiting on, once an `nf.after` callback has run, after an `nf.every` callback's 20th error in a row, or once its script has unloaded.
---@return boolean
function Task:is_active() end

--- What `nf.schedule.daily`, `weekly` and `cron` return: a schedule that will run at its next time, until it is cancelled or its script unloads.
---@class Schedule
local Schedule = {}

--- Ends it: it doesn't run again. What it last ran is kept, so a schedule made again with the same `id` doesn't catch up a run it already made. Cancelling one that has ended does nothing.
function Schedule:cancel() end

--- Whether it will still run: `false` once it's cancelled or its script has unloaded.
---@return boolean
function Schedule:is_active() end

--- When it runs next, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`; `nf.time.format` writes it as a date). `nil` once it has ended.
---@return integer?
function Schedule:next_run() end

--- A random generator from `nf.random.new`: numbers that repeat for its seed, apart from `math.random` and every other generator.
---@class Random
local Random = {}

--- A whole number from `min` to `max`, both included, each as likely as the others. `min` greater than `max` is an error.
---@param min integer
---@param max integer
---@return integer
function Random:integer(min, max) end

--- A number from 0 up to (not including) 1, or from `min` up to `max`.
---@param min? number Give both or neither.
---@param max? number
---@return number
function Random:number(min, max) end

--- One item of a list, each as likely as the others. `nil` for an empty list.
---@param list table A list, numbered from 1.
---@return any
function Random:pick(list) end

--- Puts a list in a random order, in place.
---@param list table A list, numbered from 1.
function Random:shuffle(list) end

--- One key of a table, picked with odds in proportion to its number: in `{ common = 3, rare = 1 }`, `"common"` comes up three times in four. Keys with weight 0 never come up; `nil` when there are none with more. A weight that is negative or not a number is an error. The same seed picks the same keys when the keys are strings, numbers or booleans.
---@param weights table<any, number> Each choice, with its weight.
---@return any
function Random:weighted(weights) end

--- One player's HUD: the elements of the project's resource packs (`hud` in `pack.json`: bars, rows of icons, lines of text, each at its place on the screen), shown with what a script sets here. An element nobody set isn't drawn. It's drawn in their action bar, sent again whenever something changes and every second, so `player:send_actionbar` shows its line alongside it while the HUD is up (on its own for a moment, when the server can't measure it). What it shows is the server's to remember, not saved: set it again when they join. A name that isn't one of the packs' elements, or is another kind of element, is an error.
---@class Hud
local Hud = {}

--- Whose HUD it is.
---@return Player
function Hud:player() end

--- Fills a bar: `value` out of `max`, shown as that much of its segments (to the nearest half segment, for a bar with a half picture). A `value` past `max` or below 0 is shown full or empty; a `max` of 0 or less is an error. With `segments`, only that many of the bar's segments are drawn (1 to the bar's own), placed by the bar's `align`.
---@param name string The bar, as `<pack>/<key>`: a key of a resource pack's `hud.bars` (a package's as `ns:<pack>/<key>`).
---@param value number
---@param max number
---@param options? HudFillOptions
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Hud:fill_bar(name, value, max, options) end

--- Fills several bars as one long bar, each `per_segment` of `max` a segment: as many segments are drawn as `max` needs, filling the first bar's, then the next's (rows of hearts, ten to a row). A bar no segment reaches is removed. Every name must be a bar; `per_segment` and `max` more than 0.
---@param names string[] Bars, `<pack>/<key>`, in the order they fill.
---@param value number
---@param max number
---@param per_segment number How much of `max` a segment stands for.
---@return integer # How many segments were drawn in all; 0 when they're offline.
function Hud:fill_bars(names, value, max, per_segment) end

--- What a bar shows, or `nil` when nothing set it (or they're offline).
---@param name string The bar, as `<pack>/<key>`: a key of a resource pack's `hud.bars` (a package's as `ns:<pack>/<key>`).
---@return HudBar?
function Hud:bar(name) end

--- Shows these of a row's icons, in this order: status effects, buffs, keys held. An empty list shows none. An icon the row doesn't have is an error.
---@param name string The row of icons, as `<pack>/<key>`: a key of a resource pack's `hud.icons` (a package's as `ns:<pack>/<key>`).
---@param icons string[] Keys of the row's `icons`.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Hud:set_icons(name, icons) end

--- The icons a row shows, in order, or `nil` when nothing set it (or they're offline).
---@param name string The row of icons, as `<pack>/<key>`: a key of a resource pack's `hud.icons` (a package's as `ns:<pack>/<key>`).
---@return string[]?
function Hud:icons(name) end

--- Sets a line of text, on the action bar's own line where the line's `x` and `align` put it. The server measures it with the game's font (`fonts/default.json`) to put it there; one it can't measure isn't drawn.
---@param name string The line of text, as `<pack>/<key>`: a key of a resource pack's `hud.texts` (a package's as `ns:<pack>/<key>`).
---@param text Text MiniMessage; glyphs are fine.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline.
function Hud:set_text(name, text) end

--- A line's text, or `nil` when nothing set it (or they're offline).
---@param name string The line of text, as `<pack>/<key>`: a key of a resource pack's `hud.texts` (a package's as `ns:<pack>/<key>`).
---@return Text?
function Hud:text(name) end

--- Stops drawing one element (any kind), until something sets it again.
---@param name string A HUD element, `<pack>/<key>`.
---@return boolean # `false` when they're offline, or nothing was drawn for it.
function Hud:remove(name) end

--- Stops drawing every element: their action bar is the game's again.
function Hud:clear() end

--- Three numbers: a position, an offset, a velocity, a direction or a scale. Every place the API takes or gives one of those, it's a `Vec3`. Build one with `vec3(x, y, z)`. Vectors are immutable: methods and operators return a new vector, and assigning to `v.x` is an error. `a == b` compares the numbers exactly, and `tostring(v)` is `"vec3(1, 2, 3)"`.
---@class Vec3
---@field x number East (+) and west (−).
---@field y number Up (+) and down (−).
---@field z number South (+) and north (−).
---@operator add(Vec3): Vec3
---@operator sub(Vec3): Vec3
---@operator unm: Vec3
---@operator mul(number): Vec3
---@operator mul(Vec3): Vec3
---@operator div(number): Vec3
local Vec3 = {}

--- How long it is.
---@return number
function Vec3:length() end

--- Its length squared: cheaper than `length()`, and enough for comparing distances.
---@return number
function Vec3:length_squared() end

--- The same direction with length 1. The zero vector stays zero rather than becoming NaN.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:normalized() end

--- The dot product.
---@param other Vec3
---@return number
function Vec3:dot(other) end

--- The cross product: a vector at right angles to both, by the right-hand rule.
---@param other Vec3
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:cross(other) end

--- How far it is to another point.
---@param other Vec3
---@return number
function Vec3:distance(other) end

--- The distance squared: cheaper than `distance()`, and enough for comparing.
---@param other Vec3
---@return number
function Vec3:distance_squared(other) end

--- The point `fraction` of the way to `other`: 0 is this vector, 1 is `other`.
---@param other Vec3
---@param fraction number Usually 0 to 1; outside that it carries on past either end.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:lerp(other, fraction) end

--- The same direction, scaled down to at most `max_length` long: a copy if it is no longer than that already. Keeps a velocity under a top speed.
---@param max_length number The longest it may be, 0 or more.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:clamp_length(max_length) end

--- Mirrored off a surface: `v - 2 * v:dot(n) * n` for the surface's unit normal `n`, so the part into the surface turns back out and the part along it carries on. How a shot bounces off a wall, with a sweep's `normal`.
---@param normal Vec3 Which way the surface faces. Any length but zero: it is normalized first.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:reflect(normal) end

--- The point on the line segment from `start` to `finish` nearest to this point: its foot on the segment, or the nearer end when that falls past one. `start` when the two are the same point. With `distance`, how far a point is from a beam or a path.
---@param start Vec3 One end.
---@param finish Vec3 The other end.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:closest_on_segment(start, finish) end

--- A copy with `y` set to 0: the horizontal part.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:flat() end

--- A copy with `x` replaced.
---@param x number
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:with_x(x) end

--- A copy with `y` replaced.
---@param y number
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:with_y(y) end

--- A copy with `z` replaced.
---@param z number
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:with_z(z) end

--- Each component rounded down: the block a position is in.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:floor() end

--- Each component rounded to the nearest whole number, halves up.
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:round() end

--- Turned about an axis through the origin, by the right-hand rule: with your right thumb along the axis, positive degrees turn the way your fingers curl. About `vec3.up`, 90 degrees turns east into north.
---@param axis Vec3 Any length but zero.
---@param degrees number
---@return Vec3
function Vec3:rotated(axis, degrees) end

--- The three numbers, for code that wants them separately.
---@return number # x
---@return number # y
---@return number # z
function Vec3:unpack() end

--- Builds vectors (`vec3(x, y, z)`), and holds the common ones and `vec3.from_yaw_pitch`.
---@class vec3
---@field zero Vec3 `vec3(0, 0, 0)`.
---@field one Vec3 `vec3(1, 1, 1)`.
---@field up Vec3 `vec3(0, 1, 0)`.
---@field down Vec3 `vec3(0, -1, 0)`.
---@field north Vec3 `vec3(0, 0, -1)`.
---@field south Vec3 `vec3(0, 0, 1)`.
---@field east Vec3 `vec3(1, 0, 0)`.
---@field west Vec3 `vec3(-1, 0, 0)`.
---@overload fun(x: number, y: number, z: number): Vec3
vec3 = {}

--- The unit direction a yaw and pitch face, in Minecraft's convention: yaw 0 faces south (+z), 90 west (−x), 180 north, −90 east; pitch −90 is straight up, 90 straight down.
---@param yaw number Degrees.
---@param pitch number Degrees.
---@return Vec3
function vec3.from_yaw_pitch(yaw, pitch) end

--- A position in a particular world, with an optional facing. Immutable, like `Vec3`. What `location()` returns on a player or a centity; build one with `world:location(position, yaw?, pitch?)`. Methods that place things (`teleport`, `nf.centities.spawn`) take a `Location`, or a bare `Vec3` meaning "in the same world". `a == b` when every field is equal.
---@class Location
---@field world World Which world.
---@field position Vec3 Where in the world.
---@field yaw number? Which way it faces horizontally, in degrees (0 south, 90 west), or `nil` for a location with no facing.
---@field pitch number? How far up or down it faces, in degrees (−90 up, 90 down), or `nil` for a location with no facing.
local Location = {}

--- A copy somewhere else in the same world, keeping the facing.
---@param position Vec3
---@return Location
function Location:with_position(position) end

--- A copy moved by an offset, keeping the facing.
---@param offset Vec3
---@return Location
function Location:offset(offset) end

--- The unit direction it faces (`vec3.from_yaw_pitch`, a missing pitch counting as level), or `nil` when it has no facing.
---@return Vec3?
function Location:direction() end

--- What every event handler receives: a table with the event's own fields (its payload, named `<Something>Event` in this reference) and these. What a handler returns means nothing; it changes what happens through `stop`, `cancel` and the event's writable fields. A field the event doesn't list as writable can't be assigned.
---@class Event
---@field name string The event's name where the handler is listening: `"click"` on a node, `"centity_click"` on `nf`.
---@field current any The handle whose handler is running (the node, centity, slot, menu, button or dialog), or `nil` in an `nf.on` handler. As an event bubbles it changes; `target` stays what was hit.
---@field cancelled boolean Whether what caused it will be cancelled, as things stand. Read-only: use `cancel()` and `uncancel()`.
---@field stopped boolean Whether a handler called `stop()`. Read-only.
---@field from string? On a custom event (`nf.emit`, `centity:emit`), the namespace of the package whose code emitted it; `nil` on the server's own events. Any package may emit any custom event, so a handler that grants something checks `event.from` is a package it trusts (its own namespace, as its `netherforge.json` says). Read-only.
local Event = {}

--- No later handler hears it: none further along its path (a parent node, the centity, `nf`), and none after this one on the same handle.
function Event:stop() end

--- Cancels what caused it: the click doesn't move an item, the block isn't broken. Only for cancellable events; on any other, it's an error. A later handler can still `uncancel()`.
function Event:cancel() end

--- Undoes an earlier handler's `cancel()`, or a locked menu's.
function Event:uncancel() end

--- How `:on` listens. Every key is optional, and each applies only to the events that say so: a key that isn't one of these, or one the event doesn't take, is an error.
---@class EventOptions
---@field every integer? For `tick`: run every this many ticks instead of every tick (at least 1). `every = 20` is once a second. Which of those ticks is fixed by the server's tick count and the thing it's on (a centity's handlers all agree, and different centities fall on different ticks; each `nf.on` handler has its own), not by when the handler was added, so the first call can come sooner than `every` ticks.

--- One of the package's settings changed.
---@class SettingChangedEvent: Event
---@field setting string The setting's name, as `netherforge.json` declares it.
---@field value any What it is now, as `nf.config` gives it.
---@field previous any What it was.

--- Night is falling or day is breaking in a world.
---@class DaylightEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field time_of_day integer The world's time of day when it was seen, in ticks, as `world:time_of_day()` says: 13000 or a little past at dusk, 23000 or a little past at dawn, or wherever `/time set` put it.

--- A server tick went by.
---@class TickEvent: Event
---@field tick integer The server tick it is, the same as `nf.server.tick()`.

--- A player clicked a centity's hitbox. Heard by the clicked node, then each node above it, then the centity, then `nf.on("centity_click")`.
---@class ClickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field target Node The node whose hitbox was clicked. `event.current` is the node or centity whose handler is running.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which kind of click: the left (attack) or right (use) button.
---@field hit_position Vec3? Where the player's line of sight met the hitbox, as a world position: what `node:apply_impulse_at` takes. `nil` when the server couldn't tell.
---@field hit_normal Vec3? The unit direction straight out of the face that was hit, in world space. `nil` when the server couldn't tell.

--- Something happened to a centity instance as a whole.
---@class CentityEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The instance.

--- A centity is about to appear by itself, from its `spawning` rules, near a player.
---@class CentityNaturalSpawnEvent: Event
---@field centity string Which centity, as the project names it: its folder name under `centities/` (`wisp`), or `acme:wisp` for one a package brings.
---@field location Location Where it will stand, facing included (its yaw is the way the centity turns). Writable: assign another `Location` to put it somewhere else, in any world the server has; the spawner does not check its rules again there.
---@field player Player The player it is appearing near.

--- One of a centity's animations started or ended.
---@class AnimationEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The instance.
---@field animation string The animation's name.

--- Something happened to a node's physics body.
---@class NodeEvent: Event
---@field node Node The node.

--- A physics body started touching something it wasn't touching the tick before: the moment of impact, not every tick while it rests there.
---@class CollideEvent: Event
---@field node Node The body that hit something.
---@field other Node? The other body when it was one of the same centity's, or `nil` for a block or another centity's hitbox.
---@field hit_position Vec3 Where they touched, as a world position.
---@field hit_normal Vec3 The unit direction from what it hit towards the body, in world space.
---@field speed number How fast they were closing, in blocks per second: small for a touch, large for a crash. Handy for how loud a sound should be.

--- A player joined the server.
---@class PlayerJoinEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field first_join boolean Whether they have never played here before.
---@field message Text? The join message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.

--- A player left the server.
---@class PlayerQuitEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field message Text? The leave message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.

--- A player is saying something in chat.
---@class PlayerChatEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field message string What they said, as plain text: never MiniMessage, so it can be shown as it is. Writable: assign other text to change what everyone sees.
---@field format Text How the line is shown, as MiniMessage, where `<player>` is the player's display name and `<message>` what they said (both shown as they are, never read as tags). Starts as Minecraft's own look, `"\\<<player>> <message>"`. Writable: assign another, like `"<gray><player>: <white><message>"`.

--- A player clicked a block or the air with one hand. The game asks for each hand in turn, so one right click can arrive twice: once for the main hand and, unless that did something, once for the off hand.
---@class PlayerInteractEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which kind of click: the left (attack) or right (use) button.
---@field block Block? The block, or `nil` for a click on the air.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west"? Which face of the block was clicked, or `nil` for a click on the air.
---@field item Item? What's in the hand the click was with, or `nil` for an empty hand.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- A player is breaking a block: heard by its world, then `nf.on("block_break")`.
---@class BlockBreakEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block. A handle reads the world live, so what it says depends on when you ask: `state` is what it was when this happened.
---@field state string Its whole block state as it was being broken, like `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"`.
---@field drops Item[] What it will drop, for the tool they're breaking it with: an empty list for nothing. Writable: assign another list of items (or change this one) to drop those instead.
---@field experience integer The experience it will drop (an ore's, say). Writable: assign another to change it.

--- A player is placing a block: heard by its world, then `nf.on("block_place")`.
---@class BlockPlaceEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block. A handle reads the world live, so what it says depends on when you ask: `state` is what it was when this happened.
---@field state string The whole block state being placed, like `"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,shape=straight,waterlogged=false]"`.
---@field against Block The block it was placed against: the one they clicked.

--- Something a player did that needs no more said.
---@class PlayerEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.

--- A player pressed the swap-hands key.
---@class PlayerSwapHandsEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item? What is going to their off hand: what is in their main hand, or in the slot they point at with an inventory screen open.
---@field off_hand_item Item? What is leaving their off hand.
---@field in_inventory boolean Whether they pressed it over a slot with an inventory screen open, rather than with their hands.

--- A player walked, swam, flew or fell into another block.
---@class PlayerMoveEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from Location Where they were, facing included: in the block they are leaving.
---@field to Location Where they are going, facing included: in another block from `from`.

--- A player is being teleported.
---@class PlayerTeleportEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from Location Where they are, facing included.
---@field to Location Where they are going, facing included. Writable: assign another `Location` to send them there instead (a world the server doesn't have leaves it as it was).
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"ender_pearl"`, `"chorus_fruit"`, `"nether_portal"`, `"end_portal"`, `"end_gateway"`, `"spectate"`, `"dismount"`, `"exit_bed"`, `"unknown"`, …

--- A player went from one world to another.
---@class PlayerChangeWorldEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from World The world they left.
---@field to World The world they are in now.

--- A player died.
---@class PlayerDeathEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field killer Entity? Who killed them, when someone did.
---@field cause string What killed them, as Minecraft names it, the same as `EntityDamageEvent`'s `cause`: `"entity_attack"`, `"fall"`, `"lava"`, `"void"`, …
---@field message Text? The death message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.
---@field keep_inventory boolean Whether they keep what they carry. Writable: `true` keeps their inventory, and then nothing from it drops (unless `drops` is assigned too).
---@field drops Item[] What they drop where they died. Writable: assign another list of items (or change this one) to drop those instead.

--- A player is coming back after dying (or leaving the end).
---@class PlayerRespawnEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field location Location Where they will appear: their bed, respawn anchor or the world's spawn. Writable: assign another `Location` to bring them back there (a world the server doesn't have leaves it as it was).

--- A player did something with an item stack.
---@class PlayerItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item The item stack.

--- A player is picking up a dropped item.
---@class PlayerPickupItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item The stack they are picking up.
---@field entity DroppedItem The dropped item it lies in.

--- A player is using the item in one hand.
---@class PlayerUseItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item What's in that hand.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- A player started or stopped sneaking.
---@class PlayerSneakEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field sneaking boolean Whether they are sneaking now.

--- A player typed a command.
---@class PlayerCommandEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field input string What they typed, without the leading `/`: `"give @s minecraft:diamond 2"`.

--- Which centities `nf.centities.all` lists. Every key is optional; an instance is listed when it matches all of those given. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CentityFilter
---@field kind string? Only instances of this centity: its folder name.
---@field world World? Only instances in this world.
---@field near Vec3? Only instances whose position is within `radius` of this point (in any world, unless `world` says which). Needs `radius`.
---@field radius number? How far from `near`, in blocks. Needs `near`.

--- A projectile was launched.
---@class ProjectileLaunchEvent: Event
---@field projectile Entity The projectile. It isn't in the world yet, but its methods already work: a handler can tag it, give it data, or change its velocity.
---@field shooter Entity? Who launched it, or `nil` for a dispenser.

--- A projectile hit something.
---@class ProjectileHitEvent: Event
---@field projectile Entity The projectile.
---@field shooter Entity? Who launched it, when someone did.
---@field entity Entity? The entity it hit, or `nil` for a block.
---@field block Block? The block it hit, or `nil` for an entity.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west"|nil Which face of the block it hit, or `nil` for an entity.

--- A bow or crossbow was shot.
---@class EntityShootBowEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Who shot it.
---@field bow Item? The bow or crossbow, or `nil` for a mob without one.
---@field projectile Entity What it shot: an arrow, or a firework from a crossbow. Its methods already work.
---@field force number How far the bow was drawn, 0 to 1 (always 1 for a crossbow).
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand.

--- A mob is picking a target.
---@class EntityTargetEvent: Event
---@field entity Mob The mob.
---@field target Entity? What it's going after, or `nil` when it's forgetting its target.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"closest_player"`, `"target_attacked_entity"`, `"target_attacked_nearby_entity"`, `"forgot_target"`, `"target_died"`, `"temptation"`, `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), …

--- Something exploded: an entity (`entity_explode`) or a block (`block_explode`).
---@class ExplodeEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity? What exploded, for `entity_explode`; `nil` for a block.
---@field block Block? Where the block that exploded was, for `block_explode` (it has gone already); `nil` for an entity.
---@field state string? The whole block state of the block that exploded, for `block_explode`; `nil` for an entity.
---@field location Location Where it exploded.
---@field blocks Block[] The blocks it will break, as their handles: read them to see what would go.
---@field breaks_blocks boolean Whether `blocks` will break. Writable: `false` keeps every block standing, but the explosion still hurts and pushes what is near.
---@field yield number The chance each broken block drops as an item, 0 to 1. Writable: assign another (1 drops everything).

--- An entity is catching fire.
---@class EntityCombustEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity What is catching fire.
---@field ticks integer How long it will burn, in ticks. Writable: assign another.
---@field source Entity? The entity that set it alight (a flaming arrow, whoever swung a fire aspect sword), when one did.
---@field block Block? The block that set it alight (fire, lava), when one did.

--- One of an entity's status effects is changing.
---@class EntityChangeEffectEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Whose effect it is.
---@field effect string Which effect: `"minecraft:speed"`.
---@field action "added"|"changed"|"cleared"|"removed" `"added"`: it starts; `"changed"`: it gets stronger or longer; `"cleared"`: everything was taken away at once (milk, `/effect clear`); `"removed"`: this one was taken away or ran out.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"potion_drink"`, `"potion_splash"`, `"beacon"`, `"milk"`, `"expiration"`, `"command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"food"`, `"attack"`, …
---@field from StatusEffect? The effect as it was, or `nil` when it is new.
---@field to StatusEffect? The effect as it will be, or `nil` when it goes.

--- Something happened to a block.
---@class BlockEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.

--- A block is catching fire.
---@class BlockIgniteEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"flint_and_steel"`, `"fireball"`, `"lava"`, `"lightning"`, `"spread"`, `"explosion"`, `"arrow"`, `"ender_crystal"`, …
---@field player Player? The player who set it alight, when one did.
---@field entity Entity? The entity that set it alight, when one did (a player too).
---@field source Block? The block it caught from (fire, lava), when one did.

--- A block is burning away.
---@class BlockBurnEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.
---@field source Block? The fire it's burning in, when the server knows.

--- A block is changing by itself.
---@class BlockChangeEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block that is changing.
---@field state string The whole block state it is changing to: `"minecraft:wheat[age=7]"`.
---@field source Block? For `block_spread`: the block it spreads from.
---@field entity Entity? For `block_form`: the entity that formed it (a player's frost walker boots, a snow golem), when one did.

--- A liquid is flowing into another block.
---@class BlockFlowEvent: Event
---@field block Block Where it flows from.
---@field to Block Where it flows to.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which way, from `block` to `to`.

--- A piston is moving.
---@class PistonEvent: Event
---@field block Block The piston.
---@field direction "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which way the piston faces: blocks move this way as it extends, and back towards it as it retracts.
---@field sticky boolean Whether it is a sticky piston.
---@field blocks Block[] The blocks it will move, where they are now.

--- A block's redstone power is changing.
---@class BlockRedstoneEvent: Event
---@field block Block The block.
---@field from integer The power it had, 0 to 15.
---@field to integer The power it is changing to, 0 to 15. Writable: assign another.

--- A player finished writing on a sign.
---@class SignChangeEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block.
---@field side "front"|"back" Which side they wrote on.
---@field lines string[] What they wrote, line by line, as plain text (four lines, empty ones included).

--- A player started hitting a block.
---@class BlockStartBreakEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The block.
---@field item Item? What they're hitting it with, or `nil` for a bare hand.
---@field instant boolean Whether it will break at once (in creative, or a block that breaks in one hit). Writable: `true` breaks it now.

--- A broken block is dropping its items.
---@class BlockDropItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block Where it was: it has gone already.
---@field state string Its whole block state, as it was.
---@field items Item[] What it drops, its contents included.

--- It is starting or stopping raining in a world.
---@class WeatherChangeEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field raining boolean Whether it will be raining (or snowing).
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"natural"`, `"command"`, `"sleep"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"unknown"`.

--- A thunderstorm is starting or stopping in a world.
---@class ThunderChangeEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field thundering boolean Whether there will be a thunderstorm.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"natural"`, `"command"`, `"sleep"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"unknown"`.

--- Lightning is striking.
---@class LightningStrikeEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field location Location Where it strikes.
---@field entity Entity The lightning bolt.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"weather"`, `"trident"`, `"command"`, `"spawner"` (a skeleton horse trap), `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), `"unknown"`.

--- A chunk: one just generated, or one leaving memory.
---@class ChunkEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field x integer Its x, in chunks: a block x divided by 16, rounded down.
---@field z integer Its z, in chunks.

--- A chunk came into memory.
---@class ChunkLoadEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.
---@field x integer Its x, in chunks: a block x divided by 16, rounded down.
---@field z integer Its z, in chunks.
---@field generated boolean Whether it was just generated, for the first time.

--- A world was loaded or is being unloaded.
---@class WorldEvent: Event
---@field world World The world.

--- An entity is getting on or off something it rides.
---@class MountEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity The rider.
---@field vehicle Entity What it rides.

--- A vehicle moved into another block.
---@class VehicleMoveEvent: Event
---@field vehicle Entity The boat or minecart.
---@field from Location Where it was: in the block it is leaving.
---@field to Location Where it is now: in another block from `from`.

--- Something is hitting a vehicle.
---@class VehicleDamageEvent: Event
---@field vehicle Entity The boat or minecart.
---@field attacker Entity? Who hit it, when someone did.
---@field amount number How much damage it takes. Writable: assign another to change it.

--- A vehicle is being broken.
---@class VehicleDestroyEvent: Event
---@field vehicle Entity The boat or minecart.
---@field attacker Entity? Who broke it, when someone did.

--- A player opened or closed a vanilla inventory.
---@class PlayerInventoryEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field kind string What kind of inventory it is: `"chest"`, `"barrel"`, `"shulker_box"`, `"ender_chest"`, `"furnace"`, `"workbench"` (a crafting table), `"anvil"`, `"enchanting"`, `"merchant"` (a villager), …
---@field inventory Inventory? The inventory, when it's a real one (a container block's, an entity's, their ender chest); `nil` for one with nothing kept in it (a crafting table, an anvil).
---@field block Block? The block it belongs to (a chest, a crafting table), or `nil` for an entity's.

--- A player clicked in an inventory that isn't one of the project's menus.
---@class PlayerClickInventoryEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field kind string What kind of inventory they have open: `"crafting"` when it is only their own (opened with the inventory key), else as `PlayerInventoryEvent.kind` says (`"chest"`, `"furnace"`, …).
---@field inventory Inventory? The inventory clicked, when it's a real one: their own (`player:inventory()`), a container block's, an entity's, their ender chest. `nil` for a click outside both, and in one with nothing kept in it (the crafting grid, an anvil).
---@field own boolean Whether the click landed in their own inventory (hotbar, main slots, armour, off hand) rather than in the one above it or outside both.
---@field index integer? The slot clicked, from 0, in the inventory clicked. In their own: 0 to 8 the hotbar, 9 to 35 the rest, 36 to 39 armour (boots first), 40 the off hand, as `player:inventory()` numbers them. `nil` for a click outside both.
---@field click "left"|"right"|"shift_left"|"shift_right"|"middle"|"number_key"|"swap_offhand"|"double"|"drop"|"control_drop"|"other" What kind of click it was. `swap_offhand` is the swap-hands key (F) over a slot: what is in it and what is in the off hand change places.
---@field hotbar_index integer? For `number_key`: the hotbar slot, 0 to 8.
---@field item Item? What was in the slot clicked.
---@field cursor_item Item? What the player was carrying on the cursor.

--- A player dragged a stack in an inventory that isn't one of the project's menus.
---@class PlayerDragInventoryEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field kind string What kind of inventory they have open, as `PlayerClickInventoryEvent.kind` says: `"crafting"` when it is only their own.
---@field inventory Inventory? The inventory above their own, when it's a real one (a container block's, an entity's, their ender chest); `nil` for one with nothing kept in it (the crafting grid, an anvil) and when only their own is open.
---@field indices integer[] The slots of the inventory above their own the stack is spread over, from 0, in order (the crafting grid's 1 to 4 when only their own is open, 0 being its result).
---@field own_indices integer[] The slots of their own inventory the stack is spread over, in order, numbered as `player:inventory()` numbers them.
---@field click "left"|"right" `"left"` spreads the stack evenly over the slots, `"right"` puts one in each.
---@field cursor_item Item? The stack being dragged.

--- A player is taking what they crafted.
---@class PlayerCraftEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field recipe string? Its recipe's id, like `"minecraft:oak_planks"`, or `nil` when no recipe matches.
---@field item Item What one craft makes.
---@field shift boolean Whether they shift-clicked, to make as many as they can.

--- What's in a crafting grid changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareCraftEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field recipe string? Its recipe's id, like `"minecraft:oak_planks"`, or `nil` when no recipe matches.
---@field ingredients table<integer, Item> What's in the grid, by slot: 0 to 8 for a crafting table, row by row from the top left, or 0 to 3 for the 2×2. Empty slots are left out.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.
---@field repair boolean Whether it is two damaged tools of a kind being combined into one.

--- What is in an anvil changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareAnvilEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field left Item? What is in the first slot.
---@field right Item? What is in the second slot.
---@field rename string? The name typed in, or `nil` for none.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.
---@field cost integer How many experience levels it takes. Writable: assign another.

--- What is in a smithing table changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareSmithingEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field template Item? The smithing template.
---@field base Item? What is being upgraded or trimmed.
---@field addition Item? The ingot or material.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.

--- What is in a grindstone changed.
---@class PlayerPrepareGrindstoneEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field top Item? What is in the top slot.
---@field bottom Item? What is in the bottom slot.
---@field result Item? What the result slot shows, or `nil` for nothing. Writable: assign another item to make that the result, or `nil` for none.

--- A player is enchanting an item.
---@class PlayerEnchantItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field block Block The enchanting table.
---@field item Item What is being enchanted.
---@field enchantments table<string, integer> What it gets, by id with its level: `{ ["minecraft:sharpness"] = 3 }`.
---@field cost integer How many experience levels it takes. Writable: assign another.

--- A furnace finished smelting an item.
---@class FurnaceSmeltEvent: Event
---@field block Block The furnace, smoker or blast furnace.
---@field source Item What was smelted (one of it).
---@field result Item What it makes. Writable: assign another item to make that instead.

--- A server list is asking about the server.
---@class ServerListPingEvent: Event
---@field address string The address it asks from, like `"203.0.113.7"`.
---@field description Text The server's description under its name (its MOTD), as MiniMessage: two lines at most. Writable: assign another.
---@field online integer How many players it says are on. Writable: assign another number.
---@field max_players integer How many it says fit. Writable: assign another number.
---@field hide_players boolean Whether it hides the player counts (showing `???`) and who is on. Writable.

--- What movement keys a player holds down now.
---@class PlayerInputEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field forward boolean Forward (W).
---@field backward boolean Back (S).
---@field left boolean Left (A).
---@field right boolean Right (D).
---@field jump boolean Jump (space).
---@field sneak boolean Sneak (shift).
---@field sprint boolean Sprint (control).

--- A player picked another hotbar slot.
---@class PlayerChangeSlotEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from integer The slot they had, 0 to 8.
---@field to integer The slot they picked, 0 to 8.

--- A player swung an arm.
---@class PlayerSwingEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand.

--- A player started or stopped sprinting.
---@class PlayerSprintEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field sprinting boolean Whether they are sprinting now.

--- A player started or stopped flying.
---@class PlayerFlyEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field flying boolean Whether they are flying now.

--- A player jumped.
---@class PlayerJumpEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from Location Where they jumped from, facing included.
---@field to Location Where the jump has taken them so far.

--- A player's game mode is changing.
---@class PlayerChangeGameModeEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field game_mode "survival"|"creative"|"adventure"|"spectator" The game mode they are changing to.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"default_game_mode"`, `"hardcore_death"`, `"unknown"`, …

--- A player's food level is changing.
---@class PlayerChangeFoodEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field food integer The level it's changing to, 0 to 20. Writable: assign another to set that instead.
---@field item Item? What they ate, or `nil` when they are getting hungry.

--- An entity is getting health back: heard by the entity, then `nf.on("entity_heal")`.
---@class EntityHealEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Who is healing.
---@field amount number How much: 2 is one heart. Writable: assign another to change it.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"satiated"` (a full food bar), `"regen"`, `"eating"`, `"magic"`, `"magic_regen"`, `"ender_crystal"`, `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), …

--- A player is getting experience points.
---@class PlayerGainExperienceEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field amount integer How many points. Writable: assign another to give that many instead.

--- A player's experience level changed.
---@class PlayerChangeLevelEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field from integer The level they had.
---@field to integer The level they have now.

--- A player is being kicked.
---@class PlayerKickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field text Text What their screen will say, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign other text.
---@field message Text? The leave message everyone sees, as MiniMessage. Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.
---@field cause string Why, as Minecraft names it: `"kick_command"`, `"plugin"` (a plugin or a script), `"flying_player"`, `"timeout"`, `"whitelist"`, `"ban"`, `"idling"`, `"unknown"`, …

--- A player completed an advancement.
---@class PlayerAdvancementEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field advancement string Its key, always with its namespace: the game's `"minecraft:story/mine_diamond"`, or one of the project's own as `"<namespace>:<id>"` (`"showcase:treasure_hunter"`).
---@field message Text? The message everyone sees, as MiniMessage, or `nil` when there's none (a recipe, or an advancement that doesn't announce itself). Writable: assign another, or `nil` for none.

--- A player let go of an item before it finished.
---@class PlayerStopUsingItemEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was.
---@field item Item What they were using.
---@field ticks integer How long they held it, in ticks.

--- How a centity walks where `centity:move_to` sends it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CentityPathOptions
---@field speed number? How fast, in blocks a second: more than 0, at most 20. Default 4.3, a player's walk.
---@field fly boolean? Flies there, through the air in any direction, rather than walking on the ground: no steps, drops or falling. Finding a way costs the server more. Default false.
---@field face boolean? Turns it to face the way it goes, about its feet, a little each tick. Default true.
---@field width number? How wide a gap it needs, in blocks: more than 0, at most 8. Default: its hitboxes' wider side, as they're placed when it sets off (1 without hitboxes). It's taken as square, whichever way it faces.
---@field height number? How tall a gap it needs, in blocks: more than 0, at most 16. Default: its hitboxes' height (1 without hitboxes).
---@field step_height number? Walking: how high a step it goes straight up, in blocks: at least 0. Default 1, a block, as a mob jumps one.
---@field max_drop number? Walking: how far down it will drop off an edge, in blocks: at least 0. Default 3.
---@field open_doors boolean? Walking: goes through the game's wooden doors and fence gates (`#minecraft:wooden_doors`, `#minecraft:fence_gates`), opening each as it comes to it and shutting it again once it's through (one it found open stays open). Iron doors stay in its way. Default `false`.
---@field range number? How far from where it sets off it looks for a way, in blocks: more than 0, at most 128. A target further away than this is `false`. Default 48. One search looks at no more than 4096 places, so a long way round may only take it part of the way.

--- A centity stopped walking where `move_to` sent it.
---@class CentityPathEndEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The instance.
---@field reached boolean Whether it got there: to the end of its path, within 2 blocks of where it was sent (or of what it went after, which it stops short of); `false` when it gave up.

--- How `nf.centities.spawn` spawns one. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CentitySpawnOptions
---@field temporary boolean? Not saved: its entities aren't kept with their chunk, the server forgets it on a restart, and it's removed when the script that spawned it unloads (a reload of it, or its centity removed), so nothing it made is left behind. What a shot, a damage number or a summoned helper is. `keep()` makes it a normal one. Default `false`.
---@field lifetime integer? Removed this many ticks after it's spawned (its `remove` handlers hear it), loaded or not: at least 1. A centity with a lifetime is temporary too. Default: none.

--- A centity's body, as `centity:body()` gives it and `centity:set_body` takes it: the fields of `centity.json`'s `body`, in Lua spelling. Every field is optional to `set_body`. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BodySettings
---@field width number? Blocks across, square: more than 0, at most 16.
---@field height number? Blocks tall: more than 0, at most 32.
---@field gravity number? Downward acceleration, blocks per second squared.
---@field max_fall_speed number? The fastest it falls, blocks per second.
---@field drag number? Fraction of its speed shed per second in the air, 0 to 1.
---@field liquid_drag number? The same in water or lava.
---@field liquid_gravity number? How much of its gravity pulls it in water or lava: 0 floats, 1 sinks as in air.
---@field acceleration number? How fast it reaches its walk on the ground (or flying), blocks per second squared.
---@field air_acceleration number? The same in the air.
---@field friction number? How fast it slows to a stop on the ground when it isn't walking, blocks per second squared.
---@field step_height number? The tallest step it walks straight up, blocks.
---@field bounce number? 0 stops dead against what it hits, 1 bounces off at full speed.
---@field fly boolean? No gravity: walking steers it in any direction.
---@field blocks boolean? Collides with the world's blocks.

--- How `centity:launch` flies it. Everything but `velocity` is optional. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class LaunchOptions
---@field velocity Vec3 Blocks per second, along the world's axes. Required.
---@field gravity number? Downward acceleration, blocks per second squared. Default 0: it flies straight.
---@field gravity_delay integer? Ticks it flies straight before gravity takes it, as a thrown knife's first stretch. Default 0.
---@field drag number? Fraction of its speed shed per second, 0 to 1. Default 0.
---@field radius number? How wide its path is swept for hits, as a sphere's radius in blocks; blocks stop its middle. Default 0.1.
---@field pierce integer? How many hits it goes on through before one ends it. Default 0: the first ends it.
---@field rehit integer? Ticks before it can hit the same thing again. Default: it hits each thing once.
---@field hits QueryOptions? What it can hit, as a spatial query's options say (`centities`, `players`, `entities`, `kinds`, `exclude`, `line_of_sight` aside). It never hits itself or its `owner`. Default: everything.
---@field owner (Entity|Centity)? Who launched it: never hit, and handed to every event. Default none.
---@field blocks boolean? Whether blocks stop it (their collision shapes). Default `true`.
---@field bounce number? Comes off a block instead of ending: the fraction of its speed into the face it keeps, 0 to 1. Default: it doesn't bounce.
---@field friction number? With `bounce`: the fraction of its speed along the face it keeps, 0 to 1. Default 1.
---@field max_bounces integer? With `bounce`: how many it makes before the next block ends it. Default: no end.
---@field rest_speed number? With `bounce`: below this speed, blocks per second, after a bounce off a floor it stops and lies there (a grenade) until its `lifetime` is up. Default 1.
---@field stick boolean? Sticks in the first block it hits (`stick`) instead of ending: it stays there until its `lifetime` is up. Default `false`.
---@field homing Homing? Turns toward the nearest thing it can hit within reach. Default: it doesn't.
---@field lifetime integer? Ticks from launch before it ends (`flight_end`, `"lifetime"`), flying, stuck or lying still. At least 1. Default 600: half a minute.
---@field aim string? A node that's turned every tick so its +Z points the way it flies (an arrow's nose). Default: none is turned.
---@field remove_on_end boolean? Whether it's removed when its flight ends, after `flight_end`. Default `true`.

--- How a projectile seeks: `LaunchOptions.homing`.
---@class Homing
---@field range number How far it looks, in blocks. Required.
---@field turn number? The most it turns toward its target, in degrees per second. Default 180.

--- A projectile touched something it can hit: `hit`. Exactly one of `target` and `entity` is set.
---@class FlightHitEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The projectile.
---@field target Centity? The centity it hit, or `nil`.
---@field node Node? Which of `target`'s nodes (its hitbox), or `nil`.
---@field entity Entity? The player or entity it hit, or `nil`.
---@field position Vec3 Where its middle was when it first touched.
---@field velocity Vec3 How fast it was going, blocks per second.
---@field owner (Entity|Centity)? Who launched it, or `nil`.

--- A projectile met a block it bounces off (`bounce`) or sticks in (`stick`).
---@class FlightBlockEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The projectile.
---@field block Block The block.
---@field position Vec3 Where it met it.
---@field normal Vec3 The direction straight out of the face it met.
---@field speed number How fast it was going into it, blocks per second.
---@field owner (Entity|Centity)? Who launched it, or `nil`.

--- A projectile's flight ended: `flight_end`.
---@class FlightEndEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The projectile.
---@field reason "hit"|"block"|"lifetime"|"unloaded"|"stopped" Why: see `flight_end`.
---@field position Vec3 Where it ended.
---@field target Centity? For `"hit"`, the centity it ended on, or `nil`.
---@field entity Entity? For `"hit"`, the player or entity it ended on, or `nil`.
---@field block Block? For `"block"`, the block it ended on, or `nil`.
---@field normal Vec3? For `"block"`, the direction straight out of the face it met, or `nil`.
---@field owner (Entity|Centity)? Who launched it, or `nil`.

--- Where damage dealt with `centity:damage` comes from, for its handlers. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CentityDamageOptions
---@field source (Entity|Centity)? Who or what dealt it.
---@field cause string? What kind of damage, in your own words: `"melee"`, `"fire"`.

--- A centity is about to lose health: `damage`.
---@class CentityDamageEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The centity.
---@field amount number How much it loses: assign to change it.
---@field source (Entity|Centity)? Who or what dealt it, or `nil`.
---@field cause string? The damage's `cause`, or `nil`.

--- A centity's health reached 0: `death`.
---@class CentityDeathEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The centity.
---@field source (Entity|Centity)? What dealt the last blow, or `nil`.
---@field cause string? The last blow's `cause`, or `nil`.

--- A centity's body landed: `land`.
---@class CentityLandEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The centity.
---@field speed number How fast it was falling as it landed, blocks per second.

--- A centity's body ran into something: `bump`.
---@class CentityBumpEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The centity.
---@field normal Vec3 The direction straight out of the face it hit: `vec3(0, -1, 0)` for a ceiling.
---@field speed number How fast it was going into it, blocks per second.

--- A box along the world's axes: `centity:bounds()`.
---@class Bounds
---@field min Vec3 Its lowest corner.
---@field max Vec3 Its highest corner.

--- A centity's hitboxes started touching something's: `touch`.
---@class TouchEvent: Event
---@field centity Centity The centity that hears it.
---@field node Node Which of its nodes touched (its hitbox).
---@field other Centity? The other centity, or `nil`.
---@field other_node Node? Which of the other centity's nodes it touched, or `nil`.
---@field entity Entity? The player or entity it touched, or `nil`.

--- How `node:stretch_between` lays a node. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class StretchOptions
---@field thickness number? Its X and Y scale. Default: as they are.

--- How `node:rope_between` strings a rope. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class RopeOptions
---@field length number? How long the rope is, in blocks: shorter than the ends' distance it's taut, longer it sags by its slack. Default: always taut.

--- A tween for `node:tween`: at least one of `translation`, `rotation` and `scale`. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TweenOptions
---@field translation Vec3? Where it ends up, as `set_translation` takes.
---@field rotation Vec3? Degrees, as `set_rotation` takes; not the short way round, so 0 to 360 is a whole turn.
---@field scale Vec3? As `set_scale` takes.
---@field ticks integer How long, 1 or more (each way, with `back`).
---@field easing string? `linear`, `ease_in`, `ease_out`, `ease_in_out` or `step`, as keyframes take. Default `ease_in_out`.
---@field back boolean? Eases back to where it started after, over as many ticks again. Default `false`.

--- How `centity:play_animation` plays an animation this time. The file says how it plays by default.
---@class AnimationOptions
---@field speed number? 1 is as authored (the default), 2 twice as fast, 0.5 half. Not negative.
---@field from_tick number? Where in the clip to start, in ticks from its beginning. Default 0.
---@field loop boolean? `true` loops it whatever its file says; `false` plays it through once (a `hold` clip still holds its last pose). Default: the file's `loop`.
---@field blend_ticks integer? Eases from the pose the nodes have now into the animation over this many ticks, instead of jumping to its first frame. Default 0.

--- How a text display draws its text: what `node:display_text_style()` gives and `node:set_display_text_style` takes. Every field is optional; one left out is its default.
---@class TextStyle
---@field background string? `#AARRGGBB`, alpha first: `"#00000000"` is none at all. Default: Minecraft's translucent grey.
---@field opacity integer? The text's own opacity, 0 to 255. Default 255.
---@field shadow boolean? A drop shadow under the text. Default false.
---@field alignment "center"|"left"|"right"? How lines of different lengths line up. Default `"center"`.
---@field line_width integer? How wide a line gets, in pixels, before it wraps. Default 200.
---@field see_through boolean? Drawn through blocks in front of it. Default false.

--- Fixed light levels a display is drawn with: `node:set_brightness`.
---@class Brightness
---@field block_light integer? Light from blocks (torches, lamps), 0 to 15. Required.
---@field sky_light integer? Light from the sky, 0 to 15. Required.

--- A buff someone has now: `buffs()`.
---@class ActiveBuff
---@field id string Its id.
---@field ticks integer Ticks it has left.
---@field name Text? Its file's `name`, or `nil`.
---@field icon string? Its file's `icon`, a glyph (`<pack>/<key>`), or `nil`.
---@field color string? Its file's `color`, or `nil`.
---@field debuff boolean Whether it is one.

--- A buff is about to take hold: `buff_add`. Exactly one of `player` and `centity` is set.
---@class BuffEvent: Event
---@field buff string Its id.
---@field ticks integer How long it will last: assign to change it (at least 1).
---@field player Player? Who gets it, or `nil`.
---@field centity Centity? The centity that gets it, or `nil`.

--- A buff ended: `buff_end`. Exactly one of `player` and `centity` is set.
---@class BuffEndEvent: Event
---@field buff string Its id.
---@field reason "expired"|"removed"|"cured" Why: see `buff_end`.
---@field player Player? Who had it, or `nil`.
---@field centity Centity? The centity that had it, or `nil`.

--- One making of a `station` recipe by `nf.recipes.craft`: `recipe_craft`.
---@class RecipeCraftEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who it was made for.
---@field recipe string The recipe's id.
---@field result Item What this making gives them: the recipe's result, until a handler assigns another.

--- A `station` recipe, as `nf.recipes.available` and `nf.recipes.get` give it.
---@class StationRecipe
---@field id string Its id.
---@field result Item What one making of it gives.
---@field materials RecipeMaterial[] What one making of it takes.
---@field stations string[] The blocks that must be near, as its file names them.
---@field station_range integer How near, in blocks.
---@field group string? Its `group`, or `nil`.
---@field times integer? `nf.recipes.available`: how many times over the player can make it now. `nil` from `nf.recipes.get`.

--- One material of a `station` recipe: exactly one of `item`, `kind` and `tag`, and how many.
---@class RecipeMaterial
---@field item string? A project item, by id.
---@field kind string? An item id, `"minecraft:stick"`.
---@field tag string? An item tag, without its `#`.
---@field count integer How many one making takes.

--- What `nf.recipes.available` looks at. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class RecipeAvailableOptions
---@field near Vec3? Where its stations are looked for, in the player's world. Default: where they stand.
---@field stations string[]? Stations counted as near whatever the blocks are: a menu that is a crafting station, a station item held. Default none.
---@field group string? Only recipes of this `group`. Default: any.
---@field all boolean? Every station recipe, not only those they can make now: one they can't has `times` 0. Default `false`.

--- How `nf.recipes.craft` makes a recipe. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class RecipeCraftOptions
---@field times integer? How many times over, at least 1. Default 1.
---@field near Vec3? Where its stations are looked for. Default: where the player stands.
---@field stations string[]? Stations counted as near whatever the blocks are.

--- A stack is being sent to one player's game, to be shown as this player alone sees it: `view` on a project item, `item_view` on `nf`.
---@class ItemViewEvent: Event
---@field player Player Whose game it is sent to.
---@field item Item The real stack, as the server keeps it. Changing this table changes nothing: assign `display_name` and `lore`.
---@field display_name Text? The name this player is shown: the stack's own until a handler assigns another. `nil` for the game's own name.
---@field lore Text[]? The lore lines this player is shown, the same way. `nil` for none.

--- A stack of a project item is being made, or rewritten to its item's changed file: `render`.
---@class ItemRenderEvent: Event
---@field item Item The stack as it is about to be, with its `data` and `count`. Changing this table changes nothing: assign `display_name` and `lore`.
---@field display_name Text? Its name: the file's (or what made it said), until a handler assigns another. `nil` for the game's own name.
---@field lore Text[]? Its lore lines, the same way. `nil` for none.

--- The parts of a skin's outer layer a mannequin shows, each `true` when shown. `mannequin:skin_parts()` gives every one; `set_skin_parts` changes those given.
---@class SkinParts
---@field cape boolean? Its cape, when its skin has one.
---@field jacket boolean?
---@field left_sleeve boolean?
---@field right_sleeve boolean?
---@field left_pants boolean?
---@field right_pants boolean?
---@field hat boolean?

--- Which entities `world:entities` lists. Every key is optional; an entity is listed when it matches all of those given. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class EntityFilter
---@field kind string? Only this kind: `"minecraft:zombie"`.
---@field tag string? Only entities with this scoreboard tag.
---@field near Vec3? Only entities within `radius` of this point. Needs `radius`.
---@field radius number? How far from `near`, in blocks. Needs `near`.
---@field living boolean? `true`: only living entities (mobs, animals, players); `false`: only the rest.

--- How `world:spawn_entity` sets up what it spawns, before anything (an `entity_spawn` handler included) sees it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class EntitySpawnOptions
---@field custom_name Text? Its custom name, MiniMessage.
---@field tags string[]? Scoreboard tags to give it.
---@field data any A table its `data()` table starts with: the same values a `data()` table may hold. Something that can't be saved is an error.
---@field velocity Vec3? How fast it starts moving, in blocks per tick.

--- How a status effect is given. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class EffectOptions
---@field amplifier integer? Its strength above the first: 0 is speed I, 1 speed II. Default 0.
---@field ambient boolean? Fainter particles, as from a beacon. Default `false`.
---@field particles boolean? Whether it shows particles. Default `true`.
---@field icon boolean? Whether it shows an icon on their screen. Default `true`.

--- One status effect on a living entity: what `living:effects()` lists.
---@class StatusEffect
---@field effect string Its id: `"minecraft:speed"`.
---@field ticks integer How long it has left; -1 for ever.
---@field amplifier integer Its strength above the first: 0 is level I.
---@field ambient boolean Whether its particles are faint, as from a beacon.
---@field particles boolean Whether it shows particles.
---@field icon boolean Whether it shows an icon.

--- How a mob walks where `move_to` sends it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class PathOptions
---@field speed number? How fast, times its own walking speed (its `movement_speed` attribute): 1 walks, 1.5 hurries. More than 0. Default 1.

--- One of a mob's goals: what `mob:goals` lists.
---@class MobGoal
---@field key string Its key: `"minecraft:random_stroll"` for one of the game's, `"<namespace>:guard_post"` for one the project added, or another plugin's.
---@field priority integer Its priority, the game's goals' too: smaller is more important. A goal takes its controls from running goals with a bigger number.
---@field controls ("move"|"look"|"jump"|"target")[] What it claims while it runs, so no less important goal claiming the same runs with it: `"move"`, `"look"`, `"jump"`, `"target"` (a targeting goal). Empty for a goal that claims none of these.
---@field running boolean Whether it runs now.

--- A goal written in Lua, for `mob:add_goal`. Each callback gets the mob. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class GoalDefinition
---@field priority integer How important it is: smaller goes first. A goal takes its controls from running goals with a bigger number, and waits for those with a smaller one or the same. The game's goals use about 0 (swimming up in water) to 8 (looking about).
---@field controls ("move"|"look"|"jump"|"target")[]? What it claims while it runs, so no less important goal claiming the same runs with it: `"move"` (it walks the mob), `"look"`, `"jump"`, or `"target"` alone (it picks what the mob fights). Default none: it runs alongside the goals that claim something, and one at a time with the others that claim nothing.
---@field should_start (fun(mob: Mob): boolean)? Whether to start now, asked every tick or two while it isn't running. Default: always.
---@field should_continue (fun(mob: Mob): boolean)? Whether to keep running, asked every tick or two while it runs. Default: what `should_start` says.
---@field start (fun(mob: Mob))? It starts running.
---@field tick (fun(mob: Mob))? While it runs: every tick or two, as the game updates its goals.
---@field stop (fun(mob: Mob))? It stopped: `should_continue` said no, a more important goal took one of its controls, or it was removed while running.

--- Which goals `mob:clear_goals` leaves. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class GoalClearOptions
---@field keep string[]? The keys of goals to keep, as `remove_goal` takes them: `{ "float" }` keeps `"minecraft:float"`, so a mob still swims.

--- One modifier on an entity's attribute: what `living:attribute_modifiers` lists.
---@class AttributeModifier
---@field id string Its full id: `"<namespace>:slow_zone"` for one a script added, or the game's own (`"minecraft:sprinting"`), an item's, another plugin's.
---@field amount number How much.
---@field operation "add_value"|"add_multiplied_base"|"add_multiplied_total" How `amount` applies: added; added times the base value; or the total multiplied by `1 + amount`.

--- How long a title takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TitleOptions
---@field fade_in integer? Ticks it takes to appear. Default 10.
---@field stay integer? Ticks it stays. Default 70.
---@field fade_out integer? Ticks it takes to go. Default 20.

--- Something is hurting an entity: heard by the entity, then `nf.on("entity_damage")`.
---@class EntityDamageEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity Who is hurt.
---@field amount number How much, before armour: 2 is one heart. Writable: assign another to change it.
---@field cause string What hurt it, as Minecraft names it: `"entity_attack"`, `"projectile"`, `"fall"`, `"fire"`, `"lava"`, `"drowning"`, `"void"`, …
---@field attacker Entity? Who did it, when someone did: for an arrow, whoever shot it.

--- A living entity died: heard by it, then `nf.on("entity_death")`.
---@class EntityDeathEvent: Event
---@field entity Living Who died. Its handle answers `nil` from now on.
---@field killer Entity? Who killed it, when someone did.
---@field drops Item[] What it drops. Writable: assign another list of items (or change this one) to drop those instead.
---@field experience integer The experience it drops. Writable: assign another to change it.

--- A mob stopped walking where `move_to` sent it.
---@class EntityPathEndEvent: Event
---@field entity Mob The mob.
---@field reached boolean Whether it got there, within 2 blocks; `false` when it gave up.

--- A player right-clicked an entity: heard by the entity, then the player, then `nf.on("player_interact_entity")`.
---@class PlayerInteractEntityEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field entity Entity What they clicked.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand the click was with: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- An entity is coming into a world: heard by the world, then `nf.on("entity_spawn")`.
---@class EntitySpawnEvent: Event
---@field entity Entity What's coming. Its methods already work, so a handler can name it or tag it.
---@field world World The world it's coming into.
---@field cause string What caused it, as Minecraft names it: `"natural"`, `"spawner"`, `"breeding"`, `"command"`, `"custom"` (a plugin or a script), …

--- How `player:ban` bans. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BanOptions
---@field reason string? What they're told when they're kicked and when they try to join, as plain text. Default the server's own message.
---@field expires integer? When it runs out, as Unix time in milliseconds (like `nf.server.unix_time()`): a time already past is an error. Default never.
---@field source string? Who banned them, as the ban list shows it. Default `"NetherForge"`.

--- How far a player is with an advancement, by its criteria's names as the advancement's own file calls them.
---@class AdvancementProgress
---@field done string[] The criteria they've met.
---@field remaining string[] The criteria left.

--- How a bot joins (`nf.bots.join`).
---@class BotJoinOptions
---@field skin Skin? Its skin. Without one, it wears one of the game's default skins.

--- How a bot walks (`bot:walk_to`).
---@class BotWalkOptions
---@field sprint boolean? Sprint there. Default `false`.
---@field timeout integer? Ticks it may take before it gives up, 1 to 500. Default 200.

--- How a bot right-clicks (`bot:interact`).
---@class BotInteractOptions
---@field off_hand boolean? With what it holds in its off hand. Default `false`.
---@field at Vec3? A point on it to turn to and click (a square of a board), in the world. Its middle when left out.

--- A skin, as Mojang's session servers sign it: a player profile's `textures` property. Sites that make skins for NPCs (MineSkin, …) give both values.
---@class Skin
---@field texture string The base64 `value` of the `textures` property.
---@field signature string? Its signature. Players' clients draw an unsigned skin only when the server is in offline mode.

--- What a menu slot shows: `slot:binding()`. A slot of a storage (`storage` and `index`), or what a player wears (`player` and `equipment`).
---@class SlotBinding
---@field storage Inventory? The storage (`player:storage`).
---@field index integer? The storage's slot, from 0.
---@field player Player? Whose equipment.
---@field equipment string? `head`, `chest`, `legs`, `feet` or `off_hand`.

--- What a menu slot takes (`slot:set_rule`). A stack goes in when it's any of `items`, `kinds`, `property` or `wearable` (or when none is given), and never more of it than `max`. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class SlotRule
---@field items string[]? The project's items it takes, by id.
---@field kinds string[]? The game's items it takes, by id or `#tag` (`#minecraft:head_armor`); a project item's stacks never count as their kind.
---@field property string? A project item with this key in its `properties` (and not `false`).
---@field wearable string? What's worn there: `head`, `chest`, `legs`, `feet` or `body`, by its `equipment` slot or, for the game's own items, the slot's armour tag (`#minecraft:head_armor`...).
---@field max integer? The most items it holds: 1 for a ring. Default a full stack.

--- An item stack as a plain table: what `menu:item` returns and what `menu:set_item` takes. Only `kind` is required, or `item` instead. Read one, change a field and write it back, and nothing else about it changes: whatever the stack carries that these fields can't say (a book's pages, another plugin's data) rides along in `raw`, which you never write yourself. A field that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class Item
---@field kind string? The item type: `"minecraft:diamond_sword"`, or `"diamond_sword"`. Required unless `item` names a project item, whose kind it then is; an item read from the server always has it.
---@field item string? The project item (`items/<id>/`) this stack is: `{ item = "ruby", count = 3 }`. Its file fills in every field the table leaves out (`nf.items.create` does the same), and the stack carries the item's namespaced id wherever it goes, so it's still a ruby in a chest, on the ground, or after a restart. A project item the project doesn't have is an error, and so is a `kind` that isn't the item's.
---@field count integer? 1 to 99. Defaults to 1.
---@field name Text? Display name, MiniMessage. Not italic unless you say so.
---@field lore Text[]? Lines under the name, each MiniMessage.
---@field enchantments table<string, integer>? Enchantment id → level: `{ ["minecraft:sharpness"] = 5 }`.
---@field glint boolean? The enchanted shimmer without an enchantment, as menus use for "selected".
---@field hide_tooltip boolean? No tooltip at all, for filler panes.
---@field unbreakable boolean?
---@field damage integer? Durability used: 0 is pristine.
---@field item_model string? `<pack>/<key>`: an entry of one of your package's resource packs' `items`, or another loaded package's as `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>` ([looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)). It names a look the server has, or it's an error; read back, it's as your package writes it.
---@field tooltip_style string? `<pack>/<key>`: a tooltip frame from one of your package's resource packs, or another loaded package's as `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>` ([looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)).
---@field equipment ItemEquipment? Worn as armour or equipment, drawn as one of the project's resource packs' equipment looks: `{ asset = "gear/ruby", slot = "head" }`.
---@field color string? `#RRGGBB`, for dyed leather, potions and maps.
---@field profile string? Whose head, for a player head: a name or a UUID.
---@field max_stack_size integer? How many fit in one stack, 1 to 99, instead of the kind's own. More than 1 on an item that takes damage (a sword, armour) is an error.
---@field map integer? The map a filled map shows, by its number: a screen's (`screen:item`), or any of the game's. Only a `minecraft:filled_map` draws it.
---@field rarity "common"|"uncommon"|"rare"|"epic"? The colour of its name when it has no colour of its own: white, yellow, aqua or light purple.
---@field attribute_modifiers ItemAttributeModifier[]? What it changes about whoever wears or holds it: `{ { attribute = "minecraft:attack_damage", amount = 6, operation = "add_value", slot = "main_hand" } }`. These replace the kind's own (a sword's damage) rather than adding to them.
---@field can_break string[]? The blocks a player in adventure mode can break with it: the project's by id (`"ruby_ore"`, a package's `"acme:gem_ore"`), the game's with their namespace (`"minecraft:stone"`), `#tags` of blocks (`"#minecraft:logs"`, or one a project datapack defines), or `"*"` for any block: `{ "ruby_ore", "#minecraft:logs" }`. A project block it names that the project doesn't have is an error.
---@field can_place_on string[]? The blocks a player in adventure mode can place it on, written as `can_break`'s: `{ "*" }` is anywhere.
---@field hidden_tooltip_components ("attribute_modifiers"|"banner_patterns"|"bees"|"block_entity_data"|"block_state"|"bundle_contents"|"can_break"|"can_place_on"|"charged_projectiles"|"container"|"container_loot"|"damage"|"dyed_color"|"enchantments"|"entity_data"|"firework_explosion"|"fireworks"|"instrument"|"intangible_projectile"|"jukebox_playable"|"lore"|"map_id"|"ominous_bottle_amplifier"|"painting/variant"|"pot_decorations"|"potion_contents"|"profile"|"sign_text_back"|"sign_text_front"|"stored_enchantments"|"sulfur_cube_content"|"suspicious_stew_effects"|"trim"|"tropical_fish/pattern"|"unbreakable"|"written_book_content")[]? Lines of its tooltip to hide, by the component that draws them: `{ "unbreakable", "attribute_modifiers" }` hides "Unbreakable" and the attribute lines ("+2 Attack Damage"). The components still apply: the item stays unbreakable and keeps its modifiers. Left out shows the tooltip as it is; `{}` shows every line. A value that isn't a line an item can hide is an error, and so is one the server's Minecraft version doesn't have.
---@field food ItemFood? What eating it does. Any item with `food` can be eaten, even one that isn't food: `{ nutrition = 4, saturation = 2.4 }`.
---@field cooldown ItemCooldown? A cooldown after using it (an ender pearl's): `{ seconds = 2 }`. Items with the same `group` share one.
---@field hold boolean|ItemHold? Held down with no end: a right click lasts until the player lets go, never finishes and uses nothing up. Letting go is `player_stop_using_item`, and `event.ticks` says how long it was held: a flail thrown on release, a charge that grows while held. `true` draws nothing (no zoom, no pose); `{ animation = "trident" }` poses the player as that use of the game's does, a knife raised to throw. Read back, it's `true` or that table, as it was given (a hold whose animation is `"none"` reads back `true`). Unless `use_effects` says otherwise, the player keeps their speed and can sprint, whatever the animation. It can't go with `food`, and only works on a kind with no use of its own (`minecraft:stick`, not a bow or a shield). Left clicks don't attack while it's held.
---@field use_effects ItemUseEffects? How a player moves while using it, for any use: a `hold`, eating, a bow drawn, a shield raised. `{ speed_multiplier = 1, can_sprint = true }` lets them eat at a run.
---@field data table<string, any>? Your script's own data, saved on the stack itself (its persistent data container), so it goes wherever the item goes: tag a coin with `data = { coin = true }`. A table keyed by strings, holding what a `data()` table may: strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them, `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player` and `Centity` handles, which come back as the same types. Absent when the stack carries none. Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window) is an error, naming where it was.
---@field raw string? Everything else the stack carries, opaque. Present only when the fields above don't say it all. Leave it alone: it's how a read-change-write keeps what you didn't change.

--- One of an item's `attribute_modifiers`: how much it changes one attribute of whoever wears or holds it.
---@class ItemAttributeModifier
---@field id string? A namespaced id telling it apart from the item's other modifiers. Default `"<namespace>:<attribute>_<index>"` (the project's namespace, and its place in the list from 0), which is what you get back when it has that one.
---@field attribute string The attribute: `"minecraft:attack_damage"`, `"minecraft:movement_speed"`, `"minecraft:max_health"`, …
---@field amount number How much.
---@field operation "add_value"|"add_multiplied_base"|"add_multiplied_total" How `amount` applies: added; added times the base value; or the total multiplied by `1 + amount`.
---@field slot "any"|"hand"|"main_hand"|"off_hand"|"armor"|"head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body"|"saddle"? Where the item has to be for it to count. Default `"any"`.

--- What eating an item does: an `Item`'s `food`.
---@class ItemFood
---@field nutrition integer Hunger points it restores (a full bar is 20).
---@field saturation number Saturation it adds.
---@field can_always_eat boolean? Whether it can be eaten when full. Default false.
---@field eat_seconds number? How long eating it takes. Default 1.6.

--- What an item looks like worn, and where: an `Item`'s `equipment` (the `equippable` component).
---@class ItemEquipment
---@field asset string `<pack>/<key>`: an equipment look from one of your package's resource packs, or another loaded package's as `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>` ([looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)).
---@field slot "head"|"chest"|"legs"|"feet"|"body"|"saddle" The slot it is worn in: `"body"` is a horse's or wolf's armour.

--- A cooldown after using an item: an `Item`'s `cooldown`.
---@class ItemCooldown
---@field seconds number How long, more than 0.
---@field group string? A namespaced id: items in one group share a cooldown (`player:set_item_cooldown` takes it too). Default: the item's kind.

--- A `hold` item that poses the player while it's held: an `Item`'s `hold`, as a table. Everything but the pose is a plain `hold = true`'s.
---@class ItemHold
---@field animation ("none"|"eat"|"drink"|"block"|"bow"|"trident"|"crossbow"|"spyglass"|"toot_horn"|"brush"|"bundle"|"spear")? The pose, as one of the game's uses draws it: `"trident"` (arm raised to throw), `"bow"` (both arms out, drawing), `"crossbow"` (both arms, loading), `"block"`, `"spyglass"` (raised to the eye), `"toot_horn"`, `"brush"`, `"spear"` (held low and forward), `"eat"`, `"drink"`, `"bundle"`, or `"none"`. The zoom of a bow and the spyglass's scope are the real items' only. Default `"none"`. A name that isn't one is an error.

--- How a player moves while using an item: an `Item`'s `use_effects`. A field left out is the game's default, or on a `hold` item the hold's.
---@class ItemUseEffects
---@field speed_multiplier number? Their speed while using it, from 0 to 1. Default 0.2, or 1 on a `hold` item.
---@field can_sprint boolean? Whether they can sprint while using it. Default false, or true on a `hold` item.
---@field interact_vibrations boolean? Whether starting to use it sets off sculk sensors. Default true, or false on a `hold` item.

--- What `player:open_menu` opens a window with.
---@class MenuOpenOptions
---@field context any Anything at all, handed to the new window: its script reads it with `this:context()`, and every event on the window carries it as `event.context`. It's the very value, kept on the server, so a table can carry handles and functions. A shared menu has one window for everyone, so giving it a context is an error.

--- What `player:open_dialog`, `dialog:open_for` and `dialog:ask` open a dialog with. Everything here is for this one opening: the dialog's file doesn't change, and the next opening starts from it again.
---@class DialogOpenOptions
---@field context any Anything at all, handed back as `event.context` on every press (and on `close`) of this opening. Kept on the server for this player until the dialog leaves their screen, never sent to them, so it can carry handles and functions.
---@field values table<string, string|number|boolean>? What its inputs start at, by input key, instead of the file's `initial`: a string for a text input, the option's `id` for a `single_option`, a number within the range for a `number_range`, `true` or `false` for a `boolean`. An input the dialog doesn't have is an error.
---@field title Text? The title for this opening, MiniMessage, instead of the file's.
---@field body table<string, Text>? Body text for this opening, MiniMessage, by the body element's `key`: a message's text, or an item's description. A key no body element of the dialog has is an error.

--- Someone opened or closed a menu window.
---@class MenuEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who.
---@field menu Menu The window.
---@field context any What the script that opened the window passed along (`player:open_menu(menu, { context = ... })`), the same as `menu:context()`: the very value, not a copy. `nil` when nothing was, and always for a shared window.

--- Someone clicked while a menu window was open. A click on one of the window's slots is heard by the slot, then the menu, then `nf.on("menu_click")`; a click anywhere else starts at the menu.
---@class MenuClickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field menu Menu The window.
---@field target Slot? The window's slot that was clicked, or `nil` for a click in the player's own inventory or outside both.
---@field index integer? The slot clicked, from 0: in the window when `in_menu` is true, and in the player's own inventory below it otherwise. `nil` for a click outside both.
---@field in_menu boolean Whether the click landed in the window rather than in the player's own inventory below it.
---@field click "left"|"right"|"shift_left"|"shift_right"|"middle"|"number_key"|"swap_offhand"|"double"|"drop"|"control_drop"|"other" What kind of click it was. `swap_offhand` is the swap-hands key (F) over a slot: what is in it and what is in the off hand change places.
---@field hotbar_index integer? For `number_key`: the hotbar slot, 0 to 8.
---@field item Item? What was in the slot clicked.
---@field cursor_item Item? What the player was carrying on the cursor.
---@field context any What the script that opened the window passed along (`player:open_menu(menu, { context = ... })`), the same as `menu:context()`: the very value, not a copy. `nil` when nothing was, and always for a shared window.

--- Someone dragged a stack across slots of a menu window.
---@class MenuDragEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who dragged.
---@field menu Menu The window.
---@field indices integer[] The window's slots the stack is spread over, from 0, in order.
---@field own_indices integer[] The slots of the player's own inventory below it the stack is spread over, in order, numbered as `player:inventory()` numbers them.
---@field click "left"|"right" `"left"` spreads the stack evenly over the slots, `"right"` puts one in each.
---@field cursor_item Item? The stack being dragged.
---@field context any What the script that opened the window passed along (`player:open_menu(menu, { context = ... })`), the same as `menu:context()`: the very value, not a copy. `nil` when nothing was, and always for a shared window.

--- Someone pressed a dialog's button: heard by the button, then the dialog, then `nf.on("dialog_press")`.
---@class DialogPressEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who pressed it.
---@field dialog Dialog The dialog.
---@field target Button The button pressed.
---@field key string The button's `key`.
---@field values table<string, string|number> Every input's answer, by its `key`: the text typed, the option's `id`, the slider's number, or a checkbox's `onTrue`/`onFalse` string (`"true"`/`"false"` unless the file says otherwise).
---@field context any What the script that opened the dialog passed along for this opening (`player:open_dialog(dialog, { context = ... })`): the very value, not a copy. It's kept on the server, never sent to the player, so it can be anything at all. `nil` when nothing was.

--- Someone left a dialog without pressing a button, or a script closed it.
---@class DialogEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who.
---@field dialog Dialog The dialog.
---@field context any What the script that opened the dialog passed along for this opening (`player:open_dialog(dialog, { context = ... })`): the very value, not a copy. It's kept on the server, never sent to the player, so it can be anything at all. `nil` when nothing was.

--- A partial `Item` that picks stacks: a stack matches when every field given matches, so `{ kind = "minecraft:paper", name = "<gold>Ticket" }` finds gold tickets and nothing else. `kind` may be left out (any kind), and `data` matches as a subset: `{ data = { coin = true } }` finds coins whatever else they carry. `count` and `raw` have no place in a match; a field `Item` doesn't have is an error.
---@class ItemMatch
---@field kind string? The item type: `"minecraft:diamond_sword"`, or `"diamond_sword"`. Required unless `item` names a project item, whose kind it then is; an item read from the server always has it.
---@field item string? The project item (`items/<id>/`) this stack is: `{ item = "ruby", count = 3 }`. Its file fills in every field the table leaves out (`nf.items.create` does the same), and the stack carries the item's namespaced id wherever it goes, so it's still a ruby in a chest, on the ground, or after a restart. A project item the project doesn't have is an error, and so is a `kind` that isn't the item's.
---@field name Text? Display name, MiniMessage. Not italic unless you say so.
---@field lore Text[]? Lines under the name, each MiniMessage.
---@field enchantments table<string, integer>? Enchantment id → level: `{ ["minecraft:sharpness"] = 5 }`.
---@field glint boolean? The enchanted shimmer without an enchantment, as menus use for "selected".
---@field hide_tooltip boolean? No tooltip at all, for filler panes.
---@field unbreakable boolean?
---@field damage integer? Durability used: 0 is pristine.
---@field item_model string? `<pack>/<key>`: an entry of one of your package's resource packs' `items`, or another loaded package's as `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>` ([looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)). It names a look the server has, or it's an error; read back, it's as your package writes it.
---@field tooltip_style string? `<pack>/<key>`: a tooltip frame from one of your package's resource packs, or another loaded package's as `<namespace>:<pack>/<key>` ([looks are values](packages.md#looks-are-values)).
---@field equipment ItemEquipment? Worn as armour or equipment, drawn as one of the project's resource packs' equipment looks: `{ asset = "gear/ruby", slot = "head" }`.
---@field color string? `#RRGGBB`, for dyed leather, potions and maps.
---@field profile string? Whose head, for a player head: a name or a UUID.
---@field max_stack_size integer? How many fit in one stack, 1 to 99, instead of the kind's own. More than 1 on an item that takes damage (a sword, armour) is an error.
---@field map integer? The map a filled map shows, by its number: a screen's (`screen:item`), or any of the game's. Only a `minecraft:filled_map` draws it.
---@field rarity "common"|"uncommon"|"rare"|"epic"? The colour of its name when it has no colour of its own: white, yellow, aqua or light purple.
---@field attribute_modifiers ItemAttributeModifier[]? What it changes about whoever wears or holds it: `{ { attribute = "minecraft:attack_damage", amount = 6, operation = "add_value", slot = "main_hand" } }`. These replace the kind's own (a sword's damage) rather than adding to them.
---@field can_break string[]? The blocks a player in adventure mode can break with it: the project's by id (`"ruby_ore"`, a package's `"acme:gem_ore"`), the game's with their namespace (`"minecraft:stone"`), `#tags` of blocks (`"#minecraft:logs"`, or one a project datapack defines), or `"*"` for any block: `{ "ruby_ore", "#minecraft:logs" }`. A project block it names that the project doesn't have is an error.
---@field can_place_on string[]? The blocks a player in adventure mode can place it on, written as `can_break`'s: `{ "*" }` is anywhere.
---@field hidden_tooltip_components ("attribute_modifiers"|"banner_patterns"|"bees"|"block_entity_data"|"block_state"|"bundle_contents"|"can_break"|"can_place_on"|"charged_projectiles"|"container"|"container_loot"|"damage"|"dyed_color"|"enchantments"|"entity_data"|"firework_explosion"|"fireworks"|"instrument"|"intangible_projectile"|"jukebox_playable"|"lore"|"map_id"|"ominous_bottle_amplifier"|"painting/variant"|"pot_decorations"|"potion_contents"|"profile"|"sign_text_back"|"sign_text_front"|"stored_enchantments"|"sulfur_cube_content"|"suspicious_stew_effects"|"trim"|"tropical_fish/pattern"|"unbreakable"|"written_book_content")[]? Lines of its tooltip to hide, by the component that draws them: `{ "unbreakable", "attribute_modifiers" }` hides "Unbreakable" and the attribute lines ("+2 Attack Damage"). The components still apply: the item stays unbreakable and keeps its modifiers. Left out shows the tooltip as it is; `{}` shows every line. A value that isn't a line an item can hide is an error, and so is one the server's Minecraft version doesn't have.
---@field food ItemFood? What eating it does. Any item with `food` can be eaten, even one that isn't food: `{ nutrition = 4, saturation = 2.4 }`.
---@field cooldown ItemCooldown? A cooldown after using it (an ender pearl's): `{ seconds = 2 }`. Items with the same `group` share one.
---@field hold boolean|ItemHold? Held down with no end: a right click lasts until the player lets go, never finishes and uses nothing up. Letting go is `player_stop_using_item`, and `event.ticks` says how long it was held: a flail thrown on release, a charge that grows while held. `true` draws nothing (no zoom, no pose); `{ animation = "trident" }` poses the player as that use of the game's does, a knife raised to throw. Read back, it's `true` or that table, as it was given (a hold whose animation is `"none"` reads back `true`). Unless `use_effects` says otherwise, the player keeps their speed and can sprint, whatever the animation. It can't go with `food`, and only works on a kind with no use of its own (`minecraft:stick`, not a bow or a shield). Left clicks don't attack while it's held.
---@field use_effects ItemUseEffects? How a player moves while using it, for any use: a `hold`, eating, a bow drawn, a shield raised. `{ speed_multiplier = 1, can_sprint = true }` lets them eat at a run.
---@field data table<string, any>? Your script's own data, saved on the stack itself (its persistent data container), so it goes wherever the item goes: tag a coin with `data = { coin = true }`. A table keyed by strings, holding what a `data()` table may: strings, numbers, booleans, tables of them, `Vec3`s, `Location`s, `Item` tables, and `Player` and `Centity` handles, which come back as the same types. Absent when the stack carries none. Anything else (a function, a `Menu` window) is an error, naming where it was.

--- How `nf.commands.register` describes a command: everything optional. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CommandDefinition
---@field description string? One line for the server's help.
---@field permission string? A permission node the sender needs. On Paper the command (or subcommand) doesn't exist for a player without it: not in tab completion, not when typed. The console has every permission.
---@field aliases string[]? Other names for it.
---@field arguments CommandArgument[]? What comes after it, in order. Arguments with a `default` are optional and must come last; a `text` argument takes the rest of the line, so it must be the last one.
---@field subcommands table<string, Subcommand>? Words that can come next, each with its own arguments, permission and handler: `{ add = { ... }, remove = { ... } }`. Names are lowercase letters, digits, `_` and `-`. A subcommand wins over an argument when both could take the word.
---@field players_only boolean? Refuses the console (and command blocks) with a message, so the handler can count on `event.player`. A subcommand under a `players_only` command is players-only too.

--- A word after a command (or after another subcommand) with its own arguments and handler: `/shop give <player>`. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class Subcommand
---@field description string? One line for the server's help.
---@field permission string? A permission node the sender needs. On Paper the command (or subcommand) doesn't exist for a player without it: not in tab completion, not when typed. The console has every permission.
---@field arguments CommandArgument[]? What comes after it, in order. Arguments with a `default` are optional and must come last; a `text` argument takes the rest of the line, so it must be the last one.
---@field subcommands table<string, Subcommand>? Words that can come next, each with its own arguments, permission and handler: `{ add = { ... }, remove = { ... } }`. Names are lowercase letters, digits, `_` and `-`. A subcommand wins over an argument when both could take the word.
---@field players_only boolean? Refuses the console (and command blocks) with a message, so the handler can count on `event.player`. A subcommand under a `players_only` command is players-only too.
---@field handler (fun(event: CommandEvent))? Runs when someone types it. A handler that errors is logged and whoever typed the command is told; the command stays.

--- One argument of a command. Its `type` decides what may be typed, what tab completion offers, the usage message, and the value the handler gets in `event.arguments[name]`. On Paper it's a real Brigadier argument, so the player's client checks and completes it as they type. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CommandArgument
---@field name string Its key in `event.arguments`, and what the usage message shows (`<amount>`). Letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit; unique within the command.
---@field type "word"|"text"|"integer"|"number"|"boolean"|"choice"|"player"|"players"|"entity"|"entities"|"world"|"position"|"location"|"block_state"|"item"|"centity"|"centity_kind"|"menu"|"dialog" What it takes, and what the handler gets: `"word"`: one word, a `string`. `"text"`: the rest of the line, spaces and all, a `string`. `"integer"`: a whole number, within `min` and `max` when given, a `integer`. `"number"`: any number, within `min` and `max` when given, a `number`. `"boolean"`: `true` or `false`, a `boolean`. `"choice"`: one of `choices`, as typed, a `string`. `"player"`: an online player by name, or a selector that picks exactly one (`@p`, `@s`), a `Player`. `"players"`: a name or a selector (`@a`, `@a[distance=..10]`) picking at least one player, a `Player[]`. `"entity"`: a selector that picks exactly one entity, an online player's name, or a loaded entity's UUID (a `Player` for a player), an `Entity`. `"entities"`: a selector, name or UUID picking at least one entity, an `Entity[]`. `"world"`: a loaded world by name, a `World`. `"position"`: three coordinates, each a number, `~` (relative to the sender) or `^` (local to where they look); whole numbers are block centres in x and z, as in vanilla commands, a `Vec3`. `"location"`: the same three coordinates, in the sender's world facing the way they face (the default world and no facing from the console), a `Location`. `"block_state"`: a block state like `oak_stairs[facing=east]`, given back in full with every property (`"minecraft:oak_stairs[facing=east,half=bottom,…]"`), a `string`. `"item"`: an item like `diamond_sword`, or with components on Paper (`diamond_sword[enchantments={sharpness:3}]`), an `Item`. `"centity"`: a live centity by its instance id (completed from the live ones), a `Centity`. `"centity_kind"`: one of the project's centities, by its folder name, a `string`. `"menu"`: one of the project's menus, by its id, a `string`. `"dialog"`: one of the project's dialogs, by its id, a `Dialog`.
---@field default any Makes it optional: the value the handler gets when it's left out. A value of the argument's type (a number for `integer`, a `Vec3` for `position`, one of `choices` for `choice`), or `false` for "nothing", the way to make a `player` or `item` argument optional: then `event.arguments.target or event.player`.
---@field min number? The smallest value an `integer` or `number` argument takes.
---@field max number? The largest value an `integer` or `number` argument takes.
---@field choices string[]? What a `choice` argument takes: single words. Required for `choice`, and only for it.
---@field complete (fun(event: CommandEvent, partial: string): string[])? Tab completion of its own, instead of what its type offers. Gets the event so far (`event.arguments` holds the arguments before this one) and what has been typed of this argument; returns the suggestions. NetherForge keeps those starting with `partial`. Runs in the module's scope, within its instruction budget, every time a player's client asks, so keep it cheap.

--- What a command handler, and an argument's `complete`, receives.
---@class CommandEvent
---@field name string The command's name, as registered.
---@field label string What they typed to run it: the name, or one of its aliases.
---@field arguments table<string, any> The arguments by name, typed by their declaration: a `Player` for a `player` argument, a `Vec3` for a `position`, an `integer` for an `integer`. An optional argument left out has its `default`.
---@field input string Everything typed after the command's name, subcommands included, as typed.
---@field sender Sender Who ran it, player or console. Answer them with `event.sender:send_message(text)`.
---@field player Player? The player who ran it, or `nil` from the console. Never `nil` in a `players_only` command.

--- How `nf.worlds.create` makes a world. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class WorldCreateOptions
---@field generator "normal"|"flat"|"void"|"amplified"|"large_biomes"? Which of the game's own world types generates it: the usual (`"normal"`), the default superflat (`"flat"`), nothing at all (`"void"`, for maps built or placed by scripts), or one of the other vanilla world types (`"amplified"`, `"large_biomes"`). Default `"normal"`. Not with `terrain`, which is one of the project's instead. Only a `"normal"` environment takes it: a nether or an end always generates as the game's own.
---@field terrain string? One of the project's terrains (`terrain/<id>.json`; `"acme:hills"` for a package's), which generates it in place of the game's own. Not with `generator`. Kept with the world, so it generates the same way when it's loaded again; a terrain the project changes later only shapes chunks generated afterwards. A world with a terrain is a `"normal"` environment.
---@field environment "normal"|"nether"|"end"? Which kind of dimension: its sky, its light, whether water boils. Default `"normal"`.
---@field dimension_type string? One of the project's dimension types (`dimension_types/<id>.json`; `"acme:deep"` for a package's): the world's build limits, light, sky and rules (beds, respawn anchors, piglins, raids) are its. Kept with the world, so it loads the same again. The server learns dimension types only as it starts: one it didn't start with (added or fixed since) is an error until it restarts. Default: the environment's own (the game's overworld, nether or end).
---@field seed integer? Its seed: a project generator makes the same world for the same seed. Default: a random one.
---@field structures boolean? Whether villages, temples and the like generate. Default `true`.
---@field keep_spawn_loaded boolean? Whether the chunks around its spawn stay loaded with nobody near. Default `false`.

--- How `world:unload` lets a world go. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class WorldUnloadOptions
---@field save boolean? Whether it saves first. Default `true`; `false` throws away what changed since the last save (with `delete`, nothing is saved either way).
---@field delete boolean? Whether its files are deleted afterwards, and the project forgets it. Default `false`.
---@field move_players_to World? Which world the players in it go to, at its spawn. Default: the main world. To send them somewhere exact, teleport them before unloading.

--- How `border:set_size` changes it. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BorderSizeOptions
---@field ticks integer? How long it takes to get there, moving steadily. Default 0: at once.

--- How a border hurts players outside it: what `border:damage()` gives and `border:set_damage` takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BorderDamage
---@field amount number? Damage each second for every block past `buffer` (half a heart is 1). The game starts at 0.2.
---@field buffer number? How many blocks outside the border are safe. The game starts at 5.

--- When players' screens turn red as a border comes near: what `border:warning()` gives and `border:set_warning` takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BorderWarning
---@field distance integer? Within this many blocks of it. The game starts at 5.
---@field ticks integer? When a shrinking border will reach them within this many ticks. The game starts at 300 (15 seconds).

--- What `world:save_structure` keeps. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class StructureSaveOptions
---@field entities boolean? Whether the entities in the box are saved with the blocks (never players, and never what centities are drawn with). Default `false`.

--- How `world:place_structure` places one. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class StructurePlaceOptions
---@field rotation integer? How far it turns clockwise (seen from above) about its smallest corner, in degrees: 0, 90, 180 or 270 (any multiple of 90). Default 0.
---@field mirror "none"|"left_right"|"front_back"? Whether it's mirrored first: `"left_right"` flips it along z, `"front_back"` along x. Default `"none"`.
---@field integrity number? How much of it is placed, 0 to 1: each block is kept with this chance. Default 1: all of it.
---@field entities boolean? Whether the entities saved with it are placed too. Default `true`.

--- How `world:place_gradually` places one. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class GradualPlaceOptions
---@field rotation integer? How far it turns clockwise (seen from above) about its smallest corner, in degrees: 0, 90, 180 or 270 (any multiple of 90). Default 0.
---@field mirror "none"|"left_right"|"front_back"? Whether it's mirrored first: `"left_right"` flips it along z, `"front_back"` along x. Default `"none"`.
---@field integrity number? How much of it is placed, 0 to 1: each block is kept with this chance. Default 1: all of it.
---@field entities boolean? Whether the entities saved with it are placed too, once its blocks are in. Default `true`.
---@field per_tick integer? The most blocks it places in one tick, 1 or more: slower than the server's share, never faster. Default: as many as its share allows.

--- How `world:fill_gradually` fills. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class GradualFillOptions
---@field update boolean? Whether neighbours react to each block (sand falls, water flows), as `BlockSetOptions` says. Default `true`.
---@field per_tick integer? The most blocks it goes through in one tick, 1 or more: slower than the server's share, never faster. Default: as many as its share allows.

--- What `world:blocks` gives for each block. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BlockBoxOptions
---@field what "kind"|"solid"|"class"? What a block is given as: `"kind"` its block id (`"minecraft:stone"`), `"solid"` whether it's solid, as `block:is_solid()` says, or `"class"` a number, `0` for a block nothing stands in the way of (air, grass, a torch), `1` for water or lava, `2` for a solid one. Default `"kind"`.
---@field custom boolean? Whether a block of the project's own gives its id (`"wooden_chair"`, as `block:custom():id()` says) in place of what `what` would make of the note block it's held as. Default `false`.

--- What `world:blocks` read: each block of the box, in one list, and the box's size and corner.
---@class BlockBox
---@field cells (string|integer|boolean)[] Each block, x fastest, then z, then y: the block `x`, `y`, `z` from the corner is at `1 + x + size_x * (z + size_z * y)`. What each is depends on `options.what` (and `options.custom`).
---@field size_x integer How many blocks the box is along x.
---@field size_y integer How many blocks the box is along y.
---@field size_z integer How many blocks the box is along z.
---@field corner Vec3 The block the box starts from: the lowest x, y and z of its corners, floored.

--- How a sky comes or goes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class SkyOptions
---@field fade_ticks integer? Ticks it takes to fade in or out, through the sky's steps (its file's `fade`). Default 0: at once.

--- How a block is changed. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BlockSetOptions
---@field update boolean? Whether its neighbours react (sand falls, redstone updates, water flows), as when a player places a block. Default `true`; `false` sets it quietly.

--- How particles are spawned. Every key is optional; which ones a particle takes depends on its kind, and one it doesn't take is an error, as is any key that isn't one of these.
---@class ParticleOptions
---@field count integer? How many, scattered around the position by `spread`. Default 1. With 0, one particle is sent moving along `spread` (a direction) at `speed`.
---@field spread Vec3? How far they scatter along each axis (a Gaussian spread), in blocks. Default none. With `count = 0`, the direction the one particle moves in.
---@field speed number? How fast they move, for particles that move. Default 0.
---@field color string? `"#rrggbb"`: dust, dust that changes colour, and coloured particles. Default white.
---@field to_color string? `"#rrggbb"`: what dust that changes colour fades to. Default white.
---@field size number? How big dust is, 0.01 to 4. Default 1.
---@field block_state string? Which block block particles are made of: `"minecraft:stone"`. Required for those.
---@field item Item? Which item item particles show. Required for those.
---@field viewers Player[]? Only these players see them (those near enough). Default: everyone near enough.
---@field force boolean? Show them even to players who turned particles down, and from up to 128 blocks away. Default `false`.

--- How a sound plays. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class SoundOptions
---@field volume number? How loud, from 0. Default 1. Above 1 it carries further rather than getting louder: 16 blocks per 1.
---@field pitch number? How high, 0.5 (an octave down) to 2 (an octave up). Default 1.
---@field category "master"|"music"|"record"|"weather"|"block"|"hostile"|"neutral"|"player"|"ambient"|"voice"|"ui"? Which of the player's volume sliders it follows. Default `"master"`.

--- How weather set with `world:set_weather` lasts. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class WeatherOptions
---@field ticks integer? How long it lasts before the game picks again (at least 1). Leave it out for a random length, as the game chooses.

--- How an explosion behaves. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ExplosionOptions
---@field fire boolean? Whether it sets fires. Default `false`.
---@field break_blocks boolean? Whether it breaks blocks. Default `true`.

--- Where `world:locate_biome` looks. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BiomeSearchOptions
---@field near Vec3? Where it looks from, in this world. Default the world's spawn.
---@field radius integer? How far it looks, in blocks across: 1 to 6400. Default 6400, as `/locate biome`.

--- How lightning strikes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class LightningOptions
---@field effect_only boolean? Only the flash and the thunder: no fire, no damage. Default `false`.

--- What a ray can hit. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class RaycastOptions
---@field blocks boolean? Whether blocks stop it. Default `true`.
---@field entities boolean? Whether entities (players included) stop it, by their hitboxes. Default `true`.
---@field ignore Entity[]? Entities it goes through: the one it's cast from, usually. Default none.
---@field centities boolean? Whether centities' hitboxes stop it. Default `true`.
---@field fluids boolean? Whether water and lava stop it, as solid blocks. Default `false`: it goes through them.

--- How `world:flood` fills. A block is named as it is in a game block file: an id (`"minecraft:oak_log"`), a `#tag` (`"#minecraft:logs"`) or a project block's id (`"ruby_ore"`), or by what it is: `"solid"` (what players stand on), `"liquid"` (water and lava) or `"passable"` (neither: air, torches, flowers). A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class FloodOptions
---@field through string[] The blocks it fills: each one it reaches that any of these names. Required.
---@field except string[]? Blocks it doesn't fill even when `through` names them: `{ "#minecraft:doors" }` keeps a room's air from leaking out through a door. Default none.
---@field directions Vec3[]? Which neighbours it goes on to from each block, as offsets of whole blocks (`vec3(0, 1, 0)` is the one above), at most 2 on each axis. Default the six that share a face: `vec3(1, 0, 0)`, `vec3(-1, 0, 0)` and so on. A tree felled up and sideways but never down leaves out every offset with y = -1.
---@field limit integer? The most blocks it fills, at least 1, at most 32,768. Default 4,096. `stopped` is `"limit"` when there was more.
---@field bounds Bounds? `{ min = Vec3, max = Vec3 }`: it never fills a block outside this box (both corners' blocks included), and `stopped` is `"bounds"` when it would have. Default: no box.
---@field kinds boolean? Also answer what each block is (`kinds`, `edge_kinds`): its id without properties (`"minecraft:oak_log"`), or a project block's id. Default `false`.

--- What `world:flood` filled.
---@class FloodResult
---@field positions Vec3[] Every block it filled, as its lowest corner (whole numbers), the start first, then outward a step at a time.
---@field edges Vec3[] Every block next to one it filled that it didn't fill (a tree's leaves, a room's walls and door), once each. Not a block in an unloaded chunk or outside its `bounds`.
---@field kinds string[]? With `kinds = true`, what each of `positions` is, in the same order. `nil` otherwise.
---@field edge_kinds string[]? With `kinds = true`, what each of `edges` is, in the same order. `nil` otherwise.
---@field stopped "limit"|"unloaded"|"bounds"? Why it didn't fill everything it reaches: `"limit"` (more past its limit), `"unloaded"` (a chunk that isn't loaded, or the world's top or bottom, in its way) or `"bounds"`. `nil` when it filled all of it: a closed room, a whole tree.

--- What a spatial query (`world:overlap_box`, `overlap_sphere`, `overlap_cone`, `nearest`) finds. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class QueryOptions
---@field centities boolean? Whether it finds centities. Default `true`.
---@field players boolean? Whether it finds players. Default `true`.
---@field entities boolean? Whether it finds entities that aren't players (mobs, dropped items, arrows...). Default `true`.
---@field kinds string[]? Only centities of these kinds (their folder names), and entities of these types (`"minecraft:zombie"`). Default: any.
---@field exclude (Centity|Entity)[]? What it leaves out: whatever is asking, usually. Default none.
---@field limit integer? At most this many, the nearest. At least 1. Default: all.
---@field line_of_sight boolean? Only what a ray from the query's own point (the middle of a box, the centre of a sphere, a cone's tip, a sweep's start) reaches without a block in the way, aimed at its hitbox's nearest point. Default `false`.

--- How `world:sweep` moves and what it finds: everything `QueryOptions` takes, and these. A key that isn't one of them is an error.
---@class SweepOptions
---@field centities boolean? Whether it finds centities. Default `true`.
---@field players boolean? Whether it finds players. Default `true`.
---@field entities boolean? Whether it finds entities that aren't players (mobs, dropped items, arrows...). Default `true`.
---@field kinds string[]? Only centities of these kinds (their folder names), and entities of these types (`"minecraft:zombie"`). Default: any.
---@field exclude (Centity|Entity)[]? What it leaves out: whatever is asking, usually. Default none.
---@field limit integer? At most this many, the nearest. At least 1. Default: all.
---@field line_of_sight boolean? Only what a ray from the query's own point (the middle of a box, the centre of a sphere, a cone's tip, a sweep's start) reaches without a block in the way, aimed at its hitbox's nearest point. Default `false`.
---@field radius number? The moving sphere's radius, in blocks, at least 0. Default 0: a point.
---@field blocks boolean? Whether blocks stop it (their collision shapes, as `raycast` sees them). Default `true`. A block stops the sphere's middle, not its edge.
---@field fluids boolean? Whether water and lava stop it, as solid blocks. Default `false`.

--- One thing a spatial query found. Exactly one of `centity`, `entity` and `block` is set: a `block` only ends a `world:sweep`.
---@class QueryHit
---@field centity Centity? The centity found, or `nil`.
---@field node Node? Which of `centity`'s nodes was nearest (its hitbox), or `nil` for anything else.
---@field entity Entity? The player or entity found, or `nil`.
---@field block Block? The block a `world:sweep` stopped at, or `nil`.
---@field position Vec3 Where: for an overlap or `nearest`, the point of the hitbox nearest the query's own point; for a `sweep`, where the sphere's middle is when it first touches (on a block, where it stops).
---@field distance number How far that is from the query's own point; along the way, for a `sweep`. 0 when the point is inside the hitbox.
---@field normal Vec3? For a `sweep`'s block, the unit direction straight out of the face it met: what a bounce reflects off. `nil` otherwise.

--- What `world:raycast` hit: where, and what. Exactly one of `block`, `entity` and `centity` is set.
---@class RaycastHit
---@field position Vec3 Where the ray met it, in the world.
---@field normal Vec3 The unit direction straight out of the face it hit, in the world.
---@field distance number How far along the ray that is, in blocks.
---@field block Block? The block it hit, or `nil`.
---@field entity Entity? The entity it hit, or `nil`.
---@field centity Centity? The centity whose hitbox it hit, or `nil`.
---@field node Node? Which node of `centity` it hit (its hitbox), or `nil` for anything else.

--- How `bossbar:attach` ties a bar to a centity. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BossBarAttachOptions
---@field range number? How near a player must be to see it, in blocks. Default 48.
---@field health boolean? Its progress follows the centity's `health` (when it has any). Default `true`.
---@field remove_with boolean? Removed when the centity is. Default `true`.

--- What `nf.bossbars.create` makes a bar with. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BossBarOptions
---@field text Text? What it says, MiniMessage. Default none.
---@field color "pink"|"blue"|"red"|"green"|"yellow"|"purple"|"white"? Default `"pink"`, the way vanilla bars start.
---@field style "progress"|"notched_6"|"notched_10"|"notched_12"|"notched_20"? One bar, or split into notches. Default `"progress"`, one bar.
---@field progress number? How full, 0 to 1. Default 1.
---@field icon string? A glyph (`<pack>/<key>`) drawn just left of the bar's left end, wherever the centred text puts its words: a boss's head, say. It's drawn on the text's line at the glyph's own `ascent`: the bar sits 2 pixels below the text's baseline, so a lower ascent (even a negative one) hangs it down beside the bar. Placing it needs the text's width, so text the server can't measure (no `fonts/default.json`) draws it in front of the words instead. Default none.

--- What `nf.loot.roll` and `nf.loot.fill` roll with: who and what the roll is for, which the table's conditions ask about. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class LootContext
---@field player Player? The player behind the roll: who killed the mob, broke the block or opened the chest. A `player` condition passes only with one. Default none.
---@field tool Item? What the player killed or broke it with, for `tool` and `enchantment` conditions. Default none: those conditions don't pass.
---@field luck number? The roll's luck: more `bonusRolls`, and each entry's weight shifted by its `quality`. Default 0.
---@field looted Entity? The entity whose loot this is, which a game table for a mob needs. Default none.
---@field location Location? Where a game table is rolled. Default the player's place, else the looted entity's.
---@field seed integer? Rolls with this seed give the same items every time, on the server and in the editor. Default a new one each roll.

--- What a HUD bar shows: `hud:bar(name)`.
---@class HudBar
---@field value number How much of it is full, as set.
---@field max number Out of how much.
---@field segments integer How many of its segments are drawn.

--- How `hud:fill_bar` draws a bar.
---@class HudFillOptions
---@field segments integer? Draw only this many of the bar's segments, 1 to its own. Default all of them.

--- What a spawn condition (`nf.spawning.condition`) is asked about: one centity at one place.
---@class SpawnCheck
---@field centity string Which centity, as the project names it: its folder name under `centities/` (`wisp`), or `acme:wisp` for one a package brings.
---@field location Location Where it would stand: the block its feet would be in.
---@field player Player The player it would appear near.

--- A block is about to be placed.
---@class ProjectBlockPlaceEvent: Event
---@field player Player? Who is placing it, or `nil` for a script.
---@field block CustomBlock Where it goes: what is there now is replaced, so `block:custom()` is `nil` until it has been placed (`this:id()` says which block is being placed).
---@field item Item? The stack placing it, or `nil` for a script.
---@field facing "north"|"east"|"south"|"west"|"up"|"down"? The way it will face, for a block that turns: toward the player placing it. `nil` for one that doesn't turn.

--- How `project_block:place` places one. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class BlockPlaceOptions
---@field facing "north"|"east"|"south"|"west"|"up"|"down"? The way its front faces, for a block that turns (`rotation` in its `block.json`): a way it can't face is an error. Default north.

--- A block was clicked.
---@class ProjectBlockClickEvent: Event
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field block CustomBlock The block.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which button: the left (hit) or right (use).
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west" Which face of the block was clicked.
---@field item Item? What's in the hand the click was with, or `nil` for an empty hand.
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand" Which hand: the game asks for each hand in turn.

--- A block ticked.
---@class ProjectBlockTickEvent: Event
---@field block CustomBlock The block that ticked.

--- What `nf.teams.create` makes a team with. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TeamOptions
---@field display_name Text? What it's called where Minecraft names a team, MiniMessage. Default its name.
---@field prefix Text? What goes before each member's name, MiniMessage. Default none.
---@field suffix Text? What goes after each member's name, MiniMessage. Default none.
---@field color "black"|"dark_blue"|"dark_green"|"dark_aqua"|"dark_red"|"dark_purple"|"gold"|"gray"|"dark_gray"|"blue"|"green"|"aqua"|"red"|"light_purple"|"yellow"|"white"? The colour of members' names and glow. Default none.
---@field friendly_fire boolean? Whether members can hurt each other. Default `true`.
---@field see_invisible_teammates boolean? Whether members see each other while invisible. Default `true`.
---@field nametags "always"|"never"|"hide_for_other_teams"|"hide_for_own_team"? `"always"` (the default), `"never"`, `"hide_for_other_teams"` (only teammates see them) or `"hide_for_own_team"` (only players outside the team see them).
---@field collision "always"|"never"|"push_other_teams"|"push_own_team"? `"always"` (the default), `"never"`, `"push_other_teams"` (members pass through each other) or `"push_own_team"` (members push only each other).

--- What a schedule is called and what to do about a run the server missed. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ScheduleOptions
---@field id string? A name for the schedule, which what it last ran is kept under, so it's known across restarts. Lowercase letters, digits and `_` (starting with a letter or digit, at most 64 characters), and no two of a package's live schedules share one. Default none: the schedule is forgotten when it stops.
---@field catch_up boolean? Run once when the script starts if a run was missed since the schedule last ran (the server was off over its time). Needs an `id`; a schedule seen for the first time has missed nothing. Default `false`: a missed run is skipped.

--- One statement of `db:transaction`.
---@class DatabaseStatement
---@field sql string One SQL statement in the database's own dialect (SQLite's for the package's own database; MySQL's or PostgreSQL's for a connection the server owner set up), with `?` where a value goes: the values come in `params`, never inside the text, so a name a player typed can't become SQL. A second statement after a `;` is an error: use `db:transaction` for several. On PostgreSQL a literal `?` (the JSON operators) is written `??`.
---@field params table? The values for the `?`s, in order: `{ player_name, 5 }`. Each is a `string`, `number`, `integer` or `boolean` (stored as 1 or 0); a hole in the list (`{ 1, nil, 3 }`) is SQL `NULL`, and so are the last `?`s when the list is shorter (Lua can't tell `{ name, nil }` from `{ name }`). Any other kind of value is an error, and more values than `?`s fails the statement.

--- What `db:execute` (and each statement of `db:transaction`) did.
---@class DatabaseResult
---@field changes integer How many rows it inserted, updated or deleted.
---@field last_insert_id integer? On the package's own database, the `rowid` of the last row it inserted (the new row's `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY`), or `nil` when nothing was ever inserted. On a MySQL connection, the `AUTO_INCREMENT` key the statement generated, or `nil` when it generated none. On PostgreSQL always `nil`: ask for the key with `INSERT ... RETURNING id` through `db:query`.

--- What `nf.http.request` takes.
---@class HttpRequestOptions
---@field url string An `http://` or `https://` URL, without a user name or password in it (send credentials in a header). Its host must be one the calling package declares in `requires`.
---@field method "GET"|"POST"|"PUT"|"PATCH"|"DELETE"|"HEAD"|"OPTIONS"? The HTTP method; `"GET"` when left out.
---@field headers table<string, string>? Request headers by name. `Host`, `Content-Length`, `Transfer-Encoding`, `Connection`, `Upgrade` and the like are set by the server and refused here. A `User-Agent` of `NetherForge` is sent unless you give one. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and `Proxy-Authorization` are dropped when a redirect leads to another host.
---@field body string? The request body, as text (UTF-8). Its size is limited by `http.max-request-bytes` in the server's `config.yml`. A `GET`, `HEAD` or `OPTIONS` can't have one.

--- What `nf.http.request` gives.
---@class HttpResponse
---@field status integer The status code: `200`, `404`.
---@field headers table<string, string> The response headers by name in lowercase (`response.headers["content-type"]`); a header sent more than once is its values joined with `, `.
---@field body string The body as text, decoded by the charset the response names (UTF-8 when it names none), so a response that isn't text (an image) doesn't survive. Its size is limited by `http.max-response-bytes` in the server's `config.yml`.
---@field url string The URL the response came from: the request's own, or where its redirects ended.

--- What `nf.http.stream` takes: `nf.http.request`'s options, and `format`.
---@class HttpStreamOptions
---@field url string An `http://` or `https://` URL, as for `nf.http.request`. Its host must be one the calling package declares in `requires`.
---@field method "GET"|"POST"|"PUT"|"PATCH"|"DELETE"|"OPTIONS"? The HTTP method; `"GET"` when left out.
---@field headers table<string, string>? Request headers by name, as for `nf.http.request`. A `format` of `"events"` sends `Accept: text/event-stream` unless you give an `Accept`.
---@field body string? The request body, as for `nf.http.request` (`http.max-request-bytes`).
---@field format "lines"|"events"|"chunks"? How the response is split into messages. `"lines"` (the default): one message per line, without its line ending (NDJSON, a log tail). `"events"`: Server-Sent Events (`text/event-stream`), one message per event, with its `type` and `id`; comments and `retry` are skipped. `"chunks"`: the text as it arrives, in pieces of no particular size. A line or event over `connections.max-message-bytes` ends the stream (reason `"limit"`); text is UTF-8.

--- What `nf.http.websocket` takes.
---@class WebSocketOptions
---@field url string A `ws://` or `wss://` URL, without a user name or password in it. Its host must be one the calling package declares in `requires.http`.
---@field headers table<string, string>? Headers for the opening request (an `Authorization`), with `nf.http.request`'s rules. The `Sec-WebSocket-*` headers are the server's to set.
---@field protocols string[]? Subprotocols to offer (`Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`), in order of preference; `socket:protocol()` says which one the other side chose.

--- A stream's response began.
---@class HttpStreamOpenEvent: Event
---@field stream HttpStream The stream.
---@field status integer The status code: `200`, `404`.
---@field headers table<string, string> The response headers by name in lowercase, as `nf.http.request` gives them.

--- A piece of a stream arrived.
---@class HttpStreamMessageEvent: Event
---@field stream HttpStream The stream.
---@field data string The line (without its line ending), the event's data (its `data:` lines joined with line breaks), or the chunk.
---@field type string? For `format = "events"`: the event's type, `"message"` when it names none. `nil` for the other formats.
---@field id string? For `format = "events"`: the last event id the stream set (`id:`), which a script reconnecting sends back as `Last-Event-ID`. `nil` when none was set, and for the other formats.

--- A stream ended.
---@class HttpStreamEndEvent: Event
---@field stream HttpStream The stream.
---@field reason "finished"|"closed"|"failed"|"limit"|"idle"|"unloaded" Why: the server ended the response; `close()` was called; the connection couldn't be made or broke (a name that doesn't resolve, an address that isn't allowed, a refused connection, a redirect it couldn't follow, no answer within `http.timeout-seconds`); an owner's limit in `config.yml`'s `http:` section was reached (too many connections open, the request rate, a message over `connections.max-message-bytes`, the package's `connections.bytes-per-minute`); nothing arrived for `connections.idle-seconds`; or the script that opened it unloaded.
---@field status integer? The response's status, when it had opened.
---@field detail string? What went wrong, in words, for `"failed"` and `"limit"`; `nil` otherwise.

--- A WebSocket opened.
---@class WebSocketOpenEvent: Event
---@field socket WebSocket The socket.
---@field protocol string? The subprotocol the other side chose, or `nil`.
---@field headers table<string, string> The opening response's headers by name in lowercase.

--- A text message arrived on a WebSocket.
---@class WebSocketMessageEvent: Event
---@field socket WebSocket The socket.
---@field data string The message.

--- A WebSocket ended.
---@class WebSocketEndEvent: Event
---@field socket WebSocket The socket.
---@field reason "finished"|"closed"|"failed"|"limit"|"idle"|"unloaded" Why: the other side closed it (`code` and `detail` say how); `close()` was called; the connection couldn't be made, was refused or broke (as for a stream; a server that doesn't speak WebSocket at that URL, or a binary message); an owner's limit in `config.yml`'s `http:` section was reached (too many connections open, the request rate, a message over `connections.max-message-bytes`, the package's `connections.bytes-per-minute`); nothing was sent or arrived for `connections.idle-seconds`; or the script that opened it unloaded.
---@field code integer? For `"finished"`: the close code the other side sent (`1000` is a normal close).
---@field detail string? For `"finished"`: the reason the other side gave, if any. For `"failed"` and `"limit"`: what went wrong, in words. `nil` otherwise.

--- A request a route answers.
---@class WebRequest
---@field method string `"GET"`, `"POST"`, … (`"HEAD"` for a `GET` route asked only for headers).
---@field path string The path asked for, namespace and all: `"/shop/replays/12"`.
---@field params table<string, string> The route's `:name` and `*name` segments by name, decoded.
---@field query table<string, string> The query parameters by name, decoded. A name given more than once is its first value.
---@field headers table<string, string> The request headers by name in lowercase; one sent more than once is its values joined with `, `.
---@field body string The body as text (UTF-8), at most `web.max-body-bytes`; `""` for none.
---@field form table<string, string>? The body's fields, decoded, when it's a form (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`); `nil` otherwise.
---@field ip string The address it came from. Behind a reverse proxy that's the proxy's, unless the owner set `web.trust-proxy-headers`, which makes it the client's from `X-Forwarded-For`.

--- How `nf.web.verify_signature` checks a signature.
---@class SignatureOptions
---@field secret string The secret the sender signs with.
---@field algorithm "sha256"|"sha1"|"sha512"? The HMAC's hash. Default `"sha256"`.
---@field encoding "hex"|"base64"? How the signature is written. Default `"hex"` (either case).
---@field prefix string? Text before the signature to expect and skip, like `"sha256="`.

--- What `nf.web.oauth` takes.
---@class OAuthOptions
---@field name string The flow's name, unique in the package: lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`. Its callback is `/<namespace>/oauth/<name>/callback`, and its confirmation `/<namespace>/oauth/<name>/confirm`.
---@field authorize_url string The service's authorization page: an `https://` URL.
---@field token_url string The service's token endpoint: an `https://` URL whose host the package declares in `requires.http`.
---@field client_id string The client id the service gave you.
---@field client_secret string? The client secret, for a service that wants one besides PKCE. Keep it in a setting (`nf.config`).
---@field scopes string[]? The scopes to ask for, sent separated by spaces.
---@field client_auth "body"|"basic"? How the secret is sent to the token endpoint: in the form (`"body"`, the default) or as HTTP Basic (`"basic"`).
---@field parameters table<string, string>? More query parameters for the authorization page, like `{ prompt = "consent" }`.

--- A login finished.
---@class OAuthCompleteEvent: Event
---@field flow OAuthFlow The flow.
---@field player Player Who logged in (perhaps offline by now).
---@field access_token string The access token, to call the service as them.
---@field token_type string? Its type, usually `"Bearer"`.
---@field refresh_token string? A refresh token, if the service gave one.
---@field expires_in integer? How many seconds the access token lasts, if the service said.
---@field scope string? The scopes granted, if the service said.
---@field id_token string? An OpenID Connect ID token, if the service gave one.

--- A login didn't finish.
---@class OAuthFailEvent: Event
---@field flow OAuthFlow The flow.
---@field player Player Who was logging in.
---@field error string What went wrong, in words.

--- A function a module exports, from `nf.wasm.functions`.
---@class WasmFunction
---@field name string Its name: `"sample"`.
---@field params string[] Its parameters' types in order: `"i32"`, `"i64"`, `"f32"`, `"f64"` (a `"v128"` or a reference can't be passed).
---@field results string[] Its results' types in order.

--- What `nf.wasm.run` takes.
---@class WasmRunOptions
---@field stdin string? Its whole input; after it, the program reads the end of the file. None when left out.
---@field arguments string[]? Its arguments, after its name (the module's id, `argv[0]`).
---@field environment table<string, string>? Its environment variables; none when left out (it never sees the server's).

--- What `nf.wasm.start` takes.
---@class WasmStartOptions
---@field arguments string[]? Its arguments, after its name (the module's id, `argv[0]`).
---@field environment table<string, string>? Its environment variables; none when left out (it never sees the server's).

--- What a program `nf.wasm.run` ran did.
---@class WasmRunResult
---@field exit_code integer Its exit code: `0` when it says it worked, as programs do.
---@field stdout string What it printed to its standard output, up to `wasm.max-output-bytes`.
---@field stderr string What it printed to its standard error, up to the same.

--- A program printed a line.
---@class WasmOutputEvent: Event
---@field process WasmProcess The program.
---@field line string The line, without its line ending (UTF-8).
---@field stream "stdout"|"stderr" Which it printed it to: its standard output or its standard error.

--- A program ended.
---@class WasmEndEvent: Event
---@field process WasmProcess The program.
---@field reason "exited"|"stopped"|"failed"|"limit"|"unloaded" Why: it exited (`exit_code` says how), `stop()` was called, it failed (it trapped, or the module couldn't be compiled or started: `detail` says why), an owner's limit in `config.yml`'s `wasm:` section was reached, or the script that started it unloaded.
---@field exit_code integer? Its exit code, when it exited; `nil` otherwise.
---@field detail string? What went wrong, in words, for `"failed"` and `"limit"`; `nil` otherwise.

--- A server of the network, from `nf.network.servers`.
---@class NetworkServer
---@field name string Its name, as the proxy names it.
---@field connected boolean Whether NetherForge on it is connected to the hub now (a server without NetherForge never is).
---@field players integer How many players are on it.

--- A player online in the network.
---@class NetworkPlayer
---@field id string Their UUID, as a string: the same as `player:id()` on their server.
---@field name string Their name.
---@field server string? The server they're on; `nil` for a moment while the proxy is moving them.

--- What `channel:publish` takes. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class NetworkPublishOptions
---@field server string? Only this server hears it (if it listens), by its name in the network; every server listening when left out.

--- What `map:set`, `add` and `insert` take. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class NetworkMapWriteOptions
---@field expires integer? Ticks (20 a second) from now until the hub deletes the key, as a `change` with cause `"expired"`; never when left out. Counted by the hub's clock, whatever any server's ticks do.

--- Something was published on a channel.
---@class NetworkMessageEvent: Event
---@field channel NetworkChannel The channel.
---@field value any What was published.
---@field server string The server it was published on (maybe this one).

--- A server asked this one on a channel.
---@class NetworkRequestEvent: Event
---@field channel NetworkChannel The channel.
---@field value any What it asked with.
---@field server string The server asking (maybe this one).
---@field reply any Writable: the answer, sent back once the handlers have run. Anything a `data()` table can hold, sent as JSON with the same codec: tables, strings, numbers, booleans, `Vec3`, `Location` (its world by name) and handles to entities and worlds (by UUID and name, which another server may not have). At most `max-value-bytes` as JSON (the proxy's `hub.properties`, 64 KiB unless its owner says otherwise); a value over it, or one that can't be written as JSON, is an error at the script's line.

--- A key of a shared map changed.
---@class NetworkMapChangeEvent: Event
---@field map NetworkMap The map.
---@field key string The key.
---@field value any Its value now; `nil` when it was deleted or expired.
---@field previous any Its value in this server's copy before.
---@field server string? The server that changed it (maybe this one); `nil` when it expired.
---@field cause "set"|"delete"|"expired" What changed it: a write that set it (`set`, `add` or `insert`), a `delete` (or `set` to `nil`), or its expiry.

--- A shared map's copy arrived on this server.
---@class NetworkMapReadyEvent: Event
---@field map NetworkMap The map.

--- This server connected to its network's hub.
---@class NetworkConnectEvent: Event
---@field server string This server's name in the network.

--- This server lost its connection to the network's hub.
---@class NetworkDisconnectEvent: Event
---@field detail string What happened, in words, for the log.

--- Another server of the network connected or disconnected.
---@class NetworkServerEvent: Event
---@field server string Its name in the network, as the proxy names it.

--- A player joined or left the network.
---@class NetworkPlayerEvent: Event
---@field player NetworkPlayer Who, and the server they joined or were last on.

--- A player went from one server of the network to another.
---@class NetworkPlayerSwitchEvent: Event
---@field player NetworkPlayer Who, and the server they are on now.
---@field previous string The server they came from.

--- A player sent here from another server joined.
---@class PlayerArriveEvent: Event
---@field player Player The player, on this server now.
---@field from string The server that sent them.
---@field data any What the script that sent them gave `send_player`, as `nf.json` would read it back; `nil` when it gave nothing.

--- How `nf.particles.play` plays an effect. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ParticlePlayOptions
---@field viewers Player[]? Only these players see it (still only those within range). Players who leave drop out. Default: everyone in range.
---@field scale number? Multiplies its distances: shape sizes, offsets, spreads and speeds (not dust sizes). More than 0, at most 16. Default 1.
---@field loop boolean? Whether it starts again after its last tick, until it's stopped. Default: what its file says.
---@field follow Centity|Node|Player|Entity? Follow this from the start, as `effect:follow` does. Each tick, before it spawns anything, the effect moves to the target: its position (a node's `world_position()`), facing the target's yaw (a node's centity's), pitch 0. When the target stops existing (a removed centity, a player who left, an entity that died or was removed, a centity or entity whose chunk unloaded) the effect ends with reason `"target_gone"`. `location_or_position` is still needed: it's where the effect is if the target is already gone, which ends it on its first tick.
---@field offset Vec3? With `follow`: where the effect sits from the target, as for `effect:follow`. Without `follow` it's an error.

--- A particle effect ended.
---@class EffectEndEvent: Event
---@field effect Effect The effect.
---@field reason "finished"|"stopped"|"target_gone"|"removed"|"unloaded" Why: it played to its end; `stop()` was called; what it followed went; its file was deleted; or the script that played it unloaded (a reload, its centity removed, the server stopping).

--- How `nf.cutscenes.play` plays a cutscene. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class CutscenePlayOptions
---@field origin Location|Vec3? Where the path's coordinates are measured from: every camera position is added to it (the path is moved, not turned). A `Location` also says which world the cutscene is in (its `yaw` and `pitch` are not used); a `Vec3` is in the player's world. Default: the path's coordinates are the world's, in the player's world.
---@field skippable boolean? Whether the player may end it early by sneaking (reason `"skipped"`). Default: what the cutscene's file says, which defaults to `false`.

--- A cutscene ended.
---@class CutsceneEndEvent: Event
---@field cutscene Cutscene The cutscene.
---@field player Player Who was watching.
---@field reason "finished"|"stopped"|"skipped"|"replaced"|"player_left"|"unloaded" Why: it played to its end; `stop()` was called; the player sneaked, in a skippable one; another cutscene was played for the player; the player left the server; or the script that played it unloaded (a reload, the server stopping).

--- A cutscene reached a named cue.
---@class CutsceneCueEvent: Event
---@field cutscene Cutscene The cutscene.
---@field player Player Who is watching.
---@field cue string The cue's `event` name in the cutscene's file.

--- A screen `nf.screens.create` makes: `width` and `height` are required; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class ScreenOptions
---@field width integer Maps across, 1 to 16.
---@field height integer Maps down, 1 to 16.
---@field key string? Keeps its maps' numbers for good, across restarts (see `nf.screens.create`): any name, unique among the screens this package's scripts make. Making one with a key a screen that's still there has is an error.
---@field background string? What it starts filled with. A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through. Default `"clear"`.
---@field viewers ("near"|"script")? `"near"` (the default): every player near where it hangs, or everyone online while it hangs nowhere. `"script"`: only those `show_to` shows it to.
---@field range integer? How near is near, in blocks: 1 to 256 (default 64).
---@field glowing boolean? Whether its frames glow, drawing it at full brightness (default true).
---@field click_range number? How far a click reaches it from, in blocks: 0 to 64 (default 24). Within the game's reach a click on a frame always counts.

--- How a shape is drawn: `fill`, `outline`, or both (neither is an error).
---@class ShapeStyle
---@field fill string? Its inside. A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through.
---@field outline string? Its edge. A colour, `#rrggbb` (drawn as the nearest of the colours a map can show), or `"clear"` for none: the map shows through.
---@field thickness integer? The outline's width, in pixels: 1 to 32 (default 1).
---@field radius integer? A rectangle's rounded corners, in pixels (default 0).

--- How text is drawn on a screen. Every key is optional.
---@class ScreenTextOptions
---@field font string? `"default"` (the game's own map font, the default) or one of the project's (`screen_fonts/<id>/`).
---@field color string? Its colour where the text sets none, `#rrggbb` (default white).
---@field scale integer? Each pixel of the font drawn this many pixels a side: 1 to 8 (default 1).
---@field align ("left"|"center"|"right")? Where `x` is: its left (the default), its middle or its right, line by line.
---@field width integer? Breaks lines at spaces so none is wider than this, in pixels.
---@field shadow string? Draws a shadow a pixel down and right of every pixel, in this colour.
---@field line_height integer? Pixels from one line's top to the next's (default the font's).

--- How big text is drawn, in pixels.
---@class ScreenTextSize
---@field width integer Its widest line.
---@field height integer All its lines.

--- How a picture is drawn on a screen. Every key is optional.
---@class ScreenImageOptions
---@field width integer? Drawn this wide, in pixels (default its own width, or kept in shape with `height`).
---@field height integer? Drawn this tall, in pixels (default its own height, or kept in shape with `width`).
---@field dither ("none"|"floyd_steinberg"|"ordered")? How its colours become map colours: `"floyd_steinberg"` (the default) spreads each pixel's error to its neighbours, best for photos; `"ordered"` uses a fixed pattern; `"none"` takes each pixel's nearest.

--- How `screen:play` plays a video. Every key is optional.
---@class VideoPlayOptions
---@field loop boolean? Starts it again when it ends (default false).
---@field speed number? How fast: 0.25 to 4 (default 1). Its sound plays only at 1.
---@field start number? Where to start, in seconds (default 0).
---@field volume number? Its sound's volume, 0 to 1 (default 1); 0 plays it silent.
---@field x integer? Where its left is on the screen, in pixels (default 0).
---@field y integer? Where its top is on the screen, in pixels (default 0).
---@field audio_range number? How near where the screen hangs a player must be to hear it, in blocks (default 32). A screen that hangs nowhere plays its sound to everyone it's drawn for.

--- A pixel of a screen a player aims at.
---@class ScreenPoint
---@field screen Screen The screen: the player's own view of it, when they have one.
---@field x integer From its left edge.
---@field y integer From its top edge.
---@field distance number From the player's eyes, in blocks.

--- A player clicked a screen.
---@class ScreenClickEvent: Event
---@field screen Screen The screen clicked: the player's own view of it, when they have one.
---@field player Player Who clicked.
---@field x integer The pixel aimed at, from the left edge.
---@field y integer From the top edge.
---@field click "left"|"right" Which button.

--- The pixel a player aims at on a screen moved.
---@class ScreenHoverEvent: Event
---@field screen Screen The screen: the player's own view of it, when they have one.
---@field player Player Who.
---@field x integer The pixel aimed at now, from the left edge.
---@field y integer From the top edge.

--- A player stopped aiming at a screen.
---@class ScreenLeaveEvent: Event
---@field screen Screen The screen.
---@field player Player Who.

--- A video stopped playing.
---@class PlaybackEndEvent: Event
---@field playback Playback The playback.
---@field reason "finished"|"stopped"|"replaced"|"removed"|"unloaded" Why: it played to its end (and doesn't loop); `stop()` was called; another video was played on the screen; the screen went; or the script that played it unloaded.

--- A frame of a video was drawn.
---@class PlaybackFrameEvent: Event
---@field playback Playback The playback.
---@field frame integer Which frame, 0 first.
---@field time number Its time in the video, in seconds.

--- How `nf.time.format` and `nf.time.parse` read a clock time. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TimeOptions
---@field zone string? Which time zone the clock time is in: an IANA name like `"Europe/London"` or `"America/New_York"`, `"UTC"`, or an offset like `"+02:00"`. Default the server owner's zone (`schedules.time-zone` in `config.yml`, the server's own by default), the one `nf.schedule` runs in. A zone the server doesn't know is an error.

--- Which noise `nf.random.noise2` and `noise3` give. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class NoiseOptions
---@field seed integer? Which noise: each seed is a different pattern. Default 0.
---@field scale number? What the coordinates are multiplied by first: smaller is smoother and wider (0.05 changes over about 20 blocks). Default 1.

--- A message another server of a test's network heard, from `nf.test.network_received`.
---@class TestNetworkMessage
---@field channel string The channel's name, as the calling package names it.
---@field value any What was published.
---@field server string The server it was published on.

--- What `nf.test.stub_wasm` answers a module with. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestWasmStub
---@field functions table<string, fun(arguments: any[]): any>? An instance's functions by name: each gets the call's `arguments` and returns its results, a list for `call` (`{ 5 }`) or a string for `call_text`.
---@field run (fun(options: table): WasmRunResult)? What `module:run` gives: it gets the run's options (`stdin`, `arguments`, `environment`) and returns a result table.
---@field input (fun(line: string, process: WasmProcess): string[]?)? A started program's answer to each line it's sent: the lines it prints (to its standard output), or `nil` for none.

--- Where `nf.test.shown` looks. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestShownOptions
---@field menu boolean? Whether `slot` is one of the window they have open, numbered from 0 as `menu:slot` numbers them.

--- How `nf.test.click` clicks. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestClickOptions
---@field hand "main_hand"|"off_hand"? Which hand clicks, and whose item is used. Default `"main_hand"`.
---@field block Block? The block clicked, which must be in a loaded chunk. Default: none, a click at the air.
---@field face "up"|"down"|"north"|"south"|"east"|"west"? Which face of `block` was clicked. Default `"up"`; only with `block`.

--- The keys `nf.test.input` holds down: each `true` is held, and one left out (or `false`) isn't. A key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestInputKeys
---@field forward boolean? Forward (W).
---@field backward boolean? Back (S).
---@field left boolean? Left (A).
---@field right boolean? Right (D).
---@field jump boolean? Jump (space).
---@field sneak boolean? Sneak (shift).
---@field sprint boolean? Sprint (control).

--- How `nf.test.accept` answers. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestAcceptOptions
---@field status integer? A stream's status: 100 to 599. Default `200`.
---@field headers table<string, string>? The response headers (names are lowercased). Default: none.
---@field protocol string? A WebSocket's chosen subprotocol: one of those it offered. Default: none.

--- How `nf.test.disconnect` ends a connection. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestDisconnectOptions
---@field code integer? A WebSocket's close code. Default `1000`.
---@field reason string? A WebSocket's close reason, its `end` event's `detail`.
---@field error string? Break it instead: it ends `"failed"`, with this as `detail`.

--- What `nf.test.request` sends. Every key is optional; a key that isn't one of these is an error.
---@class TestRequestOptions
---@field headers table<string, string>? Request headers by name.
---@field body string? The body. Default none.
---@field ip string? The address it comes from. Default `"127.0.0.1"`.

--- What `nf.test.request` gives back: the answer as the client got it.
---@class TestWebResponse
---@field status integer The status code.
---@field headers table<string, string> The response headers by name in lowercase.
---@field body string The body (empty for a `HEAD`).

--- What `nf.test.raise` gives back.
---@class RaisedEvent
---@field cancelled boolean Whether a handler called `event:cancel()` (always `false` for an event that can't be cancelled).
---@field event table The payload as the handlers left it: a writable field they assigned (`message`) holds what they set.

--- A custom event (a name with a `:`, raised with `emit`): an `Event` plus whatever fields its payload had, so any field reads as `any`.
---@class CustomEvent: Event
---@field [string] any

--- Writes a line to the server console (and the editor's, with the file and line it came from). Arguments are converted with `tostring` and joined with tabs.
---@type fun(...: any)
log = nil

--- The script's own handle: `Centity` in a centity script, `Menu` in a menu script, `Dialog` in a dialog script, `ProjectItem` in a item script, `ProjectBlock` in a block script, `Screen` in a screen script. Nothing in a module.
---
--- Start a script with `local this = this --[[@as Centity]]` so lua-language-server knows which.
---@type Centity|Menu|Dialog|ProjectItem|ProjectBlock|Screen
this = nil
